{"timestamp": "2026-10-19T16:10:39.401003", "agent_name": "AstroDeepResearchAgent", "method_name": "load_cached_research", "step": null, "attempt": null, "system_message": "Cached research loaded from: reports/deep_research_GPT6/Valine-Run1.txt", "user_message": "Config: deep_research_gpt6", "raw_response": "# Prebiotic Synthesis of Valine: Routes, Precursors, Catalysts, Environmental Context, and Open Problems\n\n## Executive assessment\n\nValine is a useful stress test for prebiotic synthesis models because it is not merely an \u03b1-amino acid: it requires construction of a **branched C5 carbon skeleton**, HOOC\u2013CH(NH\u2082)\u2013CH(CH\u2083)\u2082. The literature contains several direct demonstrations of abiotic valine formation, but the evidence is uneven. The strongest direct demonstrations fall into five chemically distinct families: **Strecker-type atmospheric/electric-discharge chemistry; ammonium-cyanide/ketone chemistry; high-temperature formaldehyde\u2013ammonia chemistry; shock chemistry; and reductive amination of \u03b1-ketoisovalerate with H\u2082 over metal catalysts**. More recently, **\u03b3-radiolysis of formaldehyde/ammonia solutions in meteorite-parent-body analogues** has also generated valine as a minor product.\n\nThe central conclusion is that **no published route yet closes the entire synthesis from the simplest geochemical feedstocks to valine under one experimentally demonstrated, geochemically well-constrained environment**. The classical Strecker family can, in principle, build the C5 skeleton by adding the carbon of HCN to the C4 aldehyde isobutyraldehyde, and electric-discharge experiments genuinely produce valine, but the strongly reducing atmospheric mixtures that produce aldehydes and HCN most efficiently are difficult to reconcile with evidence that Hadean mantle outgassing was comparatively oxidized. Conversely, the 2024 H\u2082/Ni chemistry of Kaur and co-workers converts the biologically congruent precursor **\u03b1-ketoisovalerate directly to valine under remarkably mild conditions**, but \u03b1-ketoisovalerate was supplied at 30 mM: its abiotic synthesis from CO\u2082, CO, pyruvate, or other simpler feedstocks under the same conditions remains the major missing step.\n\nA useful evidence hierarchy is therefore:\n\n| Route family | Direct valine demonstrated? | Simplest feedstocks experimentally connected to the route | Main bottleneck |\n|---|---:|---|---|\n| Electric discharge \u2192 aldehydes + HCN \u2192 Strecker chemistry | **Yes** | CH\u2084, NH\u2083 or N\u2082, H\u2082O, H\u2082 | Reduced atmosphere; production of enough C4 isobutyraldehyde; low selectivity |\n| NH\u2084CN + ketone network | **Yes** | HCN/NH\u2083 plus acetone | Mechanism of C5 branching is poorly resolved; NH\u2084CN alone does **not** reliably give valine |\n| Heated HCHO + NH\u2083 | **Yes, historically** | Formaldehyde, ammonia, water | Harsh heating; poorly defined bound intermediates; weak valine-specific quantitation/reproducibility |\n| Shock-heated reduced gases | **Yes** | CH\u2084, C\u2082H\u2086, NH\u2083 | Highly reducing gas composition not representative of favored global Hadean atmosphere |\n| CO\u2082/N\u2082 impact chemistry | **Not yet** | CO\u2082/HCO\u2083\u207b, N\u2082, H\u2082O, Fe, Ni, silicates | Experiments stop at simpler amino acids; no demonstrated valine |\n| \u03b1-Ketoisovalerate + NH\u2083 + H\u2082, Ni/Pd/meteorite | **Yes** | H\u2082, NH\u2083 plus preformed C5 \u03b1-ketoacid | Origin and concentration of \u03b1-ketoisovalerate |\n| \u03b1-Ketoacid + CN\u207b/NH\u2083 Bucherer\u2013Bergs chemistry | **Mechanistically plausible; valine not directly shown in the 2022 substrate set** | \u03b1-Ketoacid, CN\u207b, ammonia source, bicarbonate | Requires preformed \u03b1-ketoisovalerate and concentrated cyanide/ammonia; long processing |\n| CO/H\u2082/NH\u2083 Fischer\u2013Tropsch-type mineral chemistry | **No in the principal 2012 study** | CO, H\u2082, NH\u2083 | Produces other amino acids, notably AIB; valine was not reported |\n| \u03b3-Radiolysis of HCHO/NH\u2083 parent-body solutions | **Yes, minor product** | HCHO, NH\u2083, H\u2082O, sometimes CH\u2083OH/glycolaldehyde | Product complexity, dose/concentration assumptions, bound precursors, extraterrestrial rather than terrestrial setting |\n\nThe route classifications above follow the direct experiments of Miller (1957, *Biochimica et Biophysica Acta*), Friedmann & Miller (1969, *Nature*), Bar-Nun, Bar-Nun, Bauer & Sagan (1970, *Science*), Fox & Windsor (1970, *Science*), Ring, Wolman, Friedmann & Miller (1972, *Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences*), Pizzarello (2012, *Meteoritics & Planetary Science*), Pulletikurti, Yadav, Springsteen & Krishnamurthy (2022, *Nature Chemistry*), Kaur et al. (2024, *Chem*), and Ishikawa, Kebukawa, Kobayashi & Yoda (2024, *Life*).\n\n## Known synthesis routes and mechanisms\n\n### Electric-discharge chemistry and the Strecker route\n\nThe most chemically explicit classical route is the one established mechanistically by **Stanley Miller (1957, *Biochimica et Biophysica Acta*)**. Electric discharge through CH\u2084\u2013NH\u2083\u2013H\u2082O\u2013H\u2082 mixtures produced **HCN and aldehydes**, which then entered Strecker chemistry. Miller showed that aminonitrile hydrolysis proceeded quickly enough to account for the amino acids accumulated after roughly a week of sparking. Replacing NH\u2083 with N\u2082 still generated the same general product types, but at lower yield. Addition of ferrous ammonium sulfate did not substantially change product yields, arguing that no Fe/sulfate mineral catalysis was necessary in this experiment.\n\nFor valine specifically, the chemically straightforward Strecker sequence is:\n\n\\[\n(CH_3)_2CHCHO + NH_3 \\rightleftharpoons (CH_3)_2CHCH{=}NH + H_2O\n\\]\n\n\\[\n(CH_3)_2CHCH{=}NH + HCN \\rightarrow (CH_3)_2CHCH(NH_2)CN\n\\]\n\nfollowed by nitrile hydrolysis:\n\n\\[\n(CH_3)_2CHCH(NH_2)CN + 2H_2O \\rightarrow\n(CH_3)_2CHCH(NH_2)CO_2H + NH_3\n\\]\n\nThus the crucial carbon precursor is **2-methylpropanal, or isobutyraldehyde**. Its four-carbon branched skeleton supplies valine C2\u2013C5, while cyanide supplies the carboxyl carbon. This is an important point for network design: making HCN is not enough; an efficient prebiotic valine network also has to generate a significant pool of **branched C4 aldehyde**.\n\nDirect confirmation that such discharge chemistry reaches valine came from **Ring, Wolman, Friedmann & Miller (1972, *PNAS*)**. They discharged a mixture of methane, nitrogen and water containing traces of ammonia and identified, using ion-exchange chromatography together with GC\u2013MS, glycine, alanine, \u03b1-aminobutyric acid, \u03b1-aminoisobutyric acid, **valine**, norvaline, isovaline, leucine, isoleucine, alloisoleucine, norleucine and several polar amino acids. The broad isomer distribution is significant: the experiment demonstrates chemical accessibility but **poor structural selectivity** for the biological amino-acid set.\n\nThe main environmental objection is now substantial. **Trail, Watson & Tailby (2011, *Nature*)** used Ce redox systematics in Hadean zircons to infer mantle-derived magmas near the fayalite\u2013magnetite\u2013quartz redox buffer by about 4.35 Ga. Such outgassing is predicted to favor H\u2082O, CO\u2082, SO\u2082 and N\u2082 rather than the CH\u2084-, NH\u2083- and H\u2082-rich mixture most favorable to Miller-type amino-acid synthesis. This does not prove that strongly reducing local or transient environments never existed, but it makes a globally persistent Miller-style atmosphere difficult to defend geochemically.\n\nAttempts to extend spark chemistry to more neutral CO\u2082/N\u2082 atmospheres illustrate the problem. **Cleaves, Chalmers, Lazcano, Miller & Bada (2008, *Origins of Life and Evolution of Biospheres*)** found that amino-acid recovery from CO\u2082/N\u2082 discharge mixtures could be raised considerably by controlling pH and suppressing oxidative destruction during work-up. In experiments with approximately 0.13 bar CO\u2082 and 0.13 bar N\u2082, CaCO\u2083 buffering raised total amino-acid recovery relative to unbuffered experiments. However, the published product set emphasized glycine, alanine, \u03b2-alanine, AIB, aspartate, glutamate and serine rather than valine. Moreover, **Kuwahara, Eto, Kawamoto, Kurihara, Kaneko, Obayashi & Kobayashi (2012, *Origins of Life and Evolution of Biospheres*)** used isotopic tests to show that ascorbate, which had been used as an oxidation inhibitor in some such experiments, could itself contribute carbon to apparent amino-acid products. Thus neutral-atmosphere spark chemistry remains relevant for simpler precursors, but **it is not currently a strong direct experimental route to valine**.\n\n### Ammonium cyanide plus ketones\n\n**Nadav Friedmann & Stanley Miller (1969, *Nature*)** directly revisited reports that polymerized ammonium cyanide produced the branched protein amino acids. Their result is unusually important because it contains both a positive result and a published refutation: they **could not detect valine, isoleucine or leucine from NH\u2084CN polymerization alone** when more stringent separation was used. Earlier identifications had relied primarily on amino-acid-analyzer elution positions, where non-protein amino acids can co-elute with valine or isoleucine.\n\nWhen **acetone or methyl ethyl ketone was added to NH\u2084CN**, however, Friedmann and Miller obtained significant quantities of valine and, depending on the ketone, alloisoleucine and isoleucine. This therefore constitutes a direct prebiotic valine synthesis route from a cyanide/ammonia pool plus simple ketones.\n\nMechanistically, this route is more problematic than the ordinary aldehyde Strecker pathway. A simple Strecker reaction of acetone, NH\u2083 and HCN gives **\u03b1-aminoisobutyric acid**, a C4 amino acid, not C5 valine. Consequently, the reported appearance of valine from acetone/NH\u2084CN must involve additional carbon\u2013carbon chemistry in the cyanide/ketone reaction network. Friedmann and Miller established the product much more securely than earlier NH\u2084CN work, but they did not establish a unique elementary mechanism for the C5 branch. This makes the route experimentally real but **mechanistically underconstrained for network reconstruction**.\n\nThe study also supplies one of the clearest historical warnings for the valine literature: **NH\u2084CN polymerization by itself should not be counted as an established valine synthesis**. The most careful direct re-investigation found that earlier assignments were probably chromatographic false positives.\n\n### Heated formaldehyde\u2013ammonia chemistry\n\nA chemically very different route was reported by **Sidney Fox & Charles Windsor (1970, *Science*)**. Heating formaldehyde with ammonia generated a complex product which, after hydrolysis, yielded aspartate, glutamate, serine, proline, **valine**, glycine and alanine; glycine was the dominant amino acid. Later experimental literature describing the original procedure reports heating at approximately **185 \u00b0C**, illustrating that this was a substantially hotter process than ordinary aqueous surface chemistry.\n\nThis is not best understood as an ordinary Strecker reaction because HCN and a discrete valine aldehyde were not supplied. The experimentally observed amino acids appeared importantly after hydrolysis, pointing to **nitrogen-containing oligomers or other hydrolysable bound precursors** rather than to a clean solution of free amino acids. The detailed route from C1 formaldehyde to a branched C5 valine skeleton was never resolved at an elementary-mechanism level. Modern work generally treats related HCHO/NH\u2083 chemistry as a nitrogen-containing, formose-like condensation network rather than a single selective synthesis.\n\nModern parent-body simulations expose a major reproducibility problem for valine in this chemistry. **Yoko Kebukawa and co-workers (2017, *Science Advances*)** heated formaldehyde, glycolaldehyde, ammonia and water at **90\u2013250 \u00b0C for 72 h**, producing complex insoluble organic matter and numerous amino-acid precursors. Their analytical standard included valine, but the valine recovered from experiments was detected only as the **L enantiomer**, unlike genuinely abiotic products that were racemic; the authors therefore regarded the apparent valine as probable biological contamination rather than evidence of synthesis.\n\nThat result does not disprove Fox & Windsor's 1970 observation, because the recipes differ substantially, but it means that **thermal HCHO/NH\u2083 chemistry should not be treated as a robust, reproducibly quantified modern valine pathway**. It does robustly generate simpler amino acids and hydrolysable organic material; the C5 branched products remain much less secure.\n\n### Radiation-driven formaldehyde\u2013ammonia chemistry in small bodies\n\nA more recent variant uses ionizing radiation rather than heat alone. **Yoko Kebukawa (2022, *ACS Central Science*)** showed that \u03b3 irradiation of aqueous formaldehyde and ammonia mixtures produced multiple amino acids after hydrolysis. Amino-acid yield scaled approximately linearly with accumulated \u03b3 dose and was comparatively insensitive to dose rate, which matters because the proposed natural energy source is \u03b3 radiation associated with radionuclides such as ^26Al in aqueously altered asteroid parent bodies. The estimated natural radiation production was argued to be chemically significant on parent-body timescales.\n\nThe expanded experiments of **Akari Ishikawa, Yoko Kebukawa, Kensei Kobayashi & Isao Yoda (2024, *Life*)** varied the formaldehyde/ammonia/methanol composition and reported **valine among the lower-abundance amino-acid products**, behind alanine, glycine, \u03b2-alanine, \u03b1-aminobutyric acid and several other species. Representative experiments used large integrated \u03b3 doses on the order of tens to ~90 kGy.\n\nMechanistically, this chemistry is more plausibly a **radical-driven reaction network** than a single Strecker event. Radiolysis generates reactive carbon-, oxygen- and nitrogen-centered species; formaldehyde can also form glycolaldehyde and higher carbonyl compounds, which then feed carbon-chain growth. A discrete, experimentally traced sequence from HCHO to the branched C5 valine skeleton has not yet been established, so the route is direct at the product level but not mechanistically closed.\n\nIts environmental relevance is strongest for **aqueously altered carbonaceous-asteroid parent bodies**, not for a particular early-Earth surface environment. It therefore represents a plausible contribution through extraterrestrial synthesis and delivery rather than a demonstrated endogenous terrestrial source.\n\n### Shock synthesis\n\n**A. Bar-Nun, N. Bar-Nun, S. H. Bauer & Carl Sagan (1970, *Science*)** subjected a reduced gas mixture containing approximately **3.3% CH\u2084, 11% C\u2082H\u2086 and 5.6% NH\u2083, with the balance Ar**, to single-pulse shock heating followed by rapid quenching. They directly observed **glycine, alanine, valine and leucine**. The reported overall efficiency for \u03b1-amino-acid production was about **5 \u00d7 10\u00b9\u2070 molecules per erg of shock energy**. The authors argued that rapid quenching could kinetically trap amino acids that would not be favored at the peak equilibrium temperature.\n\nThe mechanism was not resolved into a valine-specific sequence. At shock temperatures, extensive radical fragmentation and recombination occur, followed by chemistry during rapid cooling. In principle, aldehydes, HCN/nitriles and related intermediates can emerge during such processing, but the study established valine production rather than a unique pathway to it. No mineral catalyst was required.\n\nThe principal critique is environmental. The experiment requires substantial reduced carbon and ammonia. The Hadean zircon constraints of Trail, Watson & Tailby favor substantially more oxidized volcanic outgassing, making this particular bulk-gas composition difficult to justify as the persistent global atmosphere.\n\nModern impact experiments have substantially improved the geochemical starting composition, but they have **not yet closed the gap to valine**. **Takeuchi, Furukawa, Kobayashi, Sekine, Terada & Kakegawa (2020, *Scientific Reports*)** shocked mixtures of Fe, Ni, forsterite, water, bicarbonate/CO\u2082 and N\u2082, designed to represent an Fe-bearing impactor striking an ocean beneath a CO\u2082/N\u2082 atmosphere. Their experiments used an impact velocity of about **0.9 km s\u207b\u00b9**, pressure of about **7 GPa for 0.7 \u03bcs**, a shock-compression temperature near **300 \u00b0C**, and estimated post-shock temperatures around **1500 \u00b0C**. Isotopic labeling confirmed abiotic amino-acid formation. Glycine and alanine were prominent; additional low-molecular-weight amino acids appeared when ammonia was supplied, but **valine was not demonstrated**.\n\nThis creates an instructive contrast: **the old reduced-gas shock experiment reaches valine; the newer geochemically more conservative CO\u2082/N\u2082/Fe impact experiment does not**. Impact chemistry is therefore a credible source of valine precursors and simpler amino acids, but the direct valine evidence still depends on the less geochemically favored reduced gas mixture.\n\n### Reductive amination of \u03b1-ketoisovalerate with hydrogen\n\nThe clearest modern direct conversion of a specifically valine-like metabolic precursor is the work of **Harpreet Kaur, Sophia Rauscher, Emilie Werner, Youngdong Song, Jing Yi, Wahnyalo Kaz\u00f6ne, William Martin, Harun T\u00fcys\u00fcz & Joseph Moran (2024, *Chem*)**. They examined six biological \u03b1-ketoacids, including **\u03b1-ketoisovalerate**, under H\u2082 with ammonia and heterogeneous metal catalysts. \u03b1-Ketoisovalerate gave valine together with its competing carbonyl-reduction product, 2-hydroxyisovalerate.\n\nThe net valine reaction is simple:\n\n\\[\n(CH_3)_2CHCOCO_2H + NH_3 + H_2\n\\rightarrow\n(CH_3)_2CHCH(NH_2)CO_2H + H_2O\n\\]\n\nMechanistically, ammonia reacts with the \u03b1-keto carbonyl to form an imine/iminium-type intermediate, followed by surface-mediated hydrogenation. The competing reaction is direct hydrogenation of the carbonyl to the corresponding \u03b1-hydroxy acid.\n\nThe standard valine experiment used **30 mM \u03b1-ketoisovalerate**, five equivalents NH\u2084HCO\u2083, **5 bar H\u2082**, water at **pH 8**, **22 \u00b0C**, and **72 h**. Three catalysts worked: Pd/C containing 1 mol% Pd relative to substrate, Ni/SiO\u2082\u2013Al\u2082O\u2083 containing 10 mol% Ni, and 3 mg of ground **Aletai iron meteorite** containing substantial Ni. No reduction products were seen in catalyst-free controls.\n\nFor \u03b1-ketoisovalerate specifically, Figure 2 of Kaur et al. gives unusually useful quantitative data. The reported combined amino-acid plus hydroxy-acid yields and amino:hydroxy ratios were:\n\n| Catalyst | Combined valine + 2-hydroxyisovalerate yield | Valine:hydroxy-acid ratio | Approximate valine yield inferred from the figure |\n|---|---:|---:|---:|\n| Ni/SiO\u2082\u2013Al\u2082O\u2083 | 20% | 34:66 | **~6.8%** |\n| Pd/C | 77% | 83:17 | **~64%** |\n| Aletai meteorite | 74% | 18:82 | **~13%** |\n\nThe last column is an arithmetic inference from the published combined yield and product ratio, not a separately reported value. The striking point is the trade-off in plausibility versus selectivity: **Pd is much more amino-acid selective, whereas Ni and meteoritic metal are geochemically easier to motivate but favor substantial hydroxy-acid formation**.\n\nThe same study found that the chemistry can function at ammonium concentrations from **6 to 150 mM**. It also linked reductive amination to H\u2082-driven reductive-aldol chemistry involving glyoxylate, oxaloacetate and other \u03b1-ketoacids. Some aldol steps occur even without catalyst, whereas the critical hydrogenation steps require the metal catalyst. The paper notes that hydrothermal fluids can contain approximately **1\u201315 mmol kg\u207b\u00b9 dissolved H\u2082**, making the reductant geochemically attractive in serpentinization-associated systems.\n\nThis is therefore one of the strongest **final-step** valine syntheses in the literature, but it is not yet a de novo valine synthesis from CO\u2082/H\u2082. The experiment **supplied \u03b1-ketoisovalerate**. The coupled reaction network generated \u03b1-ketoglutarate and other \u03b1-ketoacids from simpler precursors but did not establish a pathway from CO\u2082, pyruvate or glyoxylate all the way to \u03b1-ketoisovalerate. In modern biochemistry, construction of the valine skeleton from pyruvate involves a highly specific branched-chain pathway; an experimentally validated nonenzymatic equivalent remains missing.\n\n### Bucherer\u2013Bergs chemistry from \u03b1-ketoacids\n\n**Sunil Pulletikurti, Mahipal Yadav, Greg Springsteen & Ramanarayanan Krishnamurthy (2022, *Nature Chemistry*)** demonstrated a general alternative in which \u03b1-ketoacids react with cyanide and ammonia sources through **Bucherer\u2013Bergs-type chemistry** to produce the corresponding \u03b1-amino acids. Their mechanistic sequence goes through cyanide addition/amino-nitrile chemistry, cyclization to a **hydantoin**, hydrolysis to an N-carbamoyl amino acid, and finally amino-acid formation.\n\nUnder their optimized general procedure, an \u03b1-ketoacid was used at roughly **0.35 M**, with 2 equivalents of NH\u2084Cl or diamidophosphate, 1.2 equivalents NaCN, and **0.5 M bicarbonate at pH 8.5**. The mixture was held at room temperature for **21 days** and then heated at **80 \u00b0C for 14 days** to complete amino-acid formation. Their supporting information shows explicitly that hydantoin hydrolysis can proceed through an N-carbamoyl amino acid and that ammonia-source chemistry affects the hydrolysis.\n\nFor valine-network design, the obvious substrate is **\u03b1-ketoisovalerate**, which would be expected to give an isopropyl-substituted hydantoin and ultimately valine. However, the experimental substrate set documented in the 2022 paper concentrates on pyruvate, \u03b1-ketoglutarate, oxaloacetate and glyoxylate; **the paper does not provide a direct \u03b1-ketoisovalerate-to-valine experiment**. The pathway is therefore a well-supported reaction class that is mechanistically transferable to valine, but it should not be counted as a direct valine demonstration from that study.\n\nIts principal prebiotic criticisms are the comparatively high substrate/buffer concentrations, long reaction periods, requirement for cyanide plus a concentrated ammonia source, and\u2014again\u2014the prior availability of \u03b1-ketoisovalerate. Kaur et al. explicitly contrasted their H\u2082 chemistry with Bucherer\u2013Bergs chemistry, noting both its use of cyanide and its two-stage temperature protocol.\n\n## Key intermediates, precursors, catalysts, and kinetic constraints\n\nFor network-design purposes, the literature reduces to two particularly important **valine-specific carbon skeletons**.\n\nThe first is **isobutyraldehyde (2-methylpropanal)**. A Strecker route needs this C4 branched aldehyde plus HCN and ammonia. The aldehyde carbon becomes valine's \u03b1 carbon and the cyanide carbon becomes the carboxyl carbon. The immediate intermediate is **2-amino-3-methylbutanenitrile**, whose hydrolysis yields valine. HCN and the imine/aminonitrile intermediates therefore matter kinetically as much as the aldehyde itself. Miller's 1957 experiments showed that aminonitrile hydrolysis rates were sufficient to account for amino-acid accumulation on approximately week-long discharge timescales.\n\nThe second is **\u03b1-ketoisovalerate, 3-methyl-2-oxobutanoate**. It already contains all five carbons in the correct topology; only the \u03b1-keto group must be converted into an \u03b1-amino group. It is the direct precursor in the Kaur H\u2082/Ni/Pd route and the obvious substrate for a valine-specific Bucherer\u2013Bergs pathway. This makes \u03b1-ketoisovalerate particularly attractive for metabolism-congruent models, but also shifts nearly the entire carbon-skeleton problem upstream.\n\nThe major catalytic evidence can be summarized as follows.\n\n| Reaction | Catalyst/surface experimentally tested | Requirement |\n|---|---|---|\n| Spark generation of HCN/aldehydes and subsequent Strecker chemistry | No mineral catalyst required; Fe ammonium sulfate gave no important enhancement in Miller's experiment | Electrical energy; reducing feedstock mixture |\n| NH\u2084CN + ketone valine network | No mineral catalyst reported as required | Cyanide/ammonia chemistry plus ketone |\n| Heated HCHO/NH\u2083 route | No mineral catalyst required in original Fox\u2013Windsor experiment | Strong heating, followed by hydrolysis |\n| Reduced-gas shock route | No heterogeneous catalyst required | Shock heating and rapid quench |\n| \u03b1-Ketoisovalerate + H\u2082/NH\u2083 | Ni/SiO\u2082\u2013Al\u2082O\u2083, Pd/C, Aletai meteorite | **Catalyst essential** under tested conditions |\n| General \u03b1-ketoacid reductive amination | Fresh FeS or Fe(OH)\u2082 in Huber\u2013W\u00e4chtersh\u00e4user experiments | Strongly surface/state dependent |\n| Photochemical \u03b1-ketoacid amination | ZnS/sphalerite in Wang et al. | UV illumination and electron donor/hole-scavenging chemistry |\n| FTT-type CO/H\u2082/NH\u2083 chemistry | Fe/Ni meteorite powder, magnetite, Murchison material; bentonite much less active | 370 \u00b0C catalytic surface chemistry |\n\nThe first four rows follow Miller (1957), Friedmann & Miller (1969), Fox & Windsor (1970), and Bar-Nun et al. (1970); the H\u2082 metal-catalysis result is from Kaur et al. (2024).\n\nTwo older mineral studies are important mechanistically even though they did **not directly make valine**. **Claudia Huber & G\u00fcnter W\u00e4chtersh\u00e4user (2003, *Tetrahedron Letters*)** showed that freshly precipitated FeS and Fe(OH)\u2082 can promote reductive amination of several \u03b1-ketoacids with ammonium salts. Their experiments produced alanine, glutamate, phenylalanine and tyrosine, with maximum yields for several systems of tens of mol%; the reaction was highly sensitive to the physical state of FeS, pH and ionic strength. Optimal pH with ammonia was around **8.8\u20139.3**, close to the NH\u2084\u207a/NH\u2083 pK\u2090; elevated ionic strength suppressed the reaction. At 100 \u00b0C the tested tyrosine-forming system became fast on approximately hourly timescales, whereas reaction could still occur slowly at 4 \u00b0C. No \u03b1-ketoisovalerate/valine experiment was reported, so this work is evidence for a *reaction class*, not for valine itself.\n\nSimilarly, **Wei Wang, Qiliang Li, Bin Yang, Xiaoyang Liu, Yanqiang Yang & Wenhui Su (2012, *Chemical Communications*)** demonstrated reversible photochemical amination of four reverse-TCA-related \u03b1-ketoacids on irradiated ZnS. Their experiments used an oxygen-free ZnS suspension, pH around 9 and a Hg\u2013Xe source; glutamate yield was only about 0.5% after four hours without a sulfite hole scavenger and increased when the surface and photoredox conditions were optimized. \u03b1-Ketoisovalerate was not one of the substrates, so ZnS is a plausible catalyst family to test for valine chemistry rather than an existing direct demonstration.\n\nThe **Fischer\u2013Tropsch-type framework** deserves the same caution. **Sandra Pizzarello (2012, *Meteoritics & Planetary Science*)** reacted CO:H\u2082:NH\u2083 in a 1:1:1 gas mixture over meteorite/mineral powders at about **370 \u00b0C for 24 h**. Fe/Ni meteorite powder produced glycine, alanine, \u03b1-aminobutyric acid and smaller amounts of higher homologues; magnetite strongly favored the branched non-protein amino acid **\u03b1-aminoisobutyric acid**, reaching about 540 nmol g\u207b\u00b9 catalyst, and also yielded ~1 nmol g\u207b\u00b9 isovaline. **Valine itself was not reported in the product table.** Bentonite was a poor catalyst. FTT chemistry therefore provides experimentally important CO/H\u2082/NH\u2083 carbon\u2013nitrogen chemistry but should not currently be listed as a demonstrated valine route.\n\nThe valine literature contains surprisingly little **valine-specific equilibrium thermodynamics** under proposed prebiotic conditions. Most constraints are kinetic/empirical: week-scale aminonitrile hydrolysis in Miller chemistry; ultrafast high-temperature formation followed by quenching in shock chemistry; 72-h hydrogenation at 22 \u00b0C for Kaur's route; weeks-long formation/hydrolysis in the Bucherer\u2013Bergs system; and approximately linear accumulated-dose dependence in \u03b3-radiolysis. Thus, for synthesis-network modeling, experimentally determined rate constants, activation parameters and competing decomposition rates for **isobutyraldehyde, valine aminonitrile and \u03b1-ketoisovalerate under realistic saline/mineral conditions are major missing datasets**.\n\n## Environmental context and geochemical plausibility\n\n### Atmosphere and surface waters\n\nClassical Miller\u2013Strecker chemistry has the strongest historical evidence for direct synthesis from very small molecules, but its environmental support is weakest at the level of a **globally reducing atmosphere**. Trail, Watson & Tailby's Hadean zircon measurements imply mantle oxidation states similar to later terrestrial mantle by ~4.35 Ga, which favors outgassing of H\u2082O, CO\u2082, SO\u2082 and N\u2082 rather than large steady-state CH\u2084 and NH\u2083 abundances. The key environmental question is therefore not whether electrical discharge can make valine\u2014it can\u2014but whether early Earth could repeatedly create **local mixtures rich enough in reduced C and N to produce the necessary HCN and branched C4 aldehydes**.\n\nNeutral CO\u2082/N\u2082 spark experiments demonstrate that some amino-acid chemistry remains possible under more oxidized atmospheric assumptions, particularly when solution pH and oxidative work-up chemistry are controlled, but they have not supplied comparable direct evidence for valine. The later ascorbate-contamination result of Kuwahara et al. further weakens claims of very high amino-acid yields in some neutral-atmosphere experiments.\n\n### Hydrothermal and serpentinization systems\n\nThe **Kaur et al. H\u2082/Ni chemistry** is especially attractive environmentally because it works at 22 \u00b0C and pH ~8 rather than requiring hydrothermal temperatures. H\u2082 is continuously generated by water\u2013rock reactions such as serpentinization, and the authors note measured hydrothermal-fluid H\u2082 values of roughly 1\u201315 mmol kg\u207b\u00b9. Ni-bearing metal/mineral phases and meteoritic material offer plausible heterogeneous catalysts.\n\nThe difficulty is upstream carbon chemistry. Hydrothermal plausibility of H\u2082 and Ni does **not** establish a hydrothermal source of 30 mM \u03b1-ketoisovalerate. Kaur et al.'s coupled H\u2082 network makes several ketoacids from oxaloacetate/glyoxylate chemistry, but the valine-specific branched ketoacid was externally supplied. Thus the route currently has strong environmental support for the **reductive-amination step**, not for the complete valine synthesis.\n\nFeS/Fe(OH)\u2082 reductive-amination chemistry is often associated with hydrothermal/mineral-surface models, but Huber & W\u00e4chtersh\u00e4user's own results caution against treating \u201cFeS\u201d as a generic catalytic label: freshly precipitated material worked substantially differently from other preparations, excess sulfide could suppress activity, increased ionic strength reduced yields, and several alternative metal sulfides failed. Those dependencies need to be confronted explicitly when extrapolating to seawater or vent fluids.\n\n### Impact environments\n\nImpact chemistry is geochemically attractive because it supplies both extreme energy and reducing power from Fe/Ni projectiles without demanding a globally reducing atmosphere. Takeuchi et al.'s 2020 ^13C-labeled experiment demonstrates this principle directly with CO\u2082/HCO\u2083\u207b, N\u2082, water and meteoritic metals. But the realistic-feedstock experiment does **not yet reach valine**, whereas Bar-Nun et al.'s direct valine synthesis starts with methane, ethane and ammonia already in the gas phase.\n\nThus \u201cimpact synthesis of valine\u201d currently joins two pieces of evidence that have not been experimentally unified: old direct valine synthesis under a strongly reduced gas mixture, and modern geochemically motivated synthesis of simpler amino acids from CO\u2082/N\u2082/Fe/Ni. Demonstrating valine in a ^13C/^15N-labeled CO\u2082/N\u2082 impact experiment would materially strengthen this framework.\n\n### Meteorite parent bodies\n\nRadiolytic and hydrothermal formaldehyde/ammonia chemistry has perhaps the clearest environmental case for an **extraterrestrial** contribution. Carbonaceous parent bodies experienced aqueous alteration, and ^26Al provides both heat and ionizing radiation. Kebukawa's \u03b3-radiolysis experiments showed dose-dependent amino-acid synthesis, while Ishikawa et al. subsequently included valine among the minor products.\n\nThe caveat is that amino acids are generated as members of large, low-selectivity product suites, frequently after acid hydrolysis of complex precursor material. The concentration of formaldehyde, ammonia and related small organics in actual parent-body pore solutions, the extent of transport and destruction, and the quantitative contribution of \u03b3 radiation relative to ordinary thermal chemistry remain model-dependent. The 2017 Kebukawa hydrothermal experiment is particularly important as an analytical warning because its apparent valine was L-only and was judged likely contamination.\n\n## Competing frameworks, evidence against them, and unresolved bottlenecks\n\nThe strongest general criticism that applies across the field is **precursor displacement**: a route can solve the final C\u2013N bond formation while moving the harder carbon-skeleton problem upstream. The 2024 Ni/H\u2082 route is the clearest example. Once \u03b1-ketoisovalerate exists, valine formation at 22 \u00b0C is straightforward. But \u03b1-ketoisovalerate is already almost valine; the experimentally unresolved problem is the abiotic creation of its branched C5 skeleton in appreciable concentration from simpler C1\u2013C3 compounds.\n\nThe converse problem affects Strecker chemistry. Its advantage is that it **actually constructs the extra carbon** of the amino-acid carboxyl group from HCN, and electric discharges can generate HCN and carbonyl compounds from small gases. But valine specifically demands isobutyraldehyde. Producing a broad soup of C1\u2013C4 aldehydes is chemically easy compared with generating the branched aldehyde at a concentration high enough for valine to compete with glycine, alanine, AIB, norvaline and numerous non-protein isomers. Ring et al.'s broad product distribution illustrates that selectivity problem experimentally.\n\nThe **NH\u2084CN/acetone route** has a particularly unresolved carbon-accounting problem. Because ordinary Strecker chemistry of acetone should lead to AIB rather than valine, direct valine formation implies secondary C\u2013C transformations in the cyanide polymerization network. Friedmann & Miller established the product, but the detailed sequence and its yield dependence on cyanide, ketone concentration, pH and temperature have never been developed into a modern isotope-resolved mechanism. Their failure to reproduce valine from NH\u2084CN alone also invalidates the simplest version of older cyanide-polymer claims.\n\nThe **formaldehyde/ammonia route** has an analogous mechanistic gap. It starts from admirably simple C1/N1 feedstocks but must execute several selective C\u2013C couplings to reach a branched C5 skeleton. Fox & Windsor demonstrated valine among hydrolysis products, yet modern HCHO/NH\u2083 experiments yield complex polymeric material and broad amino-acid suites, and Kebukawa et al.'s hydrothermal analogue did not produce analytically secure abiotic valine. The exact branch-forming sequence remains unknown.\n\nFor **shock chemistry**, the principal controversy is geochemical rather than chemical. Valine is demonstrably made by shock-heating a CH\u2084/C\u2082H\u2086/NH\u2083 mixture, but Hadean mantle redox constraints do not support that mixture as a general background atmosphere. More realistic CO\u2082/N\u2082 impact chemistry works, but so far only for simpler amino acids.\n\nThe **Bucherer\u2013Bergs \u03b1-ketoacid route** is chemically convincing but carries three distinct relevance questions: whether \u03b1-ketoisovalerate was available; whether cyanide and ammonia could coexist at useful concentrations; and whether the long room-temperature plus 80 \u00b0C processing sequence could occur without destroying other network components. Pulletikurti et al.'s optimized experiments used 0.5 M bicarbonate and comparatively concentrated substrates, making environmental concentration mechanisms an essential part of any proposed setting.\n\nThe **FTT framework** currently should not be promoted as a valine synthesis. Pizzarello's 2012 experiment is valuable because it uses exceptionally simple CO/H\u2082/NH\u2083 feedstocks and geologically relevant catalytic materials, but its 370 \u00b0C product suite contains AIB, norvaline and isovaline rather than valine. The strong production of AIB by magnetite shows that mineral surfaces can select branching patterns, but the selection is not the biological valine skeleton in that study.\n\nThere is also a broader **congruence-with-biochemistry debate**. Strecker chemistry is efficient and geochemically versatile but has no direct analogue in extant amino-acid biosynthesis. \u03b1-Ketoacid reductive amination is structurally congruent with extant metabolism, which is one reason Kaur et al. emphasize it, but metabolic resemblance by itself does not establish historical continuity. A reaction network is compelling only if the required \u03b1-ketoacid itself can arise from geochemically available starting materials under compatible conditions.\n\nFinally, there is the **chirality problem**. None of the strongest direct routes discussed here establishes a robust mechanism for generating protein-level L-valine enantiomeric excess from achiral feedstocks. Indeed, Kebukawa et al.'s observation of exclusively L-valine in an otherwise abiotic experiment was interpreted as a warning sign for contamination rather than as spontaneous enantioselection. For synthesis-network design, valine production and valine homochirality should therefore be treated as separate problems unless an explicit symmetry-breaking mechanism is demonstrated.\n\n## Implications for synthesis-network design\n\nThe most productive network representation is to treat valine as having **two convergent immediate-precursor nodes**:\n\n**Aldehyde branch**\n\\[\n\\mathrm{CH_4/CO/CO_2 \\rightarrow C_1-C_3\\ carbonyls \\rightarrow\nisobutyraldehyde}\n\\]\n\\[\n\\mathrm{isobutyraldehyde + NH_3 + HCN \\rightarrow\nvaline\\ aminonitrile \\rightarrow valine}\n\\]\n\nand\n\n**\u03b1-ketoacid branch**\n\\[\n\\mathrm{small\\ carbonyls/pyruvate \\rightarrow branched\\ C_5\\ chemistry\n\\rightarrow \\alpha\\!-\\!ketoisovalerate}\n\\]\n\\[\n\\mathrm{\\alpha\\!-\\!ketoisovalerate + NH_3 + H_2\n\\xrightarrow[22\\,^\\circ C,\\ pH\\sim8]{Ni/Pd} valine}\n\\]\n\nThe second arrow in each branch is reasonably well supported; the first arrow is the strategic bottleneck. The Strecker branch has abundant experimental evidence for the general **HCN + aldehyde \u2192 aminonitrile \u2192 amino acid** machinery, but insufficient quantitative work on prebiotic formation of isobutyraldehyde. The \u03b1-ketoacid branch has excellent modern evidence for **\u03b1-ketoisovalerate \u2192 valine**, but currently lacks a comparably convincing abiotic synthesis of \u03b1-ketoisovalerate from pyruvate or simple C1\u2013C3 feedstocks.\n\nFor experimental prioritization, the highest-value missing experiment would therefore not simply be \u201cmake valine again.\u201d It would be an isotope-resolved synthesis of the **branched skeleton**. Particularly discriminating experiments would test whether plausible atmospheric, photochemical, mineral, impact or H\u2082-driven networks can generate ^13C-labeled isobutyraldehyde or \u03b1-ketoisovalerate from CO\u2082/CO/HCHO/pyruvate, followed *in the same reaction system* by capture as valine. The need for this follows directly from the precursor gaps in both the Miller/Ring and Kaur pathways.\n\nA second priority is to quantify competition. In Kaur's \u03b1-ketoisovalerate experiment, geochemically attractive Ni and meteoritic metal make substantial **2-hydroxyisovalerate**, whereas Pd strongly favors valine. A realistic network therefore needs to model carbon partitioning between amino-, hydroxy-, decomposition- and adsorption products, not just report the presence of valine. Likewise, electric-discharge chemistry produces many constitutional isomers and non-protein amino acids.\n\nA third priority is to introduce realistic **salinity, mineral aging and concentration cycling**. Huber & W\u00e4chtersh\u00e4user showed that ionic strength, FeS preparation and sulfide loading can change reductive-amination behavior dramatically, while Pulletikurti et al.'s Bucherer\u2013Bergs chemistry uses much more concentrated bicarbonate, cyanide and organic substrate than would be expected in an unconfined ocean. Evaporation, freezing, adsorption, pore confinement or repeated wet\u2013dry cycling therefore cannot merely be environmental decoration; where high concentrations are required, a quantitative concentration mechanism is part of the chemical hypothesis.\n\nOverall, the **best-supported direct valine-forming chemical step under mild, geochemically interpretable conditions is presently \u03b1-ketoisovalerate reductive amination with H\u2082 over Ni-bearing or meteoritic material**, especially because Kaur et al. directly quantified valine/hydroxy-acid competition at 22 \u00b0C. The **best-supported route that constructs valine from substantially smaller organic molecules remains Strecker/discharge chemistry**, but its strongest demonstrations use reducing atmospheres whose global Hadean relevance is contested. **Parent-body \u03b3 chemistry and reduced-gas shock chemistry independently show that valine can also emerge from much less pathway-specific, energy-rich reaction networks**, though with low selectivity and substantial environmental qualifications. None of these results yet establishes a uniquely favored historical route.\n\n## Primary literature cited\n\n**Miller, Stanley L. (1957), *Biochimica et Biophysica Acta* 23, 480\u2013489, \u201cThe mechanism of synthesis of amino acids by electric discharges.\u201d** Established HCN/aldehyde/aminonitrile Strecker chemistry as the mechanism underlying much of electric-discharge amino-acid synthesis and found no substantial catalytic effect from ferrous ammonium sulfate.\n\n**Friedmann, Nadav & Stanley L. Miller (1969), *Nature* 221, 1152\u20131153, \u201cSynthesis of Valine and Isoleucine in Primitive Earth Conditions.\u201d** Failed to confirm valine from NH\u2084CN polymerization alone but directly obtained valine when acetone was added.\n\n**Bar-Nun, A.; Bar-Nun, N.; Bauer, S. H.; & Sagan, C. (1970), *Science* 168, 470\u2013472, \u201cShock Synthesis of Amino Acids in Simulated Primitive Environments.\u201d** Directly produced glycine, alanine, valine and leucine in a reduced gas mixture exposed to shock heating and rapid quenching.\n\n**Fox, Sidney W. & Charles R. Windsor (1970), *Science* 170, 984\u2013986, \u201cSynthesis of Amino Acids by the Heating of Formaldehyde and Ammonia.\u201d** Reported valine among amino acids obtained after hydrolysis of heated formaldehyde/ammonia reaction products.\n\n**Ring, D.; Wolman, Y.; Friedmann, N.; & Miller, S. L. (1972), *Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences USA* 69, 765\u2013768, \u201cPrebiotic Synthesis of Hydrophobic and Protein Amino Acids.\u201d** Confirmed valine and a broad suite of hydrophobic amino acids by chromatography and GC\u2013MS after electric discharge through methane/nitrogen/water with traces of ammonia.\n\n**Huber, Claudia & G\u00fcnter W\u00e4chtersh\u00e4user (2003), *Tetrahedron Letters* 44, 1695\u20131697, \u201cPrimordial Reductive Amination Revisited.\u201d** Demonstrated FeS/Fe(OH)\u2082-mediated reductive amination of several \u03b1-ketoacids; important mechanistic precedent, but not a direct valine experiment.\n\n**Cleaves, H. James; Chalmers, John H.; Lazcano, Antonio; Miller, Stanley L.; & Bada, Jeffrey L. (2008), *Origins of Life and Evolution of Biospheres* 38, 105\u2013115, \u201cA Reassessment of Prebiotic Organic Synthesis in Neutral Planetary Atmospheres.\u201d** Re-evaluated CO\u2082/N\u2082 discharge chemistry; relevant to atmospheric plausibility but not a direct valine demonstration.\n\n**Trail, Dustin; Watson, E. Bruce; & Tailby, Nicholas D. (2011), *Nature* 480, 79\u201382, \u201cThe Oxidation State of Hadean Magmas and Implications for Early Earth's Atmosphere.\u201d** Provided zircon-based evidence against persistently strongly reducing volcanic outgassing in much of the Hadean.\n\n**Wang, Wei; Li, Qiliang; Yang, Bin; Liu, Xiaoyang; Yang, Yanqiang; & Su, Wenhui (2012), *Chemical Communications* 48, 2146\u20132148, \u201cPhotocatalytic Reversible Amination of \u03b1-Keto Acids on a ZnS Surface: Implications for the Prebiotic Metabolism.\u201d** Demonstrated mineral-photocatalytic \u03b1-ketoacid amination, although not \u03b1-ketoisovalerate/valine.\n\n**Pizzarello, Sandra (2012), *Meteoritics & Planetary Science* 47, 1291\u20131296, \u201cCatalytic Syntheses of Amino Acids and Their Significance for Nebular and Planetary Chemistry.\u201d** Demonstrated CO/H\u2082/NH\u2083 FTT-type amino-acid synthesis on meteoritic and mineral catalysts; notably produced AIB and isovaline but did not report valine.\n\n**Kuwahara, Hideharu; Eto, Midori; Kawamoto, Yukinori; Kurihara, Hironari; Kaneko, Takeo; Obayashi, Yumiko; & Kobayashi, Kensei (2012), *Origins of Life and Evolution of Biospheres* 42, 533\u2013541, \u201cThe Use of Ascorbate as an Oxidation Inhibitor in Prebiotic Amino Acid Synthesis: A Cautionary Note.\u201d** Demonstrated that ascorbate can produce misleading apparent amino-acid signals in neutral-atmosphere synthesis experiments.\n\n**Kebukawa, Yoko et al. (2017), *Science Advances* 3, e1602093, \u201cOne-pot Synthesis of Amino Acid Precursors with Insoluble Organic Matter in Planetesimals with Aqueous Activity.\u201d** Produced extensive amino-acid precursor chemistry from formaldehyde/glycolaldehyde/ammonia but treated observed L-only valine as likely contamination.\n\n**Takeuchi, Y.; Furukawa, Y.; Kobayashi, T.; Sekine, T.; Terada, N.; & Kakegawa, T. (2020), *Scientific Reports* 10, 9220, \u201cImpact-induced amino acid formation on Hadean Earth and Noachian Mars.\u201d** Demonstrated isotopically verified amino-acid formation from CO\u2082/N\u2082/H\u2082O plus Fe/Ni-bearing impact materials, but not valine.\n\n**Kebukawa, Yoko (2022), *ACS Central Science* 8, 1664\u20131671, \u201cGamma-Ray-Induced Amino Acid Formation in Aqueous Small Bodies in the Early Solar System.\u201d** Established dose-dependent amino-acid production by \u03b3 irradiation of aqueous formaldehyde/ammonia mixtures.\n\n**Pulletikurti, Sunil; Yadav, Mahipal; Springsteen, Greg; & Krishnamurthy, Ramanarayanan (2022), *Nature Chemistry* 14, 1142\u20131150, \u201cPrebiotic Synthesis of \u03b1-Amino Acids and Orotate from \u03b1-Ketoacids Potentiates Transition to Extant Metabolic Pathways.\u201d** Established Bucherer\u2013Bergs conversion of several \u03b1-ketoacids to amino acids via cyanide/ammonia chemistry; directly relevant mechanistically to \u03b1-ketoisovalerate but did not itself provide a valine-specific substrate experiment.\n\n**Kaur, Harpreet; Rauscher, Sophia A.; Werner, Emilie; Song, Youngdong; Yi, Jing; Kaz\u00f6ne, Wahnyalo; Martin, William F.; T\u00fcys\u00fcz, Harun; & Moran, Joseph (2024), *Chem* 10, 1528\u20131540, \u201cA Prebiotic Krebs Cycle Analog Generates Amino Acids with H\u2082 and NH\u2083 over Nickel.\u201d** Provides the strongest modern quantitative direct experiment for \u03b1-ketoisovalerate \u2192 valine using H\u2082 and Ni, Pd or meteoritic catalysts under mild aqueous conditions.\n\n**Ishikawa, Akari; Kebukawa, Yoko; Kobayashi, Kensei; & Yoda, Isao (2024), *Life* 14, 103, \u201cGamma-Ray-Induced Amino Acid Formation during Aqueous Alteration in Small Bodies: The Effects of Compositions of Starting Solutions.\u201d** Extended parent-body radiolysis experiments and reported valine among minor amino-acid products.", "parsed_response": {"source": "reports/deep_research_GPT6/Valine-Run1.txt", "config": "deep_research_gpt6", "length": 46453}, "reasoning": "", "duration_ms": 0.0, "success": true, "error_message": null}
{"timestamp": "2026-10-19T16:10:39.401656", "agent_name": "SynthesisNetworkAgent", "method_name": "generate_synthesis_network", "step": null, "attempt": null, "system_message": "\n# SYNTHESIS NETWORK AGENT - Complete Prebiotic Synthesis Network Generation\n\nYou are an expert prebiotic geochemist who designs complete synthesis networks from simple\nmolecules to complex biomolecules. You synthesize knowledge across three terrestrial prebiotic\nenvironments:\n\n1. **Hydrothermal Environments** (350-600K, high pressure, deep-sea vents)\n2. **Surface Environments** (300-370K, ambient pressure, wet-dry cycles, UV exposure)\n3. **Biochemical/Prebiotic** (variable conditions, complex biomolecule assembly)\n\nYour task is to generate a COMPLETE SYNTHESIS NETWORK in a SINGLE response - not individual steps.\n\n---\n\n# SECTION 1: HYDROTHERMAL ENVIRONMENTS\n\n## Context\nDeep-sea hydrothermal systems. High pressure, temperatures between 350-600K, pH gradients\nacross vent walls, and mineral surfaces.\n\n## Environments\n- **Alkaline Hydrothermal Vents**: 350-450K, pH 9-11, H\u2082-rich, serpentinization-driven\n- **Black Smoker Vents**: 500-650K, acidic, metal-sulfide rich\n- **Subseafloor Hydrothermal**: 370-470K, moderate pH, rock-water interactions\n\n## Minerals Present in This Setting (examples, non-exhaustive)\nIron sulfides (e.g., mackinawite, greigite, pyrite), iron oxides (e.g., magnetite), native metals\nand alloys. Use a mineral only if it is chemically justified for the specific reaction.\n\n---\n\n# SECTION 2: SURFACE ENVIRONMENTS\n\n## Context\nAmbient pressure, temperatures between 300-370K, wet-dry cycles, UV exposure, and evaporitic\npools.\n\n## Environments\n- **Evaporitic Pools**: 300-370K, wet-dry cycles, mineral-rich shores\n- **Volcanic Hot Springs**: 320-370K, acidic, sulfur-rich, boron minerals\n- **Tidal Flats**: 280-330K, periodic wetting/drying, UV exposure\n- **Subaerial Geothermal Fields**: 300-370K, steam vents, mineral surfaces\n\n## Minerals Present in This Setting (examples, non-exhaustive)\nClays (e.g., montmorillonite), phosphate minerals, borate minerals, metal sulfides, metal oxides.\nUse a mineral only if it is chemically justified for the specific reaction.\n\n---\n\n# SECTION 3: BIOCHEMICAL/PREBIOTIC\n\n## Context\nAssembly of more complex biomolecules under plausible prebiotic scenarios, under variable\nconditions including pools, interfaces, and mineral surfaces.\n\n## Environments\n- **Prebiotic Pools**: Concentrated solutions of organic molecules\n- **Mineral Interfaces**: Mineral surfaces that can adsorb or template reactants\n- **Lipid Membranes**: Protocell-like compartments for concentrated chemistry\n- **Geothermal Cycling**: Repeated heating/cooling\n\n## Global Constraints (all environments)\n- NO molecular oxygen (anoxic early Earth)\n- Strong UV radiation at the surface (no ozone layer)\n- Iron abundant in reduced form (Fe\u00b2\u207a)\n\n---\n\n# SECTION 4: NETWORK STRUCTURE REQUIREMENTS\n\nYour output must be a BIPARTITE GRAPH with two distinct node types:\n\n## 1. MOLECULE NODES\n- Represent chemical species (substrates, products, intermediates)\n- Each molecule appears ONCE in the network, even if used by multiple reactions\n- Include: id, formula, IUPAC name, common name, InChI, role, environment_formed\n\n## 2. REACTION NODES\n- Each reaction is a separate node with a unique ID (e.g., \"rxn_001\")\n- Has INPUT molecules (reactants) and OUTPUT molecules (products)\n- Includes: id, name, inputs[], outputs[], environment, agents[], conditions, mechanism, reasoning\n- List each molecule ID at most once in inputs[] and once in outputs[]; stoichiometric coefficients\n  are not represented and are inferred automatically, so never repeat an ID to express them\n\n## NEUTRALITY (CRITICAL)\n- No starting feedstock, intermediate, reaction family, or prebiotic framework is preferred.\n  Choose routes solely on chemical and geochemical merit for this specific target.\n- Environments may be used in any order and in any combination; a network that uses only one\n  environment is acceptable if that is what the chemistry supports.\n- Do not add intermediates, shared nodes, or cross-environment transfers merely to make the\n  network look interconnected. Include them only when the chemistry requires them.\n\n## DESIGN PRINCIPLES\n1. **Agents/catalysts**: Specify a catalyst or mineral agent in the \"agents\" field when one is\n   used and justify it; use null when no catalyst is needed.\n2. **Reaction diversity**: Use whatever reaction classes the chemistry of the target requires.\n3. **Alternative routes**: Where the chemistry supports more than one route, include them as\n   separate pathways.\n\n## NETWORK STRUCTURE REQUIREMENTS\n\n- Minimum 8-15 molecule nodes\n- Minimum 6-12 reaction nodes\n- No isolated subgraphs - all nodes must be connected\n\n---\n\n# SECTION 5: REQUIRED JSON OUTPUT FORMAT\n\nYou MUST output a JSON object with this exact structure:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"target_molecule\": \"<name of target>\",\n  \"overall_strategy\": \"<high-level explanation of the synthesis approach>\",\n  \"molecules\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_001\",\n      \"formula\": \"<chemical formula>\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"<IUPAC name>\",\n      \"common_name\": \"<common name or null>\",\n      \"inchi\": \"<InChI string or null>\",\n      \"role\": \"<starting | intermediate | hub_intermediate | target>\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"<Hydrothermal | Surface | Biochemical | null>\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"reactions\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_001\",\n      \"name\": \"<descriptive reaction name>\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_001\", \"mol_002\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_003\"],\n      \"environment\": \"<Hydrothermal | Surface | Biochemical>\",\n      \"agents\": [\"<catalyst/mineral agent, if any>\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"<e.g., 350-400K>\",\n        \"pressure\": \"<e.g., high, 1 atm>\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"<catalyst name, or null>\",\n        \"aqueous\": \"<true | false>\",\n        \"other\": \"<additional conditions>\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"<brief mechanism description>\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"<scientific justification>\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"pathways\": [\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P1\",\n      \"name\": \"<descriptive pathway name>\",\n      \"description\": \"<pathway description>\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_003\", \"rxn_007\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_004\", \"mol_008\"]\n    }\n  ],\n  \"hub_molecules\": [\"<mol_id, if any>\"],\n  \"convergence_points\": [\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_004\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\"rxn_002\", \"rxn_005\"],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\"rxn_003\", \"rxn_006\", \"rxn_009\"]\n    }\n  ]\n}\n```\n\nNotes on \"hub_molecules\" and \"convergence_points\": these are descriptive fields only. List any\nmolecules that, in the network you designed, happen to be consumed or produced by multiple reactions.\nThey may be empty lists. Do not design the network to fill them.\n\n---\n\n# SECTION 6: LITERATURE-GUIDED SYNTHESIS (when research findings are provided)\n\nWhen research findings are provided, use them as evidence to inform your design:\n\n1. **Weigh the evidence**:\n   - Prefer routes and conditions that are well supported experimentally in the provided research\n   - Take into account the critiques, contested points, and limitations the research reports\n   - You may depart from the routes described in the research when the chemistry justifies it;\n     state the justification explicitly in the \"reasoning\" field\n\n2. **CITE papers** in the \"reasoning\" field of each reaction:\n   - Reference specific studies by author and year, e.g., \"(Author, Year)\"\n   - When multiple studies support a reaction, cite the most relevant ones\n   - If a reaction has no literature support from the provided research, explicitly state it is speculative and justify why it is necessary\n\n3. **ADDRESS gaps**: If the research identifies open questions or challenges, acknowledge them in your reasoning\n\n---\n\n# IMPORTANT GUIDELINES\n\n1. Generate the ENTIRE network in one response - do not propose individual steps\n2. Ensure molecules are defined BEFORE being referenced in reactions\n3. Every molecule ID in reactions must exist in the molecules array\n4. Pathways must trace valid routes through the reaction network\n5. Consider realistic prebiotic conditions for each reaction's stated environment\n6. Include rate-limiting or key bottleneck reactions\n7. The network should tell a coherent chemical story from simple to complex\n8. When deep research findings are provided, cite relevant literature in reaction reasoning fields\n", "user_message": "\nGenerate a SYNTHESIS NETWORK for: Valine\n\nREQUIREMENTS:\n- Generate a bipartite network with molecule nodes and reaction nodes\n- Include 10 distinct pathways to the target\n- Ensure at least 8-15 molecules and 6-12 reactions\n- Choose feedstocks, intermediates, reaction types, and environments on chemical merit alone\n\nOutput the complete network as JSON following the schema in your instructions.\n\n\n=== LITERATURE RESEARCH FINDINGS ===\nThe following deep research report contains peer-reviewed literature on prebiotic synthesis\nof Valine. Use it as evidence to inform your network design.\n\n=== DEEP RESEARCH FINDINGS ===\n# Prebiotic Synthesis of Valine: Routes, Precursors, Catalysts, Environmental Context, and Open Problems\n\n## Executive assessment\n\nValine is a useful stress test for prebiotic synthesis models because it is not merely an \u03b1-amino acid: it requires construction of a **branched C5 carbon skeleton**, HOOC\u2013CH(NH\u2082)\u2013CH(CH\u2083)\u2082. The literature contains several direct demonstrations of abiotic valine formation, but the evidence is uneven. The strongest direct demonstrations fall into five chemically distinct families: **Strecker-type atmospheric/electric-discharge chemistry; ammonium-cyanide/ketone chemistry; high-temperature formaldehyde\u2013ammonia chemistry; shock chemistry; and reductive amination of \u03b1-ketoisovalerate with H\u2082 over metal catalysts**. More recently, **\u03b3-radiolysis of formaldehyde/ammonia solutions in meteorite-parent-body analogues** has also generated valine as a minor product.\n\nThe central conclusion is that **no published route yet closes the entire synthesis from the simplest geochemical feedstocks to valine under one experimentally demonstrated, geochemically well-constrained environment**. The classical Strecker family can, in principle, build the C5 skeleton by adding the carbon of HCN to the C4 aldehyde isobutyraldehyde, and electric-discharge experiments genuinely produce valine, but the strongly reducing atmospheric mixtures that produce aldehydes and HCN most efficiently are difficult to reconcile with evidence that Hadean mantle outgassing was comparatively oxidized. Conversely, the 2024 H\u2082/Ni chemistry of Kaur and co-workers converts the biologically congruent precursor **\u03b1-ketoisovalerate directly to valine under remarkably mild conditions**, but \u03b1-ketoisovalerate was supplied at 30 mM: its abiotic synthesis from CO\u2082, CO, pyruvate, or other simpler feedstocks under the same conditions remains the major missing step.\n\nA useful evidence hierarchy is therefore:\n\n| Route family | Direct valine demonstrated? | Simplest feedstocks experimentally connected to the route | Main bottleneck |\n|---|---:|---|---|\n| Electric discharge \u2192 aldehydes + HCN \u2192 Strecker chemistry | **Yes** | CH\u2084, NH\u2083 or N\u2082, H\u2082O, H\u2082 | Reduced atmosphere; production of enough C4 isobutyraldehyde; low selectivity |\n| NH\u2084CN + ketone network | **Yes** | HCN/NH\u2083 plus acetone | Mechanism of C5 branching is poorly resolved; NH\u2084CN alone does **not** reliably give valine |\n| Heated HCHO + NH\u2083 | **Yes, historically** | Formaldehyde, ammonia, water | Harsh heating; poorly defined bound intermediates; weak valine-specific quantitation/reproducibility |\n| Shock-heated reduced gases | **Yes** | CH\u2084, C\u2082H\u2086, NH\u2083 | Highly reducing gas composition not representative of favored global Hadean atmosphere |\n| CO\u2082/N\u2082 impact chemistry | **Not yet** | CO\u2082/HCO\u2083\u207b, N\u2082, H\u2082O, Fe, Ni, silicates | Experiments stop at simpler amino acids; no demonstrated valine |\n| \u03b1-Ketoisovalerate + NH\u2083 + H\u2082, Ni/Pd/meteorite | **Yes** | H\u2082, NH\u2083 plus preformed C5 \u03b1-ketoacid | Origin and concentration of \u03b1-ketoisovalerate |\n| \u03b1-Ketoacid + CN\u207b/NH\u2083 Bucherer\u2013Bergs chemistry | **Mechanistically plausible; valine not directly shown in the 2022 substrate set** | \u03b1-Ketoacid, CN\u207b, ammonia source, bicarbonate | Requires preformed \u03b1-ketoisovalerate and concentrated cyanide/ammonia; long processing |\n| CO/H\u2082/NH\u2083 Fischer\u2013Tropsch-type mineral chemistry | **No in the principal 2012 study** | CO, H\u2082, NH\u2083 | Produces other amino acids, notably AIB; valine was not reported |\n| \u03b3-Radiolysis of HCHO/NH\u2083 parent-body solutions | **Yes, minor product** | HCHO, NH\u2083, H\u2082O, sometimes CH\u2083OH/glycolaldehyde | Product complexity, dose/concentration assumptions, bound precursors, extraterrestrial rather than terrestrial setting |\n\nThe route classifications above follow the direct experiments of Miller (1957, *Biochimica et Biophysica Acta*), Friedmann & Miller (1969, *Nature*), Bar-Nun, Bar-Nun, Bauer & Sagan (1970, *Science*), Fox & Windsor (1970, *Science*), Ring, Wolman, Friedmann & Miller (1972, *Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences*), Pizzarello (2012, *Meteoritics & Planetary Science*), Pulletikurti, Yadav, Springsteen & Krishnamurthy (2022, *Nature Chemistry*), Kaur et al. (2024, *Chem*), and Ishikawa, Kebukawa, Kobayashi & Yoda (2024, *Life*).\n\n## Known synthesis routes and mechanisms\n\n### Electric-discharge chemistry and the Strecker route\n\nThe most chemically explicit classical route is the one established mechanistically by **Stanley Miller (1957, *Biochimica et Biophysica Acta*)**. Electric discharge through CH\u2084\u2013NH\u2083\u2013H\u2082O\u2013H\u2082 mixtures produced **HCN and aldehydes**, which then entered Strecker chemistry. Miller showed that aminonitrile hydrolysis proceeded quickly enough to account for the amino acids accumulated after roughly a week of sparking. Replacing NH\u2083 with N\u2082 still generated the same general product types, but at lower yield. Addition of ferrous ammonium sulfate did not substantially change product yields, arguing that no Fe/sulfate mineral catalysis was necessary in this experiment.\n\nFor valine specifically, the chemically straightforward Strecker sequence is:\n\n\\[\n(CH_3)_2CHCHO + NH_3 \\rightleftharpoons (CH_3)_2CHCH{=}NH + H_2O\n\\]\n\n\\[\n(CH_3)_2CHCH{=}NH + HCN \\rightarrow (CH_3)_2CHCH(NH_2)CN\n\\]\n\nfollowed by nitrile hydrolysis:\n\n\\[\n(CH_3)_2CHCH(NH_2)CN + 2H_2O \\rightarrow\n(CH_3)_2CHCH(NH_2)CO_2H + NH_3\n\\]\n\nThus the crucial carbon precursor is **2-methylpropanal, or isobutyraldehyde**. Its four-carbon branched skeleton supplies valine C2\u2013C5, while cyanide supplies the carboxyl carbon. This is an important point for network design: making HCN is not enough; an efficient prebiotic valine network also has to generate a significant pool of **branched C4 aldehyde**.\n\nDirect confirmation that such discharge chemistry reaches valine came from **Ring, Wolman, Friedmann & Miller (1972, *PNAS*)**. They discharged a mixture of methane, nitrogen and water containing traces of ammonia and identified, using ion-exchange chromatography together with GC\u2013MS, glycine, alanine, \u03b1-aminobutyric acid, \u03b1-aminoisobutyric acid, **valine**, norvaline, isovaline, leucine, isoleucine, alloisoleucine, norleucine and several polar amino acids. The broad isomer distribution is significant: the experiment demonstrates chemical accessibility but **poor structural selectivity** for the biological amino-acid set.\n\nThe main environmental objection is now substantial. **Trail, Watson & Tailby (2011, *Nature*)** used Ce redox systematics in Hadean zircons to infer mantle-derived magmas near the fayalite\u2013magnetite\u2013quartz redox buffer by about 4.35 Ga. Such outgassing is predicted to favor H\u2082O, CO\u2082, SO\u2082 and N\u2082 rather than the CH\u2084-, NH\u2083- and H\u2082-rich mixture most favorable to Miller-type amino-acid synthesis. This does not prove that strongly reducing local or transient environments never existed, but it makes a globally persistent Miller-style atmosphere difficult to defend geochemically.\n\nAttempts to extend spark chemistry to more neutral CO\u2082/N\u2082 atmospheres illustrate the problem. **Cleaves, Chalmers, Lazcano, Miller & Bada (2008, *Origins of Life and Evolution of Biospheres*)** found that amino-acid recovery from CO\u2082/N\u2082 discharge mixtures could be raised considerably by controlling pH and suppressing oxidative destruction during work-up. In experiments with approximately 0.13 bar CO\u2082 and 0.13 bar N\u2082, CaCO\u2083 buffering raised total amino-acid recovery relative to unbuffered experiments. However, the published product set emphasized glycine, alanine, \u03b2-alanine, AIB, aspartate, glutamate and serine rather than valine. Moreover, **Kuwahara, Eto, Kawamoto, Kurihara, Kaneko, Obayashi & Kobayashi (2012, *Origins of Life and Evolution of Biospheres*)** used isotopic tests to show that ascorbate, which had been used as an oxidation inhibitor in some such experiments, could itself contribute carbon to apparent amino-acid products. Thus neutral-atmosphere spark chemistry remains relevant for simpler precursors, but **it is not currently a strong direct experimental route to valine**.\n\n### Ammonium cyanide plus ketones\n\n**Nadav Friedmann & Stanley Miller (1969, *Nature*)** directly revisited reports that polymerized ammonium cyanide produced the branched protein amino acids. Their result is unusually important because it contains both a positive result and a published refutation: they **could not detect valine, isoleucine or leucine from NH\u2084CN polymerization alone** when more stringent separation was used. Earlier identifications had relied primarily on amino-acid-analyzer elution positions, where non-protein amino acids can co-elute with valine or isoleucine.\n\nWhen **acetone or methyl ethyl ketone was added to NH\u2084CN**, however, Friedmann and Miller obtained significant quantities of valine and, depending on the ketone, alloisoleucine and isoleucine. This therefore constitutes a direct prebiotic valine synthesis route from a cyanide/ammonia pool plus simple ketones.\n\nMechanistically, this route is more problematic than the ordinary aldehyde Strecker pathway. A simple Strecker reaction of acetone, NH\u2083 and HCN gives **\u03b1-aminoisobutyric acid**, a C4 amino acid, not C5 valine. Consequently, the reported appearance of valine from acetone/NH\u2084CN must involve additional carbon\u2013carbon chemistry in the cyanide/ketone reaction network. Friedmann and Miller established the product much more securely than earlier NH\u2084CN work, but they did not establish a unique elementary mechanism for the C5 branch. This makes the route experimentally real but **mechanistically underconstrained for network reconstruction**.\n\nThe study also supplies one of the clearest historical warnings for the valine literature: **NH\u2084CN polymerization by itself should not be counted as an established valine synthesis**. The most careful direct re-investigation found that earlier assignments were probably chromatographic false positives.\n\n### Heated formaldehyde\u2013ammonia chemistry\n\nA chemically very different route was reported by **Sidney Fox & Charles Windsor (1970, *Science*)**. Heating formaldehyde with ammonia generated a complex product which, after hydrolysis, yielded aspartate, glutamate, serine, proline, **valine**, glycine and alanine; glycine was the dominant amino acid. Later experimental literature describing the original procedure reports heating at approximately **185 \u00b0C**, illustrating that this was a substantially hotter process than ordinary aqueous surface chemistry.\n\nThis is not best understood as an ordinary Strecker reaction because HCN and a discrete valine aldehyde were not supplied. The experimentally observed amino acids appeared importantly after hydrolysis, pointing to **nitrogen-containing oligomers or other hydrolysable bound precursors** rather than to a clean solution of free amino acids. The detailed route from C1 formaldehyde to a branched C5 valine skeleton was never resolved at an elementary-mechanism level. Modern work generally treats related HCHO/NH\u2083 chemistry as a nitrogen-containing, formose-like condensation network rather than a single selective synthesis.\n\nModern parent-body simulations expose a major reproducibility problem for valine in this chemistry. **Yoko Kebukawa and co-workers (2017, *Science Advances*)** heated formaldehyde, glycolaldehyde, ammonia and water at **90\u2013250 \u00b0C for 72 h**, producing complex insoluble organic matter and numerous amino-acid precursors. Their analytical standard included valine, but the valine recovered from experiments was detected only as the **L enantiomer**, unlike genuinely abiotic products that were racemic; the authors therefore regarded the apparent valine as probable biological contamination rather than evidence of synthesis.\n\nThat result does not disprove Fox & Windsor's 1970 observation, because the recipes differ substantially, but it means that **thermal HCHO/NH\u2083 chemistry should not be treated as a robust, reproducibly quantified modern valine pathway**. It does robustly generate simpler amino acids and hydrolysable organic material; the C5 branched products remain much less secure.\n\n### Radiation-driven formaldehyde\u2013ammonia chemistry in small bodies\n\nA more recent variant uses ionizing radiation rather than heat alone. **Yoko Kebukawa (2022, *ACS Central Science*)** showed that \u03b3 irradiation of aqueous formaldehyde and ammonia mixtures produced multiple amino acids after hydrolysis. Amino-acid yield scaled approximately linearly with accumulated \u03b3 dose and was comparatively insensitive to dose rate, which matters because the proposed natural energy source is \u03b3 radiation associated with radionuclides such as ^26Al in aqueously altered asteroid parent bodies. The estimated natural radiation production was argued to be chemically significant on parent-body timescales.\n\nThe expanded experiments of **Akari Ishikawa, Yoko Kebukawa, Kensei Kobayashi & Isao Yoda (2024, *Life*)** varied the formaldehyde/ammonia/methanol composition and reported **valine among the lower-abundance amino-acid products**, behind alanine, glycine, \u03b2-alanine, \u03b1-aminobutyric acid and several other species. Representative experiments used large integrated \u03b3 doses on the order of tens to ~90 kGy.\n\nMechanistically, this chemistry is more plausibly a **radical-driven reaction network** than a single Strecker event. Radiolysis generates reactive carbon-, oxygen- and nitrogen-centered species; formaldehyde can also form glycolaldehyde and higher carbonyl compounds, which then feed carbon-chain growth. A discrete, experimentally traced sequence from HCHO to the branched C5 valine skeleton has not yet been established, so the route is direct at the product level but not mechanistically closed.\n\nIts environmental relevance is strongest for **aqueously altered carbonaceous-asteroid parent bodies**, not for a particular early-Earth surface environment. It therefore represents a plausible contribution through extraterrestrial synthesis and delivery rather than a demonstrated endogenous terrestrial source.\n\n### Shock synthesis\n\n**A. Bar-Nun, N. Bar-Nun, S. H. Bauer & Carl Sagan (1970, *Science*)** subjected a reduced gas mixture containing approximately **3.3% CH\u2084, 11% C\u2082H\u2086 and 5.6% NH\u2083, with the balance Ar**, to single-pulse shock heating followed by rapid quenching. They directly observed **glycine, alanine, valine and leucine**. The reported overall efficiency for \u03b1-amino-acid production was about **5 \u00d7 10\u00b9\u2070 molecules per erg of shock energy**. The authors argued that rapid quenching could kinetically trap amino acids that would not be favored at the peak equilibrium temperature.\n\nThe mechanism was not resolved into a valine-specific sequence. At shock temperatures, extensive radical fragmentation and recombination occur, followed by chemistry during rapid cooling. In principle, aldehydes, HCN/nitriles and related intermediates can emerge during such processing, but the study established valine production rather than a unique pathway to it. No mineral catalyst was required.\n\nThe principal critique is environmental. The experiment requires substantial reduced carbon and ammonia. The Hadean zircon constraints of Trail, Watson & Tailby favor substantially more oxidized volcanic outgassing, making this particular bulk-gas composition difficult to justify as the persistent global atmosphere.\n\nModern impact experiments have substantially improved the geochemical starting composition, but they have **not yet closed the gap to valine**. **Takeuchi, Furukawa, Kobayashi, Sekine, Terada & Kakegawa (2020, *Scientific Reports*)** shocked mixtures of Fe, Ni, forsterite, water, bicarbonate/CO\u2082 and N\u2082, designed to represent an Fe-bearing impactor striking an ocean beneath a CO\u2082/N\u2082 atmosphere. Their experiments used an impact velocity of about **0.9 km s\u207b\u00b9**, pressure of about **7 GPa for 0.7 \u03bcs**, a shock-compression temperature near **300 \u00b0C**, and estimated post-shock temperatures around **1500 \u00b0C**. Isotopic labeling confirmed abiotic amino-acid formation. Glycine and alanine were prominent; additional low-molecular-weight amino acids appeared when ammonia was supplied, but **valine was not demonstrated**.\n\nThis creates an instructive contrast: **the old reduced-gas shock experiment reaches valine; the newer geochemically more conservative CO\u2082/N\u2082/Fe impact experiment does not**. Impact chemistry is therefore a credible source of valine precursors and simpler amino acids, but the direct valine evidence still depends on the less geochemically favored reduced gas mixture.\n\n### Reductive amination of \u03b1-ketoisovalerate with hydrogen\n\nThe clearest modern direct conversion of a specifically valine-like metabolic precursor is the work of **Harpreet Kaur, Sophia Rauscher, Emilie Werner, Youngdong Song, Jing Yi, Wahnyalo Kaz\u00f6ne, William Martin, Harun T\u00fcys\u00fcz & Joseph Moran (2024, *Chem*)**. They examined six biological \u03b1-ketoacids, including **\u03b1-ketoisovalerate**, under H\u2082 with ammonia and heterogeneous metal catalysts. \u03b1-Ketoisovalerate gave valine together with its competing carbonyl-reduction product, 2-hydroxyisovalerate.\n\nThe net valine reaction is simple:\n\n\\[\n(CH_3)_2CHCOCO_2H + NH_3 + H_2\n\\rightarrow\n(CH_3)_2CHCH(NH_2)CO_2H + H_2O\n\\]\n\nMechanistically, ammonia reacts with the \u03b1-keto carbonyl to form an imine/iminium-type intermediate, followed by surface-mediated hydrogenation. The competing reaction is direct hydrogenation of the carbonyl to the corresponding \u03b1-hydroxy acid.\n\nThe standard valine experiment used **30 mM \u03b1-ketoisovalerate**, five equivalents NH\u2084HCO\u2083, **5 bar H\u2082**, water at **pH 8**, **22 \u00b0C**, and **72 h**. Three catalysts worked: Pd/C containing 1 mol% Pd relative to substrate, Ni/SiO\u2082\u2013Al\u2082O\u2083 containing 10 mol% Ni, and 3 mg of ground **Aletai iron meteorite** containing substantial Ni. No reduction products were seen in catalyst-free controls.\n\nFor \u03b1-ketoisovalerate specifically, Figure 2 of Kaur et al. gives unusually useful quantitative data. The reported combined amino-acid plus hydroxy-acid yields and amino:hydroxy ratios were:\n\n| Catalyst | Combined valine + 2-hydroxyisovalerate yield | Valine:hydroxy-acid ratio | Approximate valine yield inferred from the figure |\n|---|---:|---:|---:|\n| Ni/SiO\u2082\u2013Al\u2082O\u2083 | 20% | 34:66 | **~6.8%** |\n| Pd/C | 77% | 83:17 | **~64%** |\n| Aletai meteorite | 74% | 18:82 | **~13%** |\n\nThe last column is an arithmetic inference from the published combined yield and product ratio, not a separately reported value. The striking point is the trade-off in plausibility versus selectivity: **Pd is much more amino-acid selective, whereas Ni and meteoritic metal are geochemically easier to motivate but favor substantial hydroxy-acid formation**.\n\nThe same study found that the chemistry can function at ammonium concentrations from **6 to 150 mM**. It also linked reductive amination to H\u2082-driven reductive-aldol chemistry involving glyoxylate, oxaloacetate and other \u03b1-ketoacids. Some aldol steps occur even without catalyst, whereas the critical hydrogenation steps require the metal catalyst. The paper notes that hydrothermal fluids can contain approximately **1\u201315 mmol kg\u207b\u00b9 dissolved H\u2082**, making the reductant geochemically attractive in serpentinization-associated systems.\n\nThis is therefore one of the strongest **final-step** valine syntheses in the literature, but it is not yet a de novo valine synthesis from CO\u2082/H\u2082. The experiment **supplied \u03b1-ketoisovalerate**. The coupled reaction network generated \u03b1-ketoglutarate and other \u03b1-ketoacids from simpler precursors but did not establish a pathway from CO\u2082, pyruvate or glyoxylate all the way to \u03b1-ketoisovalerate. In modern biochemistry, construction of the valine skeleton from pyruvate involves a highly specific branched-chain pathway; an experimentally validated nonenzymatic equivalent remains missing.\n\n### Bucherer\u2013Bergs chemistry from \u03b1-ketoacids\n\n**Sunil Pulletikurti, Mahipal Yadav, Greg Springsteen & Ramanarayanan Krishnamurthy (2022, *Nature Chemistry*)** demonstrated a general alternative in which \u03b1-ketoacids react with cyanide and ammonia sources through **Bucherer\u2013Bergs-type chemistry** to produce the corresponding \u03b1-amino acids. Their mechanistic sequence goes through cyanide addition/amino-nitrile chemistry, cyclization to a **hydantoin**, hydrolysis to an N-carbamoyl amino acid, and finally amino-acid formation.\n\nUnder their optimized general procedure, an \u03b1-ketoacid was used at roughly **0.35 M**, with 2 equivalents of NH\u2084Cl or diamidophosphate, 1.2 equivalents NaCN, and **0.5 M bicarbonate at pH 8.5**. The mixture was held at room temperature for **21 days** and then heated at **80 \u00b0C for 14 days** to complete amino-acid formation. Their supporting information shows explicitly that hydantoin hydrolysis can proceed through an N-carbamoyl amino acid and that ammonia-source chemistry affects the hydrolysis.\n\nFor valine-network design, the obvious substrate is **\u03b1-ketoisovalerate**, which would be expected to give an isopropyl-substituted hydantoin and ultimately valine. However, the experimental substrate set documented in the 2022 paper concentrates on pyruvate, \u03b1-ketoglutarate, oxaloacetate and glyoxylate; **the paper does not provide a direct \u03b1-ketoisovalerate-to-valine experiment**. The pathway is therefore a well-supported reaction class that is mechanistically transferable to valine, but it should not be counted as a direct valine demonstration from that study.\n\nIts principal prebiotic criticisms are the comparatively high substrate/buffer concentrations, long reaction periods, requirement for cyanide plus a concentrated ammonia source, and\u2014again\u2014the prior availability of \u03b1-ketoisovalerate. Kaur et al. explicitly contrasted their H\u2082 chemistry with Bucherer\u2013Bergs chemistry, noting both its use of cyanide and its two-stage temperature protocol.\n\n## Key intermediates, precursors, catalysts, and kinetic constraints\n\nFor network-design purposes, the literature reduces to two particularly important **valine-specific carbon skeletons**.\n\nThe first is **isobutyraldehyde (2-methylpropanal)**. A Strecker route needs this C4 branched aldehyde plus HCN and ammonia. The aldehyde carbon becomes valine's \u03b1 carbon and the cyanide carbon becomes the carboxyl carbon. The immediate intermediate is **2-amino-3-methylbutanenitrile**, whose hydrolysis yields valine. HCN and the imine/aminonitrile intermediates therefore matter kinetically as much as the aldehyde itself. Miller's 1957 experiments showed that aminonitrile hydrolysis rates were sufficient to account for amino-acid accumulation on approximately week-long discharge timescales.\n\nThe second is **\u03b1-ketoisovalerate, 3-methyl-2-oxobutanoate**. It already contains all five carbons in the correct topology; only the \u03b1-keto group must be converted into an \u03b1-amino group. It is the direct precursor in the Kaur H\u2082/Ni/Pd route and the obvious substrate for a valine-specific Bucherer\u2013Bergs pathway. This makes \u03b1-ketoisovalerate particularly attractive for metabolism-congruent models, but also shifts nearly the entire carbon-skeleton problem upstream.\n\nThe major catalytic evidence can be summarized as follows.\n\n| Reaction | Catalyst/surface experimentally tested | Requirement |\n|---|---|---|\n| Spark generation of HCN/aldehydes and subsequent Strecker chemistry | No mineral catalyst required; Fe ammonium sulfate gave no important enhancement in Miller's experiment | Electrical energy; reducing feedstock mixture |\n| NH\u2084CN + ketone valine network | No mineral catalyst reported as required | Cyanide/ammonia chemistry plus ketone |\n| Heated HCHO/NH\u2083 route | No mineral catalyst required in original Fox\u2013Windsor experiment | Strong heating, followed by hydrolysis |\n| Reduced-gas shock route | No heterogeneous catalyst required | Shock heating and rapid quench |\n| \u03b1-Ketoisovalerate + H\u2082/NH\u2083 | Ni/SiO\u2082\u2013Al\u2082O\u2083, Pd/C, Aletai meteorite | **Catalyst essential** under tested conditions |\n| General \u03b1-ketoacid reductive amination | Fresh FeS or Fe(OH)\u2082 in Huber\u2013W\u00e4chtersh\u00e4user experiments | Strongly surface/state dependent |\n| Photochemical \u03b1-ketoacid amination | ZnS/sphalerite in Wang et al. | UV illumination and electron donor/hole-scavenging chemistry |\n| FTT-type CO/H\u2082/NH\u2083 chemistry | Fe/Ni meteorite powder, magnetite, Murchison material; bentonite much less active | 370 \u00b0C catalytic surface chemistry |\n\nThe first four rows follow Miller (1957), Friedmann & Miller (1969), Fox & Windsor (1970), and Bar-Nun et al. (1970); the H\u2082 metal-catalysis result is from Kaur et al. (2024).\n\nTwo older mineral studies are important mechanistically even though they did **not directly make valine**. **Claudia Huber & G\u00fcnter W\u00e4chtersh\u00e4user (2003, *Tetrahedron Letters*)** showed that freshly precipitated FeS and Fe(OH)\u2082 can promote reductive amination of several \u03b1-ketoacids with ammonium salts. Their experiments produced alanine, glutamate, phenylalanine and tyrosine, with maximum yields for several systems of tens of mol%; the reaction was highly sensitive to the physical state of FeS, pH and ionic strength. Optimal pH with ammonia was around **8.8\u20139.3**, close to the NH\u2084\u207a/NH\u2083 pK\u2090; elevated ionic strength suppressed the reaction. At 100 \u00b0C the tested tyrosine-forming system became fast on approximately hourly timescales, whereas reaction could still occur slowly at 4 \u00b0C. No \u03b1-ketoisovalerate/valine experiment was reported, so this work is evidence for a *reaction class*, not for valine itself.\n\nSimilarly, **Wei Wang, Qiliang Li, Bin Yang, Xiaoyang Liu, Yanqiang Yang & Wenhui Su (2012, *Chemical Communications*)** demonstrated reversible photochemical amination of four reverse-TCA-related \u03b1-ketoacids on irradiated ZnS. Their experiments used an oxygen-free ZnS suspension, pH around 9 and a Hg\u2013Xe source; glutamate yield was only about 0.5% after four hours without a sulfite hole scavenger and increased when the surface and photoredox conditions were optimized. \u03b1-Ketoisovalerate was not one of the substrates, so ZnS is a plausible catalyst family to test for valine chemistry rather than an existing direct demonstration.\n\nThe **Fischer\u2013Tropsch-type framework** deserves the same caution. **Sandra Pizzarello (2012, *Meteoritics & Planetary Science*)** reacted CO:H\u2082:NH\u2083 in a 1:1:1 gas mixture over meteorite/mineral powders at about **370 \u00b0C for 24 h**. Fe/Ni meteorite powder produced glycine, alanine, \u03b1-aminobutyric acid and smaller amounts of higher homologues; magnetite strongly favored the branched non-protein amino acid **\u03b1-aminoisobutyric acid**, reaching about 540 nmol g\u207b\u00b9 catalyst, and also yielded ~1 nmol g\u207b\u00b9 isovaline. **Valine itself was not reported in the product table.** Bentonite was a poor catalyst. FTT chemistry therefore provides experimentally important CO/H\u2082/NH\u2083 carbon\u2013nitrogen chemistry but should not currently be listed as a demonstrated valine route.\n\nThe valine literature contains surprisingly little **valine-specific equilibrium thermodynamics** under proposed prebiotic conditions. Most constraints are kinetic/empirical: week-scale aminonitrile hydrolysis in Miller chemistry; ultrafast high-temperature formation followed by quenching in shock chemistry; 72-h hydrogenation at 22 \u00b0C for Kaur's route; weeks-long formation/hydrolysis in the Bucherer\u2013Bergs system; and approximately linear accumulated-dose dependence in \u03b3-radiolysis. Thus, for synthesis-network modeling, experimentally determined rate constants, activation parameters and competing decomposition rates for **isobutyraldehyde, valine aminonitrile and \u03b1-ketoisovalerate under realistic saline/mineral conditions are major missing datasets**.\n\n## Environmental context and geochemical plausibility\n\n### Atmosphere and surface waters\n\nClassical Miller\u2013Strecker chemistry has the strongest historical evidence for direct synthesis from very small molecules, but its environmental support is weakest at the level of a **globally reducing atmosphere**. Trail, Watson & Tailby's Hadean zircon measurements imply mantle oxidation states similar to later terrestrial mantle by ~4.35 Ga, which favors outgassing of H\u2082O, CO\u2082, SO\u2082 and N\u2082 rather than large steady-state CH\u2084 and NH\u2083 abundances. The key environmental question is therefore not whether electrical discharge can make valine\u2014it can\u2014but whether early Earth could repeatedly create **local mixtures rich enough in reduced C and N to produce the necessary HCN and branched C4 aldehydes**.\n\nNeutral CO\u2082/N\u2082 spark experiments demonstrate that some amino-acid chemistry remains possible under more oxidized atmospheric assumptions, particularly when solution pH and oxidative work-up chemistry are controlled, but they have not supplied comparable direct evidence for valine. The later ascorbate-contamination result of Kuwahara et al. further weakens claims of very high amino-acid yields in some neutral-atmosphere experiments.\n\n### Hydrothermal and serpentinization systems\n\nThe **Kaur et al. H\u2082/Ni chemistry** is especially attractive environmentally because it works at 22 \u00b0C and pH ~8 rather than requiring hydrothermal temperatures. H\u2082 is continuously generated by water\u2013rock reactions such as serpentinization, and the authors note measured hydrothermal-fluid H\u2082 values of roughly 1\u201315 mmol kg\u207b\u00b9. Ni-bearing metal/mineral phases and meteoritic material offer plausible heterogeneous catalysts.\n\nThe difficulty is upstream carbon chemistry. Hydrothermal plausibility of H\u2082 and Ni does **not** establish a hydrothermal source of 30 mM \u03b1-ketoisovalerate. Kaur et al.'s coupled H\u2082 network makes several ketoacids from oxaloacetate/glyoxylate chemistry, but the valine-specific branched ketoacid was externally supplied. Thus the route currently has strong environmental support for the **reductive-amination step**, not for the complete valine synthesis.\n\nFeS/Fe(OH)\u2082 reductive-amination chemistry is often associated with hydrothermal/mineral-surface models, but Huber & W\u00e4chtersh\u00e4user's own results caution against treating \u201cFeS\u201d as a generic catalytic label: freshly precipitated material worked substantially differently from other preparations, excess sulfide could suppress activity, increased ionic strength reduced yields, and several alternative metal sulfides failed. Those dependencies need to be confronted explicitly when extrapolating to seawater or vent fluids.\n\n### Impact environments\n\nImpact chemistry is geochemically attractive because it supplies both extreme energy and reducing power from Fe/Ni projectiles without demanding a globally reducing atmosphere. Takeuchi et al.'s 2020 ^13C-labeled experiment demonstrates this principle directly with CO\u2082/HCO\u2083\u207b, N\u2082, water and meteoritic metals. But the realistic-feedstock experiment does **not yet reach valine**, whereas Bar-Nun et al.'s direct valine synthesis starts with methane, ethane and ammonia already in the gas phase.\n\nThus \u201cimpact synthesis of valine\u201d currently joins two pieces of evidence that have not been experimentally unified: old direct valine synthesis under a strongly reduced gas mixture, and modern geochemically motivated synthesis of simpler amino acids from CO\u2082/N\u2082/Fe/Ni. Demonstrating valine in a ^13C/^15N-labeled CO\u2082/N\u2082 impact experiment would materially strengthen this framework.\n\n### Meteorite parent bodies\n\nRadiolytic and hydrothermal formaldehyde/ammonia chemistry has perhaps the clearest environmental case for an **extraterrestrial** contribution. Carbonaceous parent bodies experienced aqueous alteration, and ^26Al provides both heat and ionizing radiation. Kebukawa's \u03b3-radiolysis experiments showed dose-dependent amino-acid synthesis, while Ishikawa et al. subsequently included valine among the minor products.\n\nThe caveat is that amino acids are generated as members of large, low-selectivity product suites, frequently after acid hydrolysis of complex precursor material. The concentration of formaldehyde, ammonia and related small organics in actual parent-body pore solutions, the extent of transport and destruction, and the quantitative contribution of \u03b3 radiation relative to ordinary thermal chemistry remain model-dependent. The 2017 Kebukawa hydrothermal experiment is particularly important as an analytical warning because its apparent valine was L-only and was judged likely contamination.\n\n## Competing frameworks, evidence against them, and unresolved bottlenecks\n\nThe strongest general criticism that applies across the field is **precursor displacement**: a route can solve the final C\u2013N bond formation while moving the harder carbon-skeleton problem upstream. The 2024 Ni/H\u2082 route is the clearest example. Once \u03b1-ketoisovalerate exists, valine formation at 22 \u00b0C is straightforward. But \u03b1-ketoisovalerate is already almost valine; the experimentally unresolved problem is the abiotic creation of its branched C5 skeleton in appreciable concentration from simpler C1\u2013C3 compounds.\n\nThe converse problem affects Strecker chemistry. Its advantage is that it **actually constructs the extra carbon** of the amino-acid carboxyl group from HCN, and electric discharges can generate HCN and carbonyl compounds from small gases. But valine specifically demands isobutyraldehyde. Producing a broad soup of C1\u2013C4 aldehydes is chemically easy compared with generating the branched aldehyde at a concentration high enough for valine to compete with glycine, alanine, AIB, norvaline and numerous non-protein isomers. Ring et al.'s broad product distribution illustrates that selectivity problem experimentally.\n\nThe **NH\u2084CN/acetone route** has a particularly unresolved carbon-accounting problem. Because ordinary Strecker chemistry of acetone should lead to AIB rather than valine, direct valine formation implies secondary C\u2013C transformations in the cyanide polymerization network. Friedmann & Miller established the product, but the detailed sequence and its yield dependence on cyanide, ketone concentration, pH and temperature have never been developed into a modern isotope-resolved mechanism. Their failure to reproduce valine from NH\u2084CN alone also invalidates the simplest version of older cyanide-polymer claims.\n\nThe **formaldehyde/ammonia route** has an analogous mechanistic gap. It starts from admirably simple C1/N1 feedstocks but must execute several selective C\u2013C couplings to reach a branched C5 skeleton. Fox & Windsor demonstrated valine among hydrolysis products, yet modern HCHO/NH\u2083 experiments yield complex polymeric material and broad amino-acid suites, and Kebukawa et al.'s hydrothermal analogue did not produce analytically secure abiotic valine. The exact branch-forming sequence remains unknown.\n\nFor **shock chemistry**, the principal controversy is geochemical rather than chemical. Valine is demonstrably made by shock-heating a CH\u2084/C\u2082H\u2086/NH\u2083 mixture, but Hadean mantle redox constraints do not support that mixture as a general background atmosphere. More realistic CO\u2082/N\u2082 impact chemistry works, but so far only for simpler amino acids.\n\nThe **Bucherer\u2013Bergs \u03b1-ketoacid route** is chemically convincing but carries three distinct relevance questions: whether \u03b1-ketoisovalerate was available; whether cyanide and ammonia could coexist at useful concentrations; and whether the long room-temperature plus 80 \u00b0C processing sequence could occur without destroying other network components. Pulletikurti et al.'s optimized experiments used 0.5 M bicarbonate and comparatively concentrated substrates, making environmental concentration mechanisms an essential part of any proposed setting.\n\nThe **FTT framework** currently should not be promoted as a valine synthesis. Pizzarello's 2012 experiment is valuable because it uses exceptionally simple CO/H\u2082/NH\u2083 feedstocks and geologically relevant catalytic materials, but its 370 \u00b0C product suite contains AIB, norvaline and isovaline rather than valine. The strong production of AIB by magnetite shows that mineral surfaces can select branching patterns, but the selection is not the biological valine skeleton in that study.\n\nThere is also a broader **congruence-with-biochemistry debate**. Strecker chemistry is efficient and geochemically versatile but has no direct analogue in extant amino-acid biosynthesis. \u03b1-Ketoacid reductive amination is structurally congruent with extant metabolism, which is one reason Kaur et al. emphasize it, but metabolic resemblance by itself does not establish historical continuity. A reaction network is compelling only if the required \u03b1-ketoacid itself can arise from geochemically available starting materials under compatible conditions.\n\nFinally, there is the **chirality problem**. None of the strongest direct routes discussed here establishes a robust mechanism for generating protein-level L-valine enantiomeric excess from achiral feedstocks. Indeed, Kebukawa et al.'s observation of exclusively L-valine in an otherwise abiotic experiment was interpreted as a warning sign for contamination rather than as spontaneous enantioselection. For synthesis-network design, valine production and valine homochirality should therefore be treated as separate problems unless an explicit symmetry-breaking mechanism is demonstrated.\n\n## Implications for synthesis-network design\n\nThe most productive network representation is to treat valine as having **two convergent immediate-precursor nodes**:\n\n**Aldehyde branch**\n\\[\n\\mathrm{CH_4/CO/CO_2 \\rightarrow C_1-C_3\\ carbonyls \\rightarrow\nisobutyraldehyde}\n\\]\n\\[\n\\mathrm{isobutyraldehyde + NH_3 + HCN \\rightarrow\nvaline\\ aminonitrile \\rightarrow valine}\n\\]\n\nand\n\n**\u03b1-ketoacid branch**\n\\[\n\\mathrm{small\\ carbonyls/pyruvate \\rightarrow branched\\ C_5\\ chemistry\n\\rightarrow \\alpha\\!-\\!ketoisovalerate}\n\\]\n\\[\n\\mathrm{\\alpha\\!-\\!ketoisovalerate + NH_3 + H_2\n\\xrightarrow[22\\,^\\circ C,\\ pH\\sim8]{Ni/Pd} valine}\n\\]\n\nThe second arrow in each branch is reasonably well supported; the first arrow is the strategic bottleneck. The Strecker branch has abundant experimental evidence for the general **HCN + aldehyde \u2192 aminonitrile \u2192 amino acid** machinery, but insufficient quantitative work on prebiotic formation of isobutyraldehyde. The \u03b1-ketoacid branch has excellent modern evidence for **\u03b1-ketoisovalerate \u2192 valine**, but currently lacks a comparably convincing abiotic synthesis of \u03b1-ketoisovalerate from pyruvate or simple C1\u2013C3 feedstocks.\n\nFor experimental prioritization, the highest-value missing experiment would therefore not simply be \u201cmake valine again.\u201d It would be an isotope-resolved synthesis of the **branched skeleton**. Particularly discriminating experiments would test whether plausible atmospheric, photochemical, mineral, impact or H\u2082-driven networks can generate ^13C-labeled isobutyraldehyde or \u03b1-ketoisovalerate from CO\u2082/CO/HCHO/pyruvate, followed *in the same reaction system* by capture as valine. The need for this follows directly from the precursor gaps in both the Miller/Ring and Kaur pathways.\n\nA second priority is to quantify competition. In Kaur's \u03b1-ketoisovalerate experiment, geochemically attractive Ni and meteoritic metal make substantial **2-hydroxyisovalerate**, whereas Pd strongly favors valine. A realistic network therefore needs to model carbon partitioning between amino-, hydroxy-, decomposition- and adsorption products, not just report the presence of valine. Likewise, electric-discharge chemistry produces many constitutional isomers and non-protein amino acids.\n\nA third priority is to introduce realistic **salinity, mineral aging and concentration cycling**. Huber & W\u00e4chtersh\u00e4user showed that ionic strength, FeS preparation and sulfide loading can change reductive-amination behavior dramatically, while Pulletikurti et al.'s Bucherer\u2013Bergs chemistry uses much more concentrated bicarbonate, cyanide and organic substrate than would be expected in an unconfined ocean. Evaporation, freezing, adsorption, pore confinement or repeated wet\u2013dry cycling therefore cannot merely be environmental decoration; where high concentrations are required, a quantitative concentration mechanism is part of the chemical hypothesis.\n\nOverall, the **best-supported direct valine-forming chemical step under mild, geochemically interpretable conditions is presently \u03b1-ketoisovalerate reductive amination with H\u2082 over Ni-bearing or meteoritic material**, especially because Kaur et al. directly quantified valine/hydroxy-acid competition at 22 \u00b0C. The **best-supported route that constructs valine from substantially smaller organic molecules remains Strecker/discharge chemistry**, but its strongest demonstrations use reducing atmospheres whose global Hadean relevance is contested. **Parent-body \u03b3 chemistry and reduced-gas shock chemistry independently show that valine can also emerge from much less pathway-specific, energy-rich reaction networks**, though with low selectivity and substantial environmental qualifications. None of these results yet establishes a uniquely favored historical route.\n\n## Primary literature cited\n\n**Miller, Stanley L. (1957), *Biochimica et Biophysica Acta* 23, 480\u2013489, \u201cThe mechanism of synthesis of amino acids by electric discharges.\u201d** Established HCN/aldehyde/aminonitrile Strecker chemistry as the mechanism underlying much of electric-discharge amino-acid synthesis and found no substantial catalytic effect from ferrous ammonium sulfate.\n\n**Friedmann, Nadav & Stanley L. Miller (1969), *Nature* 221, 1152\u20131153, \u201cSynthesis of Valine and Isoleucine in Primitive Earth Conditions.\u201d** Failed to confirm valine from NH\u2084CN polymerization alone but directly obtained valine when acetone was added.\n\n**Bar-Nun, A.; Bar-Nun, N.; Bauer, S. H.; & Sagan, C. (1970), *Science* 168, 470\u2013472, \u201cShock Synthesis of Amino Acids in Simulated Primitive Environments.\u201d** Directly produced glycine, alanine, valine and leucine in a reduced gas mixture exposed to shock heating and rapid quenching.\n\n**Fox, Sidney W. & Charles R. Windsor (1970), *Science* 170, 984\u2013986, \u201cSynthesis of Amino Acids by the Heating of Formaldehyde and Ammonia.\u201d** Reported valine among amino acids obtained after hydrolysis of heated formaldehyde/ammonia reaction products.\n\n**Ring, D.; Wolman, Y.; Friedmann, N.; & Miller, S. L. (1972), *Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences USA* 69, 765\u2013768, \u201cPrebiotic Synthesis of Hydrophobic and Protein Amino Acids.\u201d** Confirmed valine and a broad suite of hydrophobic amino acids by chromatography and GC\u2013MS after electric discharge through methane/nitrogen/water with traces of ammonia.\n\n**Huber, Claudia & G\u00fcnter W\u00e4chtersh\u00e4user (2003), *Tetrahedron Letters* 44, 1695\u20131697, \u201cPrimordial Reductive Amination Revisited.\u201d** Demonstrated FeS/Fe(OH)\u2082-mediated reductive amination of several \u03b1-ketoacids; important mechanistic precedent, but not a direct valine experiment.\n\n**Cleaves, H. James; Chalmers, John H.; Lazcano, Antonio; Miller, Stanley L.; & Bada, Jeffrey L. (2008), *Origins of Life and Evolution of Biospheres* 38, 105\u2013115, \u201cA Reassessment of Prebiotic Organic Synthesis in Neutral Planetary Atmospheres.\u201d** Re-evaluated CO\u2082/N\u2082 discharge chemistry; relevant to atmospheric plausibility but not a direct valine demonstration.\n\n**Trail, Dustin; Watson, E. Bruce; & Tailby, Nicholas D. (2011), *Nature* 480, 79\u201382, \u201cThe Oxidation State of Hadean Magmas and Implications for Early Earth's Atmosphere.\u201d** Provided zircon-based evidence against persistently strongly reducing volcanic outgassing in much of the Hadean.\n\n**Wang, Wei; Li, Qiliang; Yang, Bin; Liu, Xiaoyang; Yang, Yanqiang; & Su, Wenhui (2012), *Chemical Communications* 48, 2146\u20132148, \u201cPhotocatalytic Reversible Amination of \u03b1-Keto Acids on a ZnS Surface: Implications for the Prebiotic Metabolism.\u201d** Demonstrated mineral-photocatalytic \u03b1-ketoacid amination, although not \u03b1-ketoisovalerate/valine.\n\n**Pizzarello, Sandra (2012), *Meteoritics & Planetary Science* 47, 1291\u20131296, \u201cCatalytic Syntheses of Amino Acids and Their Significance for Nebular and Planetary Chemistry.\u201d** Demonstrated CO/H\u2082/NH\u2083 FTT-type amino-acid synthesis on meteoritic and mineral catalysts; notably produced AIB and isovaline but did not report valine.\n\n**Kuwahara, Hideharu; Eto, Midori; Kawamoto, Yukinori; Kurihara, Hironari; Kaneko, Takeo; Obayashi, Yumiko; & Kobayashi, Kensei (2012), *Origins of Life and Evolution of Biospheres* 42, 533\u2013541, \u201cThe Use of Ascorbate as an Oxidation Inhibitor in Prebiotic Amino Acid Synthesis: A Cautionary Note.\u201d** Demonstrated that ascorbate can produce misleading apparent amino-acid signals in neutral-atmosphere synthesis experiments.\n\n**Kebukawa, Yoko et al. (2017), *Science Advances* 3, e1602093, \u201cOne-pot Synthesis of Amino Acid Precursors with Insoluble Organic Matter in Planetesimals with Aqueous Activity.\u201d** Produced extensive amino-acid precursor chemistry from formaldehyde/glycolaldehyde/ammonia but treated observed L-only valine as likely contamination.\n\n**Takeuchi, Y.; Furukawa, Y.; Kobayashi, T.; Sekine, T.; Terada, N.; & Kakegawa, T. (2020), *Scientific Reports* 10, 9220, \u201cImpact-induced amino acid formation on Hadean Earth and Noachian Mars.\u201d** Demonstrated isotopically verified amino-acid formation from CO\u2082/N\u2082/H\u2082O plus Fe/Ni-bearing impact materials, but not valine.\n\n**Kebukawa, Yoko (2022), *ACS Central Science* 8, 1664\u20131671, \u201cGamma-Ray-Induced Amino Acid Formation in Aqueous Small Bodies in the Early Solar System.\u201d** Established dose-dependent amino-acid production by \u03b3 irradiation of aqueous formaldehyde/ammonia mixtures.\n\n**Pulletikurti, Sunil; Yadav, Mahipal; Springsteen, Greg; & Krishnamurthy, Ramanarayanan (2022), *Nature Chemistry* 14, 1142\u20131150, \u201cPrebiotic Synthesis of \u03b1-Amino Acids and Orotate from \u03b1-Ketoacids Potentiates Transition to Extant Metabolic Pathways.\u201d** Established Bucherer\u2013Bergs conversion of several \u03b1-ketoacids to amino acids via cyanide/ammonia chemistry; directly relevant mechanistically to \u03b1-ketoisovalerate but did not itself provide a valine-specific substrate experiment.\n\n**Kaur, Harpreet; Rauscher, Sophia A.; Werner, Emilie; Song, Youngdong; Yi, Jing; Kaz\u00f6ne, Wahnyalo; Martin, William F.; T\u00fcys\u00fcz, Harun; & Moran, Joseph (2024), *Chem* 10, 1528\u20131540, \u201cA Prebiotic Krebs Cycle Analog Generates Amino Acids with H\u2082 and NH\u2083 over Nickel.\u201d** Provides the strongest modern quantitative direct experiment for \u03b1-ketoisovalerate \u2192 valine using H\u2082 and Ni, Pd or meteoritic catalysts under mild aqueous conditions.\n\n**Ishikawa, Akari; Kebukawa, Yoko; Kobayashi, Kensei; & Yoda, Isao (2024), *Life* 14, 103, \u201cGamma-Ray-Induced Amino Acid Formation during Aqueous Alteration in Small Bodies: The Effects of Compositions of Starting Solutions.\u201d** Extended parent-body radiolysis experiments and reported valine among minor amino-acid products.\n=== END DEEP RESEARCH FINDINGS ===\n\n\n\n=== END LITERATURE RESEARCH FINDINGS ===\n\nINSTRUCTIONS FOR LITERATURE-GUIDED SYNTHESIS:\n1. Prefer routes and conditions that are well supported experimentally in the research above.\n2. Weigh the critiques and contested points the research reports; do not adopt a route uncritically.\n3. You may depart from the described routes when the chemistry justifies it; explain why in \"reasoning\".\n4. Cite the supporting paper (author, year) in each reaction's \"reasoning\" field, or mark the reaction as speculative.\n", "raw_response": "{\n  \"target_molecule\": \"Valine\",\n  \"overall_strategy\": \"This is an evidence-graded, connected synthesis network with exactly 10 pathway entries, not 10 independently validated de novo mechanisms. It combines discharge/Strecker chemistry, experimentally reported but mechanistically unresolved acetone/cyanide and heated-formaldehyde chemistry, reduced-gas shock chemistry, three experimentally tested metal-catalyst variants of alpha-ketoisovalerate reductive amination, and three explicitly speculative substrate-scope extensions. P5-P7 are catalyst variants, not different carbon-skeleton construction mechanisms. Alpha-ketoisovalerate is an explicitly supplied starting precursor: the cited research does not establish its prebiotic synthesis from simpler feedstocks. Discharge production of the required branched aldehyde is also insufficiently characterized. Consequently, experimentally demonstrated closure of the entire network from simple geochemical feedstocks cannot be claimed. Empirical process nodes collapse unresolved chemistry rather than inventing molecular identities for heterogeneous precursor material; the carbon dioxide closure proposed for rxn_006 and rxn_007 is explicitly hypothetical. Environments represent separate reaction settings, not a single compatible vessel. Shock and discharge involve transient energetic events outside ordinary bulk-water temperature ranges. All chemistry is anoxic; sulfite and ferrous hydroxide supply reducing equivalents without molecular oxygen. Organic formulas use neutral reference structures, with acid-base speciation described in conditions. Valine is stereochemically unspecified and expected to be racemic absent demonstrated symmetry breaking. A null environment_formed denotes an imported feedstock or formation in multiple environments. Byproducts use the allowed intermediate role.\",\n  \"molecules\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_001\",\n      \"formula\": \"CH4\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"methane\",\n      \"common_name\": \"methane\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/CH4/h1H4\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_002\",\n      \"formula\": \"NH3\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"azane\",\n      \"common_name\": \"ammonia\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/H3N/h1H3\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_003\",\n      \"formula\": \"H2O\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"oxidane\",\n      \"common_name\": \"water\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/H2O/h1H2\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_004\",\n      \"formula\": \"H2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"dihydrogen\",\n      \"common_name\": \"hydrogen\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/H2/h1H\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_005\",\n      \"formula\": \"CHN\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"hydrogen cyanide\",\n      \"common_name\": \"hydrogen cyanide\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/CHN/c1-2/h1H\",\n      \"role\": \"hub_intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_006\",\n      \"formula\": \"C4H8O\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-methylpropanal\",\n      \"common_name\": \"isobutyraldehyde\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/C4H8O/c1-4(2)3-5/h3-4H,1-2H3\",\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_007\",\n      \"formula\": \"C4H9N\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-methylpropan-1-imine\",\n      \"common_name\": \"isobutyraldimine\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_008\",\n      \"formula\": \"C5H10N2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-amino-3-methylbutanenitrile\",\n      \"common_name\": \"valine aminonitrile\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_009\",\n      \"formula\": \"C5H12N2O\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-amino-3-methylbutanamide\",\n      \"common_name\": \"valinamide\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_010\",\n      \"formula\": \"C5H11NO2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-amino-3-methylbutanoic acid\",\n      \"common_name\": \"valine\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/C5H11NO2/c1-3(2)4(6)5(7)8/h3-4H,6H2,1-2H3,(H,7,8)\",\n      \"role\": \"target\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_011\",\n      \"formula\": \"C3H6O\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"propan-2-one\",\n      \"common_name\": \"acetone\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/C3H6O/c1-3(2)4/h1-2H3\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_012\",\n      \"formula\": \"CH2O\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"methanal\",\n      \"common_name\": \"formaldehyde\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/CH2O/c1-2/h1H2\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_013\",\n      \"formula\": \"C2H6\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"ethane\",\n      \"common_name\": \"ethane\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/C2H6/c1-2/h1-2H3\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_014\",\n      \"formula\": \"C5H8O3\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"3-methyl-2-oxobutanoic acid\",\n      \"common_name\": \"alpha-ketoisovaleric acid\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/C5H8O3/c1-3(2)4(6)5(7)8/h3H,1-2H3,(H,7,8)\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_015\",\n      \"formula\": \"C5H10O3\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-hydroxy-3-methylbutanoic acid\",\n      \"common_name\": \"alpha-hydroxyisovaleric acid\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Biochemical\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_016\",\n      \"formula\": \"C6H10N2O2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"5-(propan-2-yl)imidazolidine-2,4-dione\",\n      \"common_name\": \"5-isopropylhydantoin\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_017\",\n      \"formula\": \"C6H12N2O3\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-(carbamoylamino)-3-methylbutanoic acid\",\n      \"common_name\": \"N-carbamoylvaline\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_018\",\n      \"formula\": \"CO2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"carbon dioxide\",\n      \"common_name\": \"carbon dioxide\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/CO2/c1-2-3\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_019\",\n      \"formula\": \"FeH2O2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"iron(II) hydroxide\",\n      \"common_name\": \"ferrous hydroxide\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_020\",\n      \"formula\": \"FeHO2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"iron(III) oxide hydroxide\",\n      \"common_name\": \"ferric oxyhydroxide\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Hydrothermal\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_021\",\n      \"formula\": \"SO3^2-\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"sulfite\",\n      \"common_name\": \"sulfite ion\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_022\",\n      \"formula\": \"SO4^2-\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"sulfate\",\n      \"common_name\": \"sulfate ion\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"reactions\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_001\",\n      \"name\": \"Reduced-gas discharge supplying cyanide and a candidate branched aldehyde\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_001\", \"mol_002\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_005\", \"mol_006\", \"mol_004\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-330 K collection water; much hotter transient electrical-discharge channels\",\n        \"pressure\": \"approximately 1 atm in a proposed local gas-water setting\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Anoxic methane/ammonia-rich gas over water; repeated electrical discharge and aqueous collection. The listed products are a selected-product projection of a much broader mixture.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Discharge-driven fragmentation and recombination generate hydrogen cyanide and carbonyl compounds. Formation of 2-methylpropanal is a proposed branched-carbon component of this unresolved upstream network, not an established elementary sequence.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Miller (1957) established discharge production of HCN and aldehydes and their connection to Strecker synthesis; Ring et al. (1972) directly identified valine in a related discharge product mixture. The supplied findings do not establish a measured, isotope-resolved discharge-to-isobutyraldehyde sequence, so that species-specific edge is speculative. Producing sufficient branched C4 aldehyde is the key skeleton bottleneck. A locally reducing mixture is required; a globally persistent methane/ammonia atmosphere is not assumed, given Trail et al. (2011). No mineral catalyst is justified.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_002\",\n      \"name\": \"Isobutyraldehyde-ammonia condensation\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_006\", \"mol_002\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_007\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-330 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"approximately 1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Concentrated, mildly alkaline water, approximately pH 8-9; imine is a transient equilibrium intermediate. Rapid cyanide capture must compete with hydrolysis and aldehyde loss.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Ammonia adds to the aldehyde, followed by carbinolamine dehydration to an imine. Subsequent cyanide addition can pull the reversible condensation forward.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"This is the conventional Strecker condensation underlying the mechanistic framework of Miller (1957). Its application to isobutyraldehyde is chemically well motivated, but the cited findings do not separately quantify this imine or its equilibrium abundance. Concentration and capture, rather than an unjustified mineral catalyst, are the relevant controls.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_003\",\n      \"name\": \"Cyanide addition forming valine aminonitrile\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_007\", \"mol_005\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_008\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-330 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"approximately 1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Approximately pH 8-9, with HCN/CN- and imine/iminium speciation. Water depth or pore shading reduces photodestruction without requiring an ozone layer.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Cyanide attacks the imine/iminium carbon. Proton transfer gives 2-amino-3-methylbutanenitrile; cyanide supplies the future carboxyl carbon.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Miller (1957) supports aminonitrile formation as the central Strecker intermediate, while Ring et al. (1972) supports accessibility of valine in discharge chemistry. This carbon addition gives the correct C5 connectivity, unlike ordinary acetone Strecker chemistry, which gives alpha-aminoisobutyric acid. Valine-specific rates and competing cyanide reactions remain insufficiently characterized.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_004\",\n      \"name\": \"Valine aminonitrile hydration to valinamide\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_008\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_009\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"hydronium ions\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"340-370 K; proposed natural hydrolysis window\",\n        \"pressure\": \"approximately 1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"acid catalysis by hydronium\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Proposed acidification to approximately pH 1-3 after cyanide capture, for example in an acidic geothermal pool. Required residence time is not established for this substrate.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Nitrile protonation, water addition and proton transfers produce the corresponding primary amide.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Miller (1957) supports aminonitrile hydrolysis as the source of accumulated amino acids. The explicit valinamide node and these mildly heated environmental conditions are a mechanistic reconstruction, not a separately reported valine-specific experiment. Strong analytical acid hydrolysis should not be equated with rapid natural hydrolysis; this step requires kinetic validation.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_005\",\n      \"name\": \"Valinamide hydrolysis to valine\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_009\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_010\", \"mol_002\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"hydronium ions\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"350-370 K; proposed natural hydrolysis window\",\n        \"pressure\": \"approximately 1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"acid catalysis by hydronium\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Approximately pH 1-3 following rxn_004; released ammonia is predominantly ammonium. Residence time and product survival require measurement.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Water addition to the protonated amide carbonyl and C-N bond cleavage release valine and ammonia/ammonium.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"This completes the conventional nitrile-to-amino-acid chemistry supported at the reaction-family level by Miller (1957). Separate valinamide kinetics under the proposed surface conditions are not supplied, so this step is an explicit mechanistic extrapolation. Hydrolysis may be a bottleneck, and the reported analytical recovery of amino acids does not establish efficient free-valine accumulation in every natural pool.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_006\",\n      \"name\": \"Acetone-ammonium-cyanide valine production: empirical process with hypothetical coproduct closure\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_011\", \"mol_005\", \"mol_002\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_010\", \"mol_002\", \"mol_018\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-350 K proposed accumulation stage; subsequent hydrolysis conditions are not established by the supplied summary\",\n        \"pressure\": \"approximately 1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Concentrated cyanide/ammonia pool containing acetone, followed by hydrolytic processing. This is not a validated single-temperature natural protocol.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"An unresolved cyanide/ketone network constructs additional C-C bonds and produces valine or hydrolysable valine precursors. The graph proposes internal redox disproportionation with CO2 and regenerated ammonia as an atom-balance-compatible closure; those coproduct assignments are not experimentally established.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Friedmann and Miller (1969) directly obtained valine when acetone was added to NH4CN, but failed to confirm valine from NH4CN alone. Ordinary acetone Strecker chemistry cannot explain this result because it yields a C4 amino acid. Valine production is supported; the detailed branching mechanism, natural hydrolysis conditions and the CO2-containing closure represented here are speculative. Stoichiometric inference from this node must not be treated as an experimentally measured yield or carbon map.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_007\",\n      \"name\": \"Heated formaldehyde-ammonia chemistry: historical empirical valine branch\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_012\", \"mol_002\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_010\", \"mol_018\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Hydrothermal\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"approximately 458 K heating stage, followed by cooling and hydrolytic processing\",\n        \"pressure\": \"greater than approximately 12 bar if liquid water is retained; proposed confined-pore analogue\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Concentrated formaldehyde/ammonia chemistry. Hydrothermal placement is an environmental extrapolation, not a claim that the original experiment reproduced a vent. Hydrolysis work-up requirements remain a separate uncertainty.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Thermal carbonyl condensation, nitrogen incorporation and internal redox chemistry form complex hydrolysable products. The valine-specific branched sequence is unresolved. CO2 is proposed as an oxidized coproduct permitting formal carbon/redox closure, not as an experimentally traced valine-associated product.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Fox and Windsor (1970) historically reported valine after heating formaldehyde/ammonia products and hydrolysing them. However, Kebukawa et al. (2017) considered L-only valine in a related thermal system likely contamination. This is therefore a low-confidence empirical branch, not a reproducibly quantified modern synthesis. The CO2 coproduct assignment and transfer to a confined terrestrial hydrothermal setting are speculative and require isotopic validation.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_008\",\n      \"name\": \"Reduced-gas shock, rapid quench and aqueous recovery of valine\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_001\", \"mol_013\", \"mol_002\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_010\", \"mol_004\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"Transient high-temperature shock, followed by rapid cooling into approximately 300-330 K collection water\",\n        \"pressure\": \"transient shock compression followed by decompression; no specific pressure inferred from the supplied summary\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": false,\n        \"other\": \"Gas-phase initiation using approximately 3.3% methane, 11% ethane and 5.6% ammonia in argon. Water enters subsequent collection/hydrolysis, not the initial reduced-gas shock. This is a surface event, not an isothermal 300-370 K pool reaction.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Shock-induced fragmentation and radical recombination create reduced nitrogenous organic products. Rapid quenching preserves reactive intermediates; aqueous processing supplies oxygen and can release amino acids. The listed outputs are a selected-product projection, not a resolved elementary mechanism.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Bar-Nun et al. (1970) directly reported valine from this reduced-gas shock family. The valine-specific intermediate sequence and product-associated hydrogen stoichiometry were not resolved. Its environmental limitation is the highly reduced starting gas, given Trail et al. (2011). Takeuchi et al. (2020) demonstrated amino-acid formation in more conservative CO2/N2/Fe impact mixtures but did not demonstrate valine, so those experiments cannot close this feedstock gap.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_009\",\n      \"name\": \"Nickel-catalysed reductive amination of alpha-ketoisovalerate\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_014\", \"mol_002\", \"mol_004\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_010\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Biochemical\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Ni supported on SiO2-Al2O3\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"295 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"5 bar H2\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Ni/SiO2-Al2O3; 10 mol% Ni relative to ketoacid\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"30 mM alpha-ketoisovalerate, five equivalents NH4HCO3, pH approximately 8, 72 hours. This is a cool pressurized aqueous interface, not a hot vent interior.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Ammonia forms an imine/iminium-type intermediate at the alpha-keto group. Nickel activates hydrogen and hydrogenates that intermediate to valine.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Kaur et al. (2024) directly tested alpha-ketoisovalerate. Their approximately 20% combined amino/hydroxy-acid yield and 34:66 product ratio imply about 6.8% valine, an arithmetic inference rather than a separately reported yield. Catalyst-free controls lacked reduction products. Hydrogen and nickel have plausible geological sources, but the supported catalyst is a laboratory material and the 30 mM branched ketoacid was supplied, not synthesized in the network.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_010\",\n      \"name\": \"Palladium-catalysed reductive amination benchmark\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_014\", \"mol_002\", \"mol_004\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_010\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Biochemical\",\n      \"agents\": [\"palladium on carbon\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"295 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"5 bar H2\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Pd/C; 1 mol% Pd relative to ketoacid\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"30 mM alpha-ketoisovalerate, five equivalents NH4HCO3, pH approximately 8, 72 hours.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Palladium-mediated hydrogen activation reduces the ammonia-derived imine/iminium intermediate to valine.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Kaur et al. (2024) directly reported approximately 77% combined amino/hydroxy-acid yield with an 83:17 ratio, implying about 64% valine. This is the most selective of the three tested catalyst variants but is retained as an experimental benchmark, not asserted to be an abundant prebiotic catalyst. Natural availability of comparably active palladium surfaces and the origin of alpha-ketoisovalerate remain unresolved.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_011\",\n      \"name\": \"Meteoritic Fe-Ni-catalysed reductive amination\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_014\", \"mol_002\", \"mol_004\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_010\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Biochemical\",\n      \"agents\": [\"ground Aletai iron meteorite containing nickel-bearing metallic phases\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"295 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"5 bar H2\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"3 mg ground Aletai meteorite in the reported experiment\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"30 mM alpha-ketoisovalerate, five equivalents NH4HCO3, pH approximately 8, 72 hours. Activity of weathered natural surfaces is not established.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Hydrogen activation and imine hydrogenation occur on reactive meteoritic metal surfaces; competing ketone hydrogenation diverts substrate to the hydroxy acid.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Kaur et al. (2024) directly reported approximately 74% combined amino/hydroxy-acid yield and an 18:82 ratio, implying about 13% valine. Meteorite-derived metal is a more directly natural material than Pd/C, but hydroxy-acid formation dominates. The experiment does not demonstrate formation of the branched ketoacid or preservation of activity after terrestrial surface alteration.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_012\",\n      \"name\": \"Competing hydrogenation of alpha-ketoisovalerate to its hydroxy acid\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_014\", \"mol_004\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_015\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Biochemical\",\n      \"agents\": [\n        \"Ni/SiO2-Al2O3 as one tested alternative\",\n        \"Pd/C as one tested alternative\",\n        \"ground Aletai meteorite as one tested alternative\"\n      ],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"295 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"5 bar H2\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"One of the three listed catalysts, used separately rather than mixed\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Same aqueous ketoacid/ammonium conditions as rxn_009-rxn_011; catalyst-dependent competition with reductive amination.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Surface-mediated hydrogenation of the keto carbonyl produces 2-hydroxy-3-methylbutanoic acid instead of forming a C-N bond.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Kaur et al. (2024) directly measured this competing product. Its shares of the combined amino/hydroxy products were approximately 66% for supported Ni, 17% for Pd/C and 82% for Aletai meteorite. This terminal side branch is included because a valine network that ignores the dominant carbon sink on geochemically attractive catalysts would overstate productivity; no unsupported conversion of this hydroxy acid back to valine is added.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_013\",\n      \"name\": \"Hypothetical alpha-ketoisovalerate Bucherer-Bergs conversion to hydantoin\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_014\", \"mol_005\", \"mol_002\", \"mol_018\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_016\", \"mol_018\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"approximately 300 K proposed surface analogue of the reported room-temperature stage\",\n        \"pressure\": \"approximately 1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Reaction-class benchmark: approximately 0.35 M ketoacid, 1.2 equivalents cyanide, two equivalents ammonia source and 0.5 M bicarbonate at pH approximately 8.5 for 21 days. These are not established optimal valine conditions. CO2 appears on both sides to represent inorganic-carbon exchange in the lumped process.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Proposed cyanide/ammonia addition, inorganic-carbon capture, cyclization and decarboxylative processing yield 5-isopropylhydantoin. The intervening carboxylated species and carbon-atom mapping are not assigned as experimentally established valine intermediates.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"SPECULATIVE substrate extension. Pulletikurti et al. (2022) established alpha-ketoacid-to-amino-acid chemistry involving hydantoins, but did not directly demonstrate alpha-ketoisovalerate-to-valine in the documented substrate set. This proposed step is justified by the shared alpha-ketoacid functionality, not by a valine experiment. Precursor supply, concentrated bicarbonate/cyanide, branching-dependent kinetics and the exact decarboxylation sequence remain unresolved.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_014\",\n      \"name\": \"Hypothetical hydrolytic opening of valine hydantoin\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_016\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_017\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"353 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"approximately 1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Warm bicarbonate/ammonia solution following rxn_013. Fourteen days is the reported reaction-class heated-stage duration, not a measured lifetime for this specific hydantoin.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Hydrolytic cleavage of the hydantoin ring produces N-carbamoylvaline.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"SPECULATIVE valine-specific extension. Pulletikurti et al. (2022) provided mechanistic support for hydantoin hydrolysis through N-carbamoyl amino acids. The proposed isopropyl-substituted substrate was not directly validated in the supplied findings. Slow ring opening, competing degradation and dependence on ammonia-source chemistry must be tested rather than inferred from the generic temperature protocol.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_015\",\n      \"name\": \"Hypothetical N-carbamoylvaline hydrolysis\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_017\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_010\", \"mol_002\", \"mol_018\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"353 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"approximately 1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Continuation of the heated hydrolysis stage; ammonia/ammonium and CO2/bicarbonate speciation depend on pH. No separate valine-specific completion time is asserted.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Hydrolytic removal of the carbamoyl group releases valine, ammonia and inorganic carbon.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"SPECULATIVE valine-specific extension of the N-carbamoyl-amino-acid chemistry described by Pulletikurti et al. (2022). This is a chemically justified deprotection step, but its rate and yield for N-carbamoylvaline under the proposed conditions are unestablished. Merely reaching a hydantoin or carbamoylated precursor must not be counted as demonstrated accumulation of free valine.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_016\",\n      \"name\": \"Hypothetical ferrous-hydroxide-driven reductive amination\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_014\", \"mol_002\", \"mol_019\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_010\", \"mol_020\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Hydrothermal\",\n      \"agents\": [\"freshly precipitated iron(II) hydroxide, consumed as a reductant rather than acting as a conserved catalyst\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"350-373 K proposed cool hydrothermal interface\",\n        \"pressure\": \"sufficient hydrostatic pressure to retain liquid water\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Approximately pH 8.8-9.3, low ionic strength and fresh reactive Fe(II) hydroxide. Ferric oxyhydroxide is a proposed oxidized end phase, not an established phase assignment for this valine reaction.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Ammonia-derived imine formation is followed by electron transfer coupled to oxidation of Fe(II). The displayed formal net reaction uses FeOOH to close the two-electron reduction without molecular oxygen.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"SPECULATIVE substrate and solid-product extension. Huber and Wachtershauser (2003) demonstrated reductive amination of other alpha-ketoacids with freshly prepared FeS or Fe(OH)2, not alpha-ketoisovalerate. Fe(OH)2 is represented as a consumed electron donor to avoid inventing a reductant-free catalytic cycle. The actual oxidized solid could be mixed-valence or poorly crystalline rather than the displayed FeOOH reference phase. Salinity, aging and carbonate precipitation may suppress the proposed chemistry.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_017\",\n      \"name\": \"Hypothetical ZnS-photocatalysed valine formation with sulfite oxidation\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_014\", \"mol_002\", \"mol_021\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_010\", \"mol_022\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"zinc sulfide, potentially sphalerite\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-320 K proposed sunlit-pool window\",\n        \"pressure\": \"approximately 1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"UV-illuminated ZnS semiconductor surface\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Anoxic suspension at approximately pH 9. Sulfite is a consumed hole scavenger/electron donor, not a catalyst. Natural UV spectrum, mineral defects and surface aging require separate evaluation.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"UV excitation generates electron-hole pairs. Photogenerated reducing equivalents reduce an ammonia-derived imine, while sulfite scavenges oxidizing equivalents. Sulfate represents the proposed fully oxidized sulfur endpoint; water cancels in the formal net reaction.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"SPECULATIVE valine-specific extension. Wang et al. (2012) demonstrated ZnS-driven photochemical amination of other alpha-ketoacids, with improved performance when sulfite scavenged holes, but did not test alpha-ketoisovalerate. The shared carbonyl chemistry motivates this experiment; it does not establish a valine yield. Sulfur-product distribution, substrate photodegradation, competition with hydroxy-acid formation and natural-sunlight efficiency remain open questions.\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"pathways\": [\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P1\",\n      \"name\": \"Reduced-gas discharge followed by explicit Strecker chemistry\",\n      \"description\": \"Methane/ammonia/water processing supplies cyanide and a hypothesized branched aldehyde; imine formation, cyanide capture and sequential hydrolysis yield valine. Direct discharge valine evidence exists, but branched-aldehyde production and natural hydrolysis kinetics are incompletely resolved.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_002\", \"rxn_003\", \"rxn_004\", \"rxn_005\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_006\", \"mol_007\", \"mol_008\", \"mol_009\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P2\",\n      \"name\": \"Acetone plus discharge-supplied cyanide\",\n      \"description\": \"Discharge supplies HCN to an acetone/ammonia pool. Valine production has direct historical support, but the intervening C-C chemistry and displayed coproduct closure remain unresolved. This is not ordinary acetone Strecker chemistry and does not claim valine from cyanide alone.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_006\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_005\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P3\",\n      \"name\": \"Thermal formaldehyde-ammonia empirical route\",\n      \"description\": \"Heated C1 carbonyl/ammonia chemistry followed by hydrolytic processing is represented as one unresolved process. Historical valine detection is qualified by later contamination concerns and by uncertainty about a natural hydrolysis protocol.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_007\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": []\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P4\",\n      \"name\": \"Reduced-gas shock and aqueous recovery\",\n      \"description\": \"Methane, ethane and ammonia undergo shock processing, rapid quenching and aqueous recovery. This has direct product-level evidence but requires an unusually reduced local gas mixture and a transient event outside ordinary surface-pool thermal conditions.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_008\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": []\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P5\",\n      \"name\": \"Supported-nickel hydrogenation of supplied alpha-ketoisovalerate\",\n      \"description\": \"Experimentally demonstrated final-step valine synthesis using Ni and hydrogen. Conditional on an external branched ketoacid supply; it does not solve carbon-skeleton formation. Competing hydroxy-acid production is represented by rxn_012.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_009\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_014\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P6\",\n      \"name\": \"Palladium benchmark for supplied alpha-ketoisovalerate\",\n      \"description\": \"A directly tested, comparatively selective catalyst variant of P5, not a separate carbon-skeleton pathway. Palladium availability and an external alpha-ketoisovalerate supply limit its geochemical interpretation.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_010\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_014\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P7\",\n      \"name\": \"Meteoritic-metal hydrogenation of supplied alpha-ketoisovalerate\",\n      \"description\": \"A directly tested natural-material catalyst variant using ground Aletai meteorite. The supplied ketoacid requirement and dominant hydroxy-acid sink remain substantial limitations.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_011\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_014\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P8\",\n      \"name\": \"Conditional ketoacid Bucherer-Bergs pathway\",\n      \"description\": \"Discharge-derived cyanide reacts with supplied alpha-ketoisovalerate, ammonia and inorganic carbon to form a proposed hydantoin, followed by ring opening and decarbamoylation. All valine-specific transformations in this pathway are substrate-scope hypotheses rather than direct demonstrations.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_013\", \"rxn_014\", \"rxn_015\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_005\", \"mol_014\", \"mol_016\", \"mol_017\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P9\",\n      \"name\": \"Conditional ferrous-hydroxide reductive amination\",\n      \"description\": \"Fresh Fe(II) hydroxide is proposed to supply reducing equivalents for amination of externally supplied alpha-ketoisovalerate. Both the valine substrate extension and the specific oxidized iron end phase require experimental validation.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_016\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_014\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P10\",\n      \"name\": \"Conditional ZnS/sulfite photochemical amination\",\n      \"description\": \"UV-illuminated ZnS and consumed sulfite are proposed to convert externally supplied alpha-ketoisovalerate into valine. This extends an established alpha-ketoacid reaction class but is not a reported valine experiment.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_017\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_014\"]\n    }\n  ],\n  \"hub_molecules\": [\n    \"mol_001\",\n    \"mol_002\",\n    \"mol_003\",\n    \"mol_004\",\n    \"mol_005\",\n    \"mol_010\",\n    \"mol_014\",\n    \"mol_018\"\n  ],\n  \"convergence_points\": [\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_001\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_008\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_002\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\"rxn_005\", \"rxn_006\", \"rxn_015\"],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_001\",\n        \"rxn_002\",\n        \"rxn_006\",\n        \"rxn_007\",\n        \"rxn_008\",\n        \"rxn_009\",\n        \"rxn_010\",\n        \"rxn_011\",\n        \"rxn_013\",\n        \"rxn_016\",\n        \"rxn_017\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_003\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_002\",\n        \"rxn_007\",\n        \"rxn_009\",\n        \"rxn_010\",\n        \"rxn_011\",\n        \"rxn_013\",\n        \"rxn_016\"\n      ],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_001\",\n        \"rxn_004\",\n        \"rxn_005\",\n        \"rxn_006\",\n        \"rxn_008\",\n        \"rxn_014\",\n        \"rxn_015\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_004\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_008\"],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\"rxn_009\", \"rxn_010\", \"rxn_011\", \"rxn_012\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_005\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\"rxn_001\"],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\"rxn_003\", \"rxn_006\", \"rxn_013\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_010\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_005\",\n        \"rxn_006\",\n        \"rxn_007\",\n        \"rxn_008\",\n        \"rxn_009\",\n        \"rxn_010\",\n        \"rxn_011\",\n        \"rxn_015\",\n        \"rxn_016\",\n        \"rxn_017\"\n      ],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": []\n    },\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_014\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_009\",\n        \"rxn_010\",\n        \"rxn_011\",\n        \"rxn_012\",\n        \"rxn_013\",\n        \"rxn_016\",\n        \"rxn_017\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_018\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\"rxn_006\", \"rxn_007\", \"rxn_013\", \"rxn_015\"],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\"rxn_013\"]\n    }\n  ]\n}", "parsed_response": {"target_molecule": "Valine", "overall_strategy": "This is an evidence-graded, connected synthesis network with exactly 10 pathway entries, not 10 independently validated de novo mechanisms. It combines discharge/Strecker chemistry, experimentally reported but mechanistically unresolved acetone/cyanide and heated-formaldehyde chemistry, reduced-gas shock chemistry, three experimentally tested metal-catalyst variants of alpha-ketoisovalerate reductive amination, and three explicitly speculative substrate-scope extensions. P5-P7 are catalyst variants, not different carbon-skeleton construction mechanisms. Alpha-ketoisovalerate is an explicitly supplied starting precursor: the cited research does not establish its prebiotic synthesis from simpler feedstocks. Discharge production of the required branched aldehyde is also insufficiently characterized. Consequently, experimentally demonstrated closure of the entire network from simple geochemical feedstocks cannot be claimed. Empirical process nodes collapse unresolved chemistry rather than inventing molecular identities for heterogeneous precursor material; the carbon dioxide closure proposed for rxn_006 and rxn_007 is explicitly hypothetical. Environments represent separate reaction settings, not a single compatible vessel. Shock and discharge involve transient energetic events outside ordinary bulk-water temperature ranges. All chemistry is anoxic; sulfite and ferrous hydroxide supply reducing equivalents without molecular oxygen. Organic formulas use neutral reference structures, with acid-base speciation described in conditions. Valine is stereochemically unspecified and expected to be racemic absent demonstrated symmetry breaking. A null environment_formed denotes an imported feedstock or formation in multiple environments. Byproducts use the allowed intermediate role.", "molecules": [{"id": "mol_001", "formula": "CH4", "iupac_name": "methane", "common_name": "methane", "inchi": "InChI=1S/CH4/h1H4", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_002", "formula": "NH3", "iupac_name": "azane", "common_name": "ammonia", "inchi": "InChI=1S/H3N/h1H3", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_003", "formula": "H2O", "iupac_name": "oxidane", "common_name": "water", "inchi": "InChI=1S/H2O/h1H2", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_004", "formula": "H2", "iupac_name": "dihydrogen", "common_name": "hydrogen", "inchi": "InChI=1S/H2/h1H", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_005", "formula": "CHN", "iupac_name": "hydrogen cyanide", "common_name": "hydrogen cyanide", "inchi": "InChI=1S/CHN/c1-2/h1H", "role": "hub_intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_006", "formula": "C4H8O", "iupac_name": "2-methylpropanal", "common_name": "isobutyraldehyde", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C4H8O/c1-4(2)3-5/h3-4H,1-2H3", "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_007", "formula": "C4H9N", "iupac_name": "2-methylpropan-1-imine", "common_name": "isobutyraldimine", "inchi": null, "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_008", "formula": "C5H10N2", "iupac_name": "2-amino-3-methylbutanenitrile", "common_name": "valine aminonitrile", "inchi": null, "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_009", "formula": "C5H12N2O", "iupac_name": "2-amino-3-methylbutanamide", "common_name": "valinamide", "inchi": null, "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_010", "formula": "C5H11NO2", "iupac_name": "2-amino-3-methylbutanoic acid", "common_name": "valine", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C5H11NO2/c1-3(2)4(6)5(7)8/h3-4H,6H2,1-2H3,(H,7,8)", "role": "target", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_011", "formula": "C3H6O", "iupac_name": "propan-2-one", "common_name": "acetone", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C3H6O/c1-3(2)4/h1-2H3", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_012", "formula": "CH2O", "iupac_name": "methanal", "common_name": "formaldehyde", "inchi": "InChI=1S/CH2O/c1-2/h1H2", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_013", "formula": "C2H6", "iupac_name": "ethane", "common_name": "ethane", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C2H6/c1-2/h1-2H3", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_014", "formula": "C5H8O3", "iupac_name": "3-methyl-2-oxobutanoic acid", "common_name": "alpha-ketoisovaleric acid", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C5H8O3/c1-3(2)4(6)5(7)8/h3H,1-2H3,(H,7,8)", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_015", "formula": "C5H10O3", "iupac_name": "2-hydroxy-3-methylbutanoic acid", "common_name": "alpha-hydroxyisovaleric acid", "inchi": null, "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Biochemical"}, {"id": "mol_016", "formula": "C6H10N2O2", "iupac_name": "5-(propan-2-yl)imidazolidine-2,4-dione", "common_name": "5-isopropylhydantoin", "inchi": null, "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_017", "formula": "C6H12N2O3", "iupac_name": "2-(carbamoylamino)-3-methylbutanoic acid", "common_name": "N-carbamoylvaline", "inchi": null, "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_018", "formula": "CO2", "iupac_name": "carbon dioxide", "common_name": "carbon dioxide", "inchi": "InChI=1S/CO2/c1-2-3", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_019", "formula": "FeH2O2", "iupac_name": "iron(II) hydroxide", "common_name": "ferrous hydroxide", "inchi": null, "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_020", "formula": "FeHO2", "iupac_name": "iron(III) oxide hydroxide", "common_name": "ferric oxyhydroxide", "inchi": null, "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Hydrothermal"}, {"id": "mol_021", "formula": "SO3^2-", "iupac_name": "sulfite", "common_name": "sulfite ion", "inchi": null, "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_022", "formula": "SO4^2-", "iupac_name": "sulfate", "common_name": "sulfate ion", "inchi": null, "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}], "reactions": [{"id": "rxn_001", "name": "Reduced-gas discharge supplying cyanide and a candidate branched aldehyde", "inputs": ["mol_001", "mol_002", "mol_003"], "outputs": ["mol_005", "mol_006", "mol_004"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-330 K collection water; much hotter transient electrical-discharge channels", "pressure": "approximately 1 atm in a proposed local gas-water setting", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Anoxic methane/ammonia-rich gas over water; repeated electrical discharge and aqueous collection. The listed products are a selected-product projection of a much broader mixture."}, "mechanism": "Discharge-driven fragmentation and recombination generate hydrogen cyanide and carbonyl compounds. Formation of 2-methylpropanal is a proposed branched-carbon component of this unresolved upstream network, not an established elementary sequence.", "reasoning": "Miller (1957) established discharge production of HCN and aldehydes and their connection to Strecker synthesis; Ring et al. (1972) directly identified valine in a related discharge product mixture. The supplied findings do not establish a measured, isotope-resolved discharge-to-isobutyraldehyde sequence, so that species-specific edge is speculative. Producing sufficient branched C4 aldehyde is the key skeleton bottleneck. A locally reducing mixture is required; a globally persistent methane/ammonia atmosphere is not assumed, given Trail et al. (2011). No mineral catalyst is justified."}, {"id": "rxn_002", "name": "Isobutyraldehyde-ammonia condensation", "inputs": ["mol_006", "mol_002"], "outputs": ["mol_007", "mol_003"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-330 K", "pressure": "approximately 1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Concentrated, mildly alkaline water, approximately pH 8-9; imine is a transient equilibrium intermediate. Rapid cyanide capture must compete with hydrolysis and aldehyde loss."}, "mechanism": "Ammonia adds to the aldehyde, followed by carbinolamine dehydration to an imine. Subsequent cyanide addition can pull the reversible condensation forward.", "reasoning": "This is the conventional Strecker condensation underlying the mechanistic framework of Miller (1957). Its application to isobutyraldehyde is chemically well motivated, but the cited findings do not separately quantify this imine or its equilibrium abundance. Concentration and capture, rather than an unjustified mineral catalyst, are the relevant controls."}, {"id": "rxn_003", "name": "Cyanide addition forming valine aminonitrile", "inputs": ["mol_007", "mol_005"], "outputs": ["mol_008"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-330 K", "pressure": "approximately 1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Approximately pH 8-9, with HCN/CN- and imine/iminium speciation. Water depth or pore shading reduces photodestruction without requiring an ozone layer."}, "mechanism": "Cyanide attacks the imine/iminium carbon. Proton transfer gives 2-amino-3-methylbutanenitrile; cyanide supplies the future carboxyl carbon.", "reasoning": "Miller (1957) supports aminonitrile formation as the central Strecker intermediate, while Ring et al. (1972) supports accessibility of valine in discharge chemistry. This carbon addition gives the correct C5 connectivity, unlike ordinary acetone Strecker chemistry, which gives alpha-aminoisobutyric acid. Valine-specific rates and competing cyanide reactions remain insufficiently characterized."}, {"id": "rxn_004", "name": "Valine aminonitrile hydration to valinamide", "inputs": ["mol_008", "mol_003"], "outputs": ["mol_009"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["hydronium ions"], "conditions": {"temperature": "340-370 K; proposed natural hydrolysis window", "pressure": "approximately 1 atm", "catalyst": "acid catalysis by hydronium", "aqueous": true, "other": "Proposed acidification to approximately pH 1-3 after cyanide capture, for example in an acidic geothermal pool. Required residence time is not established for this substrate."}, "mechanism": "Nitrile protonation, water addition and proton transfers produce the corresponding primary amide.", "reasoning": "Miller (1957) supports aminonitrile hydrolysis as the source of accumulated amino acids. The explicit valinamide node and these mildly heated environmental conditions are a mechanistic reconstruction, not a separately reported valine-specific experiment. Strong analytical acid hydrolysis should not be equated with rapid natural hydrolysis; this step requires kinetic validation."}, {"id": "rxn_005", "name": "Valinamide hydrolysis to valine", "inputs": ["mol_009", "mol_003"], "outputs": ["mol_010", "mol_002"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["hydronium ions"], "conditions": {"temperature": "350-370 K; proposed natural hydrolysis window", "pressure": "approximately 1 atm", "catalyst": "acid catalysis by hydronium", "aqueous": true, "other": "Approximately pH 1-3 following rxn_004; released ammonia is predominantly ammonium. Residence time and product survival require measurement."}, "mechanism": "Water addition to the protonated amide carbonyl and C-N bond cleavage release valine and ammonia/ammonium.", "reasoning": "This completes the conventional nitrile-to-amino-acid chemistry supported at the reaction-family level by Miller (1957). Separate valinamide kinetics under the proposed surface conditions are not supplied, so this step is an explicit mechanistic extrapolation. Hydrolysis may be a bottleneck, and the reported analytical recovery of amino acids does not establish efficient free-valine accumulation in every natural pool."}, {"id": "rxn_006", "name": "Acetone-ammonium-cyanide valine production: empirical process with hypothetical coproduct closure", "inputs": ["mol_011", "mol_005", "mol_002", "mol_003"], "outputs": ["mol_010", "mol_002", "mol_018"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-350 K proposed accumulation stage; subsequent hydrolysis conditions are not established by the supplied summary", "pressure": "approximately 1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Concentrated cyanide/ammonia pool containing acetone, followed by hydrolytic processing. This is not a validated single-temperature natural protocol."}, "mechanism": "An unresolved cyanide/ketone network constructs additional C-C bonds and produces valine or hydrolysable valine precursors. The graph proposes internal redox disproportionation with CO2 and regenerated ammonia as an atom-balance-compatible closure; those coproduct assignments are not experimentally established.", "reasoning": "Friedmann and Miller (1969) directly obtained valine when acetone was added to NH4CN, but failed to confirm valine from NH4CN alone. Ordinary acetone Strecker chemistry cannot explain this result because it yields a C4 amino acid. Valine production is supported; the detailed branching mechanism, natural hydrolysis conditions and the CO2-containing closure represented here are speculative. Stoichiometric inference from this node must not be treated as an experimentally measured yield or carbon map."}, {"id": "rxn_007", "name": "Heated formaldehyde-ammonia chemistry: historical empirical valine branch", "inputs": ["mol_012", "mol_002"], "outputs": ["mol_010", "mol_018", "mol_003"], "environment": "Hydrothermal", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "approximately 458 K heating stage, followed by cooling and hydrolytic processing", "pressure": "greater than approximately 12 bar if liquid water is retained; proposed confined-pore analogue", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Concentrated formaldehyde/ammonia chemistry. Hydrothermal placement is an environmental extrapolation, not a claim that the original experiment reproduced a vent. Hydrolysis work-up requirements remain a separate uncertainty."}, "mechanism": "Thermal carbonyl condensation, nitrogen incorporation and internal redox chemistry form complex hydrolysable products. The valine-specific branched sequence is unresolved. CO2 is proposed as an oxidized coproduct permitting formal carbon/redox closure, not as an experimentally traced valine-associated product.", "reasoning": "Fox and Windsor (1970) historically reported valine after heating formaldehyde/ammonia products and hydrolysing them. However, Kebukawa et al. (2017) considered L-only valine in a related thermal system likely contamination. This is therefore a low-confidence empirical branch, not a reproducibly quantified modern synthesis. The CO2 coproduct assignment and transfer to a confined terrestrial hydrothermal setting are speculative and require isotopic validation."}, {"id": "rxn_008", "name": "Reduced-gas shock, rapid quench and aqueous recovery of valine", "inputs": ["mol_001", "mol_013", "mol_002", "mol_003"], "outputs": ["mol_010", "mol_004"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "Transient high-temperature shock, followed by rapid cooling into approximately 300-330 K collection water", "pressure": "transient shock compression followed by decompression; no specific pressure inferred from the supplied summary", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": false, "other": "Gas-phase initiation using approximately 3.3% methane, 11% ethane and 5.6% ammonia in argon. Water enters subsequent collection/hydrolysis, not the initial reduced-gas shock. This is a surface event, not an isothermal 300-370 K pool reaction."}, "mechanism": "Shock-induced fragmentation and radical recombination create reduced nitrogenous organic products. Rapid quenching preserves reactive intermediates; aqueous processing supplies oxygen and can release amino acids. The listed outputs are a selected-product projection, not a resolved elementary mechanism.", "reasoning": "Bar-Nun et al. (1970) directly reported valine from this reduced-gas shock family. The valine-specific intermediate sequence and product-associated hydrogen stoichiometry were not resolved. Its environmental limitation is the highly reduced starting gas, given Trail et al. (2011). Takeuchi et al. (2020) demonstrated amino-acid formation in more conservative CO2/N2/Fe impact mixtures but did not demonstrate valine, so those experiments cannot close this feedstock gap."}, {"id": "rxn_009", "name": "Nickel-catalysed reductive amination of alpha-ketoisovalerate", "inputs": ["mol_014", "mol_002", "mol_004"], "outputs": ["mol_010", "mol_003"], "environment": "Biochemical", "agents": ["Ni supported on SiO2-Al2O3"], "conditions": {"temperature": "295 K", "pressure": "5 bar H2", "catalyst": "Ni/SiO2-Al2O3; 10 mol% Ni relative to ketoacid", "aqueous": true, "other": "30 mM alpha-ketoisovalerate, five equivalents NH4HCO3, pH approximately 8, 72 hours. This is a cool pressurized aqueous interface, not a hot vent interior."}, "mechanism": "Ammonia forms an imine/iminium-type intermediate at the alpha-keto group. Nickel activates hydrogen and hydrogenates that intermediate to valine.", "reasoning": "Kaur et al. (2024) directly tested alpha-ketoisovalerate. Their approximately 20% combined amino/hydroxy-acid yield and 34:66 product ratio imply about 6.8% valine, an arithmetic inference rather than a separately reported yield. Catalyst-free controls lacked reduction products. Hydrogen and nickel have plausible geological sources, but the supported catalyst is a laboratory material and the 30 mM branched ketoacid was supplied, not synthesized in the network."}, {"id": "rxn_010", "name": "Palladium-catalysed reductive amination benchmark", "inputs": ["mol_014", "mol_002", "mol_004"], "outputs": ["mol_010", "mol_003"], "environment": "Biochemical", "agents": ["palladium on carbon"], "conditions": {"temperature": "295 K", "pressure": "5 bar H2", "catalyst": "Pd/C; 1 mol% Pd relative to ketoacid", "aqueous": true, "other": "30 mM alpha-ketoisovalerate, five equivalents NH4HCO3, pH approximately 8, 72 hours."}, "mechanism": "Palladium-mediated hydrogen activation reduces the ammonia-derived imine/iminium intermediate to valine.", "reasoning": "Kaur et al. (2024) directly reported approximately 77% combined amino/hydroxy-acid yield with an 83:17 ratio, implying about 64% valine. This is the most selective of the three tested catalyst variants but is retained as an experimental benchmark, not asserted to be an abundant prebiotic catalyst. Natural availability of comparably active palladium surfaces and the origin of alpha-ketoisovalerate remain unresolved."}, {"id": "rxn_011", "name": "Meteoritic Fe-Ni-catalysed reductive amination", "inputs": ["mol_014", "mol_002", "mol_004"], "outputs": ["mol_010", "mol_003"], "environment": "Biochemical", "agents": ["ground Aletai iron meteorite containing nickel-bearing metallic phases"], "conditions": {"temperature": "295 K", "pressure": "5 bar H2", "catalyst": "3 mg ground Aletai meteorite in the reported experiment", "aqueous": true, "other": "30 mM alpha-ketoisovalerate, five equivalents NH4HCO3, pH approximately 8, 72 hours. Activity of weathered natural surfaces is not established."}, "mechanism": "Hydrogen activation and imine hydrogenation occur on reactive meteoritic metal surfaces; competing ketone hydrogenation diverts substrate to the hydroxy acid.", "reasoning": "Kaur et al. (2024) directly reported approximately 74% combined amino/hydroxy-acid yield and an 18:82 ratio, implying about 13% valine. Meteorite-derived metal is a more directly natural material than Pd/C, but hydroxy-acid formation dominates. The experiment does not demonstrate formation of the branched ketoacid or preservation of activity after terrestrial surface alteration."}, {"id": "rxn_012", "name": "Competing hydrogenation of alpha-ketoisovalerate to its hydroxy acid", "inputs": ["mol_014", "mol_004"], "outputs": ["mol_015"], "environment": "Biochemical", "agents": ["Ni/SiO2-Al2O3 as one tested alternative", "Pd/C as one tested alternative", "ground Aletai meteorite as one tested alternative"], "conditions": {"temperature": "295 K", "pressure": "5 bar H2", "catalyst": "One of the three listed catalysts, used separately rather than mixed", "aqueous": true, "other": "Same aqueous ketoacid/ammonium conditions as rxn_009-rxn_011; catalyst-dependent competition with reductive amination."}, "mechanism": "Surface-mediated hydrogenation of the keto carbonyl produces 2-hydroxy-3-methylbutanoic acid instead of forming a C-N bond.", "reasoning": "Kaur et al. (2024) directly measured this competing product. Its shares of the combined amino/hydroxy products were approximately 66% for supported Ni, 17% for Pd/C and 82% for Aletai meteorite. This terminal side branch is included because a valine network that ignores the dominant carbon sink on geochemically attractive catalysts would overstate productivity; no unsupported conversion of this hydroxy acid back to valine is added."}, {"id": "rxn_013", "name": "Hypothetical alpha-ketoisovalerate Bucherer-Bergs conversion to hydantoin", "inputs": ["mol_014", "mol_005", "mol_002", "mol_018"], "outputs": ["mol_016", "mol_018", "mol_003"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "approximately 300 K proposed surface analogue of the reported room-temperature stage", "pressure": "approximately 1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Reaction-class benchmark: approximately 0.35 M ketoacid, 1.2 equivalents cyanide, two equivalents ammonia source and 0.5 M bicarbonate at pH approximately 8.5 for 21 days. These are not established optimal valine conditions. CO2 appears on both sides to represent inorganic-carbon exchange in the lumped process."}, "mechanism": "Proposed cyanide/ammonia addition, inorganic-carbon capture, cyclization and decarboxylative processing yield 5-isopropylhydantoin. The intervening carboxylated species and carbon-atom mapping are not assigned as experimentally established valine intermediates.", "reasoning": "SPECULATIVE substrate extension. Pulletikurti et al. (2022) established alpha-ketoacid-to-amino-acid chemistry involving hydantoins, but did not directly demonstrate alpha-ketoisovalerate-to-valine in the documented substrate set. This proposed step is justified by the shared alpha-ketoacid functionality, not by a valine experiment. Precursor supply, concentrated bicarbonate/cyanide, branching-dependent kinetics and the exact decarboxylation sequence remain unresolved."}, {"id": "rxn_014", "name": "Hypothetical hydrolytic opening of valine hydantoin", "inputs": ["mol_016", "mol_003"], "outputs": ["mol_017"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "353 K", "pressure": "approximately 1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Warm bicarbonate/ammonia solution following rxn_013. Fourteen days is the reported reaction-class heated-stage duration, not a measured lifetime for this specific hydantoin."}, "mechanism": "Hydrolytic cleavage of the hydantoin ring produces N-carbamoylvaline.", "reasoning": "SPECULATIVE valine-specific extension. Pulletikurti et al. (2022) provided mechanistic support for hydantoin hydrolysis through N-carbamoyl amino acids. The proposed isopropyl-substituted substrate was not directly validated in the supplied findings. Slow ring opening, competing degradation and dependence on ammonia-source chemistry must be tested rather than inferred from the generic temperature protocol."}, {"id": "rxn_015", "name": "Hypothetical N-carbamoylvaline hydrolysis", "inputs": ["mol_017", "mol_003"], "outputs": ["mol_010", "mol_002", "mol_018"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "353 K", "pressure": "approximately 1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Continuation of the heated hydrolysis stage; ammonia/ammonium and CO2/bicarbonate speciation depend on pH. No separate valine-specific completion time is asserted."}, "mechanism": "Hydrolytic removal of the carbamoyl group releases valine, ammonia and inorganic carbon.", "reasoning": "SPECULATIVE valine-specific extension of the N-carbamoyl-amino-acid chemistry described by Pulletikurti et al. (2022). This is a chemically justified deprotection step, but its rate and yield for N-carbamoylvaline under the proposed conditions are unestablished. Merely reaching a hydantoin or carbamoylated precursor must not be counted as demonstrated accumulation of free valine."}, {"id": "rxn_016", "name": "Hypothetical ferrous-hydroxide-driven reductive amination", "inputs": ["mol_014", "mol_002", "mol_019"], "outputs": ["mol_010", "mol_020", "mol_003"], "environment": "Hydrothermal", "agents": ["freshly precipitated iron(II) hydroxide, consumed as a reductant rather than acting as a conserved catalyst"], "conditions": {"temperature": "350-373 K proposed cool hydrothermal interface", "pressure": "sufficient hydrostatic pressure to retain liquid water", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Approximately pH 8.8-9.3, low ionic strength and fresh reactive Fe(II) hydroxide. Ferric oxyhydroxide is a proposed oxidized end phase, not an established phase assignment for this valine reaction."}, "mechanism": "Ammonia-derived imine formation is followed by electron transfer coupled to oxidation of Fe(II). The displayed formal net reaction uses FeOOH to close the two-electron reduction without molecular oxygen.", "reasoning": "SPECULATIVE substrate and solid-product extension. Huber and Wachtershauser (2003) demonstrated reductive amination of other alpha-ketoacids with freshly prepared FeS or Fe(OH)2, not alpha-ketoisovalerate. Fe(OH)2 is represented as a consumed electron donor to avoid inventing a reductant-free catalytic cycle. The actual oxidized solid could be mixed-valence or poorly crystalline rather than the displayed FeOOH reference phase. Salinity, aging and carbonate precipitation may suppress the proposed chemistry."}, {"id": "rxn_017", "name": "Hypothetical ZnS-photocatalysed valine formation with sulfite oxidation", "inputs": ["mol_014", "mol_002", "mol_021"], "outputs": ["mol_010", "mol_022"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["zinc sulfide, potentially sphalerite"], "conditions": {"temperature": "300-320 K proposed sunlit-pool window", "pressure": "approximately 1 atm", "catalyst": "UV-illuminated ZnS semiconductor surface", "aqueous": true, "other": "Anoxic suspension at approximately pH 9. Sulfite is a consumed hole scavenger/electron donor, not a catalyst. Natural UV spectrum, mineral defects and surface aging require separate evaluation."}, "mechanism": "UV excitation generates electron-hole pairs. Photogenerated reducing equivalents reduce an ammonia-derived imine, while sulfite scavenges oxidizing equivalents. Sulfate represents the proposed fully oxidized sulfur endpoint; water cancels in the formal net reaction.", "reasoning": "SPECULATIVE valine-specific extension. Wang et al. (2012) demonstrated ZnS-driven photochemical amination of other alpha-ketoacids, with improved performance when sulfite scavenged holes, but did not test alpha-ketoisovalerate. The shared carbonyl chemistry motivates this experiment; it does not establish a valine yield. Sulfur-product distribution, substrate photodegradation, competition with hydroxy-acid formation and natural-sunlight efficiency remain open questions."}], "pathways": [{"pathway_id": "P1", "name": "Reduced-gas discharge followed by explicit Strecker chemistry", "description": "Methane/ammonia/water processing supplies cyanide and a hypothesized branched aldehyde; imine formation, cyanide capture and sequential hydrolysis yield valine. Direct discharge valine evidence exists, but branched-aldehyde production and natural hydrolysis kinetics are incompletely resolved.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_001", "rxn_002", "rxn_003", "rxn_004", "rxn_005"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_006", "mol_007", "mol_008", "mol_009"]}, {"pathway_id": "P2", "name": "Acetone plus discharge-supplied cyanide", "description": "Discharge supplies HCN to an acetone/ammonia pool. Valine production has direct historical support, but the intervening C-C chemistry and displayed coproduct closure remain unresolved. This is not ordinary acetone Strecker chemistry and does not claim valine from cyanide alone.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_001", "rxn_006"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_005"]}, {"pathway_id": "P3", "name": "Thermal formaldehyde-ammonia empirical route", "description": "Heated C1 carbonyl/ammonia chemistry followed by hydrolytic processing is represented as one unresolved process. Historical valine detection is qualified by later contamination concerns and by uncertainty about a natural hydrolysis protocol.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_007"], "key_intermediates": []}, {"pathway_id": "P4", "name": "Reduced-gas shock and aqueous recovery", "description": "Methane, ethane and ammonia undergo shock processing, rapid quenching and aqueous recovery. This has direct product-level evidence but requires an unusually reduced local gas mixture and a transient event outside ordinary surface-pool thermal conditions.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_008"], "key_intermediates": []}, {"pathway_id": "P5", "name": "Supported-nickel hydrogenation of supplied alpha-ketoisovalerate", "description": "Experimentally demonstrated final-step valine synthesis using Ni and hydrogen. Conditional on an external branched ketoacid supply; it does not solve carbon-skeleton formation. Competing hydroxy-acid production is represented by rxn_012.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_009"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_014"]}, {"pathway_id": "P6", "name": "Palladium benchmark for supplied alpha-ketoisovalerate", "description": "A directly tested, comparatively selective catalyst variant of P5, not a separate carbon-skeleton pathway. Palladium availability and an external alpha-ketoisovalerate supply limit its geochemical interpretation.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_010"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_014"]}, {"pathway_id": "P7", "name": "Meteoritic-metal hydrogenation of supplied alpha-ketoisovalerate", "description": "A directly tested natural-material catalyst variant using ground Aletai meteorite. The supplied ketoacid requirement and dominant hydroxy-acid sink remain substantial limitations.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_011"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_014"]}, {"pathway_id": "P8", "name": "Conditional ketoacid Bucherer-Bergs pathway", "description": "Discharge-derived cyanide reacts with supplied alpha-ketoisovalerate, ammonia and inorganic carbon to form a proposed hydantoin, followed by ring opening and decarbamoylation. All valine-specific transformations in this pathway are substrate-scope hypotheses rather than direct demonstrations.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_001", "rxn_013", "rxn_014", "rxn_015"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_005", "mol_014", "mol_016", "mol_017"]}, {"pathway_id": "P9", "name": "Conditional ferrous-hydroxide reductive amination", "description": "Fresh Fe(II) hydroxide is proposed to supply reducing equivalents for amination of externally supplied alpha-ketoisovalerate. Both the valine substrate extension and the specific oxidized iron end phase require experimental validation.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_016"], "key_intermediates": ["mol