nd gas availability remain unspecified.", "approved": true, "problematic_reactions": ["rxn_008"], "suggestions": ["Specify gas composition, inert diluent, shock temperature and pressure, pulse duration, cooling rate and aqueous recovery procedure.", "Distinguish measured amino-acid production from the hypothetical net H2 yield and other uncharacterized products.", "If making a geological claim, justify a local or transient reduced-gas reservoir rather than assuming a persistent global atmosphere."]}, {"pathway_id": "P5", "reaction_scores": {"rxn_009": 0.95, "rxn_012": 0.96}, "chemical_feasibility": 0.95, "pathway_coherence": 0.94, "environmental_progression": 0.75, "target_achievement": 0.96, "overall_confidence": 0.89, "feedback": "Kaur et al. (2024) directly support nickel-catalyzed conversion of supplied alpha-ketoisovalerate to valine. The declared starting precursor makes this a valid one-step synthesis without requiring an upstream carbon-skeleton construction route. The competing hydroxy-acid branch is appropriately included: the reported 20% combined yield and 34:66 amino-to-hydroxy ratio imply approximately 6.8% valine, not 20%. Environmental confidence is limited by the absence of an explicit operating recipe and justification of the active supported-nickel material. rxn_012 is evaluated as a competing branch, not a second productive step.", "approved": true, "problematic_reactions": [], "suggestions": ["Specify the demonstrated conditions: 30 mM ketoacid, five equivalents NH4HCO3, pH 8, 5 bar H2, 22 degrees Celsius, 72 hours and supported catalyst containing 10 mol% Ni relative to substrate.", "Track valine and hydroxy-acid yields separately and quantify additional carbon losses.", "Distinguish the tested supported catalyst from untested natural nickel phases, and retain the supplied-precursor scope limitation."]}, {"pathway_id": "P6", "reaction_scores": {"rxn_010": 0.97, "rxn_012": 0.96}, "chemical_feasibility": 0.97, "pathway_coherence": 0.94, "environmental_progression": 0.75, "target_achievement": 0.98, "overall_confidence": 0.91, "feedback": "This is the strongest demonstrated productive branch for the stated feedstocks. Kaur et al. (2024) reported a 77% combined valine-plus-hydroxy-acid yield with an 83:17 product ratio, implying approximately 64% valine. The reductive-amination mechanism and final product are well supported. Calling Pd/C a benchmark appropriately avoids claiming that its laboratory performance establishes natural availability. The generic environment label still needs an explicit recipe. Selection as the best pathway reflects experimental evidence and selectivity, not a preference for palladium or for a particular precursor framework.", "approved": true, "problematic_reactions": [], "suggestions": ["Specify the Kaur et al. conditions and Pd/C loading containing 1 mol% Pd relative to substrate.", "Report the approximately 64% inferred valine yield separately from the 77% combined product yield.", "Maintain the laboratory-benchmark designation; substantiate active catalyst availability separately if extending the claim to a natural setting."]}, {"pathway_id": "P7", "reaction_scores": {"rxn_011": 0.95, "rxn_012": 0.96}, "chemical_feasibility": 0.95, "pathway_coherence": 0.94, "environmental_progression": 0.75, "target_achievement": 0.96, "overall_confidence": 0.89, "feedback": "Kaur et al. (2024) directly demonstrated valine formation using ground Aletai meteorite. The reported 74% combined yield and 18:82 amino-to-hydroxy ratio imply approximately 13% valine, with hydroxy-acid formation dominating. This is strong evidence for the final conversion, but not proof that arbitrary Fe-Ni minerals or weathered meteorites have equivalent activity. Imported alpha-ketoisovalerate is a valid boundary condition. The missing operating details and catalyst surface-state specification prevent stronger environmental confidence.", "approved": true, "problematic_reactions": [], "suggestions": ["Specify the tested meteorite identity, composition, grinding or surface preparation, 3 mg loading, and corresponding solution and H2 conditions.", "Quantify hydroxy-acid diversion explicitly rather than equating total substrate reduction with valine production.", "Test catalyst aging and passivation before extrapolating from freshly ground Aletai material to a natural exposure."]}, {"pathway_id": "P8", "reaction_scores": {"rxn_001": 0.65, "rxn_013": 0.66, "rxn_014": 0.84, "rxn_015": 0.84}, "chemical_feasibility": 0.74, "pathway_coherence": 0.75, "environmental_progression": 0.6, "target_achievement": 0.68, "overall_confidence": 0.69, "feedback": "Interpreted as rxn_001 supplying HCN followed by rxn_013 through rxn_015, this matches the stated four-step pathway. Pulletikurti et al. (2022) support the reaction class and hydantoin/N-carbamoyl sequence, but not alpha-ketoisovalerate-to-valine specifically. The proposed intermediate identities are chemically reasonable, while substrate-specific cyclization, decarboxylation and hydrolysis performance remain untested. The main environmental gap is achieving and maintaining the required concentrations and long staged processing, not the order or number of settings.", "approved": false, "problematic_reactions": ["rxn_001", "rxn_013"], "suggestions": ["Directly test alpha-ketoisovalerate and identify 5-isopropylhydantoin and N-carbamoylvaline using orthogonal analytical methods.", "Track carbon incorporation and decarboxylation rather than inferring the mechanism from the balanced net equation.", "Specify or experimentally relax the reference conditions of approximately 0.35 M ketoacid, concentrated cyanide/ammonia, 0.5 M bicarbonate, 21 days near room temperature and 14 days at 80 degrees Celsius; justify any required concentration process."]}, {"pathway_id": "P9", "reaction_scores": {"rxn_016": 0.68}, "chemical_feasibility": 0.68, "pathway_coherence": 0.77, "environmental_progression": 0.59, "target_achievement": 0.6, "overall_confidence": 0.65, "feedback": "Huber and Wachtershauser (2003) provide experimental precedent for ferrous-hydroxide-mediated reductive amination, but not for the valine substrate. The balanced two-iron oxidation supplies an appropriate formal reducing-equivalent inventory without requiring molecular oxygen. Nevertheless, alpha-ketoisovalerate conversion, the assumed FeOOH endpoint and effective mineral surface state are unverified. Freshness, pH, ionic strength and temperature materially affect the cited chemistry, so a generic hydrothermal designation is insufficient.", "approved": false, "problematic_reactions": ["rxn_016"], "suggestions": ["Test alpha-ketoisovalerate with freshly precipitated Fe(OH)2 while controlling pH, ionic strength, temperature and mineral aging.", "Use the cited pH range near 8.8-9.3 as a starting point rather than assuming unrestricted hydrothermal compatibility.", "Measure iron oxidation states and solid products, and quantify valine against competing reduction and decomposition products."]}, {"pathway_id": "P10", "reaction_scores": {"rxn_017": 0.62}, "chemical_feasibility": 0.63, "pathway_coherence": 0.75, "environmental_progression": 0.58, "target_achievement": 0.54, "overall_confidence": 0.61, "feedback": "Wang et al. (2012) support ZnS-mediated photochemical amination of other alpha-ketoacids, not alpha-ketoisovalerate. Sulfite oxidation can supply the formal reducing equivalents, and the supplied check establishes conservation without an oxygen requirement. However, valine formation, selectivity, photostability and sulfate production have not been demonstrated for this substrate. An illuminated anoxic surface setting is plausible in principle, but light spectrum, photon flux, catalyst state and solution conditions are missing.", "approved": false, "problematic_reactions": ["rxn_017"], "suggestions": ["Specify ZnS identity and loading, UV spectrum and flux, pH near the cited value of 9, reactant concentrations and oxygen exclusion.", "Test alpha-ketoisovalerate directly with dark, catalyst-free and sulfite-free controls and isotope-confirmed valine identification.", "Quantify sulfate and other sulfur products together with amino-acid, hydroxy-acid and photodecomposition products."]}], "best_pathway_id": "P6", "overall_confidence": 0.79, "network_feedback": "The network has sufficient nodes, consistent molecule IDs and no isolated subgraphs. All 17 reactions are accepted as atom/charge conserving under the supplied deterministic check; five require explicit coefficients. The unparsable mol_018 InChI is a metadata defect, not a conservation violation, because its stated CO2 formula was checked. Formal balancing does not establish empirical product stoichiometry, particularly for rxn_006 and rxn_007. The network contains a well-supported productive core in rxn_009-rxn_011 and correctly represents rxn_012 as a competing sink; its scores under P5-P7 refer to that side branch, not additional productive steps. Imported alpha-ketoisovalerate is a legitimate starting material, and its unestablished simpler-feedstock origin limits claim scope rather than invalidating these conversions. Whole-network confidence is constrained by the unquantified branched-aldehyde output of rxn_001, unresolved empirical closures, weak modern validation of rxn_007, and untested valine-specific extensions in rxn_013, rxn_016 and rxn_017. Evidence grading aligns substantially with the supplied literature, but reaction-level citations and the thermal-route contamination caveat should be added. Separate environments and catalyst variants are acceptable and receive no numerical bonus or penalty. Environmental confidence is reduced by missing operating parameters and catalyst-state details; transfers need justification only where actually required. P6 is the strongest demonstrated conversion from its declared precursor, not a demonstrated natural end-to-end synthesis. The complete network does not meet the 0.90 approval threshold."}, "reasoning": "", "duration_ms": 228432.83247947693, "success": true, "error_message": null}
{"timestamp": "2026-10-19T16:20:57.981326", "agent_name": "SynthesisNetworkAgent", "method_name": "generate_synthesis_network", "step": null, "attempt": null, "system_message": "\n# SYNTHESIS NETWORK AGENT - Complete Prebiotic Synthesis Network Generation\n\nYou are an expert prebiotic geochemist who designs complete synthesis networks from simple\nmolecules to complex biomolecules. You synthesize knowledge across three terrestrial prebiotic\nenvironments:\n\n1. **Hydrothermal Environments** (350-600K, high pressure, deep-sea vents)\n2. **Surface Environments** (300-370K, ambient pressure, wet-dry cycles, UV exposure)\n3. **Biochemical/Prebiotic** (variable conditions, complex biomolecule assembly)\n\nYour task is to generate a COMPLETE SYNTHESIS NETWORK in a SINGLE response - not individual steps.\n\n---\n\n# SECTION 1: HYDROTHERMAL ENVIRONMENTS\n\n## Context\nDeep-sea hydrothermal systems. High pressure, temperatures between 350-600K, pH gradients\nacross vent walls, and mineral surfaces.\n\n## Environments\n- **Alkaline Hydrothermal Vents**: 350-450K, pH 9-11, H\u2082-rich, serpentinization-driven\n- **Black Smoker Vents**: 500-650K, acidic, metal-sulfide rich\n- **Subseafloor Hydrothermal**: 370-470K, moderate pH, rock-water interactions\n\n## Minerals Present in This Setting (examples, non-exhaustive)\nIron sulfides (e.g., mackinawite, greigite, pyrite), iron oxides (e.g., magnetite), native metals\nand alloys. Use a mineral only if it is chemically justified for the specific reaction.\n\n---\n\n# SECTION 2: SURFACE ENVIRONMENTS\n\n## Context\nAmbient pressure, temperatures between 300-370K, wet-dry cycles, UV exposure, and evaporitic\npools.\n\n## Environments\n- **Evaporitic Pools**: 300-370K, wet-dry cycles, mineral-rich shores\n- **Volcanic Hot Springs**: 320-370K, acidic, sulfur-rich, boron minerals\n- **Tidal Flats**: 280-330K, periodic wetting/drying, UV exposure\n- **Subaerial Geothermal Fields**: 300-370K, steam vents, mineral surfaces\n\n## Minerals Present in This Setting (examples, non-exhaustive)\nClays (e.g., montmorillonite), phosphate minerals, borate minerals, metal sulfides, metal oxides.\nUse a mineral only if it is chemically justified for the specific reaction.\n\n---\n\n# SECTION 3: BIOCHEMICAL/PREBIOTIC\n\n## Context\nAssembly of more complex biomolecules under plausible prebiotic scenarios, under variable\nconditions including pools, interfaces, and mineral surfaces.\n\n## Environments\n- **Prebiotic Pools**: Concentrated solutions of organic molecules\n- **Mineral Interfaces**: Mineral surfaces that can adsorb or template reactants\n- **Lipid Membranes**: Protocell-like compartments for concentrated chemistry\n- **Geothermal Cycling**: Repeated heating/cooling\n\n## Global Constraints (all environments)\n- NO molecular oxygen (anoxic early Earth)\n- Strong UV radiation at the surface (no ozone layer)\n- Iron abundant in reduced form (Fe\u00b2\u207a)\n\n---\n\n# SECTION 4: NETWORK STRUCTURE REQUIREMENTS\n\nYour output must be a BIPARTITE GRAPH with two distinct node types:\n\n## 1. MOLECULE NODES\n- Represent chemical species (substrates, products, intermediates)\n- Each molecule appears ONCE in the network, even if used by multiple reactions\n- Include: id, formula, IUPAC name, common name, InChI, role, environment_formed\n\n## 2. REACTION NODES\n- Each reaction is a separate node with a unique ID (e.g., \"rxn_001\")\n- Has INPUT molecules (reactants) and OUTPUT molecules (products)\n- Includes: id, name, inputs[], outputs[], environment, agents[], conditions, mechanism, reasoning\n- List each molecule ID at most once in inputs[] and once in outputs[]; stoichiometric coefficients\n  are not represented and are inferred automatically, so never repeat an ID to express them\n\n## NEUTRALITY (CRITICAL)\n- No starting feedstock, intermediate, reaction family, or prebiotic framework is preferred.\n  Choose routes solely on chemical and geochemical merit for this specific target.\n- Environments may be used in any order and in any combination; a network that uses only one\n  environment is acceptable if that is what the chemistry supports.\n- Do not add intermediates, shared nodes, or cross-environment transfers merely to make the\n  network look interconnected. Include them only when the chemistry requires them.\n\n## DESIGN PRINCIPLES\n1. **Agents/catalysts**: Specify a catalyst or mineral agent in the \"agents\" field when one is\n   used and justify it; use null when no catalyst is needed.\n2. **Reaction diversity**: Use whatever reaction classes the chemistry of the target requires.\n3. **Alternative routes**: Where the chemistry supports more than one route, include them as\n   separate pathways.\n\n## NETWORK STRUCTURE REQUIREMENTS\n\n- Minimum 8-15 molecule nodes\n- Minimum 6-12 reaction nodes\n- No isolated subgraphs - all nodes must be connected\n\n---\n\n# SECTION 5: REQUIRED JSON OUTPUT FORMAT\n\nYou MUST output a JSON object with this exact structure:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"target_molecule\": \"<name of target>\",\n  \"overall_strategy\": \"<high-level explanation of the synthesis approach>\",\n  \"molecules\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_001\",\n      \"formula\": \"<chemical formula>\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"<IUPAC name>\",\n      \"common_name\": \"<common name or null>\",\n      \"inchi\": \"<InChI string or null>\",\n      \"role\": \"<starting | intermediate | hub_intermediate | target>\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"<Hydrothermal | Surface | Biochemical | null>\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"reactions\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_001\",\n      \"name\": \"<descriptive reaction name>\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_001\", \"mol_002\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_003\"],\n      \"environment\": \"<Hydrothermal | Surface | Biochemical>\",\n      \"agents\": [\"<catalyst/mineral agent, if any>\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"<e.g., 350-400K>\",\n        \"pressure\": \"<e.g., high, 1 atm>\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"<catalyst name, or null>\",\n        \"aqueous\": \"<true | false>\",\n        \"other\": \"<additional conditions>\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"<brief mechanism description>\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"<scientific justification>\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"pathways\": [\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P1\",\n      \"name\": \"<descriptive pathway name>\",\n      \"description\": \"<pathway description>\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_003\", \"rxn_007\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_004\", \"mol_008\"]\n    }\n  ],\n  \"hub_molecules\": [\"<mol_id, if any>\"],\n  \"convergence_points\": [\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_004\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\"rxn_002\", \"rxn_005\"],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\"rxn_003\", \"rxn_006\", \"rxn_009\"]\n    }\n  ]\n}\n```\n\nNotes on \"hub_molecules\" and \"convergence_points\": these are descriptive fields only. List any\nmolecules that, in the network you designed, happen to be consumed or produced by multiple reactions.\nThey may be empty lists. Do not design the network to fill them.\n\n---\n\n# SECTION 6: LITERATURE-GUIDED SYNTHESIS (when research findings are provided)\n\nWhen research findings are provided, use them as evidence to inform your design:\n\n1. **Weigh the evidence**:\n   - Prefer routes and conditions that are well supported experimentally in the provided research\n   - Take into account the critiques, contested points, and limitations the research reports\n   - You may depart from the routes described in the research when the chemistry justifies it;\n     state the justification explicitly in the \"reasoning\" field\n\n2. **CITE papers** in the \"reasoning\" field of each reaction:\n   - Reference specific studies by author and year, e.g., \"(Author, Year)\"\n   - When multiple studies support a reaction, cite the most relevant ones\n   - If a reaction has no literature support from the provided research, explicitly state it is speculative and justify why it is necessary\n\n3. **ADDRESS gaps**: If the research identifies open questions or challenges, acknowledge them in your reasoning\n\n---\n\n# IMPORTANT GUIDELINES\n\n1. Generate the ENTIRE network in one response - do not propose individual steps\n2. Ensure molecules are defined BEFORE being referenced in reactions\n3. Every molecule ID in reactions must exist in the molecules array\n4. Pathways must trace valid routes through the reaction network\n5. Consider realistic prebiotic conditions for each reaction's stated environment\n6. Include rate-limiting or key bottleneck reactions\n7. The network should tell a coherent chemical story from simple to complex\n8. When deep research findings are provided, cite relevant literature in reaction reasoning fields\n", "user_message": "\nGenerate a SYNTHESIS NETWORK for: Valine\n\nREQUIREMENTS:\n- Generate a bipartite network with molecule nodes and reaction nodes\n- Include 10 distinct pathways to the target\n- Ensure at least 8-15 molecules and 6-12 reactions\n- Choose feedstocks, intermediates, reaction types, and environments on chemical merit alone\n\nOutput the complete network as JSON following the schema in your instructions.\n\n\n=== LITERATURE RESEARCH FINDINGS ===\nThe following deep research report contains peer-reviewed literature on prebiotic synthesis\nof Valine. Use it as evidence to inform your network design.\n\n=== DEEP RESEARCH FINDINGS ===\n# Prebiotic Synthesis of Valine: Routes, Precursors, Catalysts, Environmental Context, and Open Problems\n\n## Executive assessment\n\nValine is a useful stress test for prebiotic synthesis models because it is not merely an \u03b1-amino acid: it requires construction of a **branched C5 carbon skeleton**, HOOC\u2013CH(NH\u2082)\u2013CH(CH\u2083)\u2082. The literature contains several direct demonstrations of abiotic valine formation, but the evidence is uneven. The strongest direct demonstrations fall into five chemically distinct families: **Strecker-type atmospheric/electric-discharge chemistry; ammonium-cyanide/ketone chemistry; high-temperature formaldehyde\u2013ammonia chemistry; shock chemistry; and reductive amination of \u03b1-ketoisovalerate with H\u2082 over metal catalysts**. More recently, **\u03b3-radiolysis of formaldehyde/ammonia solutions in meteorite-parent-body analogues** has also generated valine as a minor product.\n\nThe central conclusion is that **no published route yet closes the entire synthesis from the simplest geochemical feedstocks to valine under one experimentally demonstrated, geochemically well-constrained environment**. The classical Strecker family can, in principle, build the C5 skeleton by adding the carbon of HCN to the C4 aldehyde isobutyraldehyde, and electric-discharge experiments genuinely produce valine, but the strongly reducing atmospheric mixtures that produce aldehydes and HCN most efficiently are difficult to reconcile with evidence that Hadean mantle outgassing was comparatively oxidized. Conversely, the 2024 H\u2082/Ni chemistry of Kaur and co-workers converts the biologically congruent precursor **\u03b1-ketoisovalerate directly to valine under remarkably mild conditions**, but \u03b1-ketoisovalerate was supplied at 30 mM: its abiotic synthesis from CO\u2082, CO, pyruvate, or other simpler feedstocks under the same conditions remains the major missing step.\n\nA useful evidence hierarchy is therefore:\n\n| Route family | Direct valine demonstrated? | Simplest feedstocks experimentally connected to the route | Main bottleneck |\n|---|---:|---|---|\n| Electric discharge \u2192 aldehydes + HCN \u2192 Strecker chemistry | **Yes** | CH\u2084, NH\u2083 or N\u2082, H\u2082O, H\u2082 | Reduced atmosphere; production of enough C4 isobutyraldehyde; low selectivity |\n| NH\u2084CN + ketone network | **Yes** | HCN/NH\u2083 plus acetone | Mechanism of C5 branching is poorly resolved; NH\u2084CN alone does **not** reliably give valine |\n| Heated HCHO + NH\u2083 | **Yes, historically** | Formaldehyde, ammonia, water | Harsh heating; poorly defined bound intermediates; weak valine-specific quantitation/reproducibility |\n| Shock-heated reduced gases | **Yes** | CH\u2084, C\u2082H\u2086, NH\u2083 | Highly reducing gas composition not representative of favored global Hadean atmosphere |\n| CO\u2082/N\u2082 impact chemistry | **Not yet** | CO\u2082/HCO\u2083\u207b, N\u2082, H\u2082O, Fe, Ni, silicates | Experiments stop at simpler amino acids; no demonstrated valine |\n| \u03b1-Ketoisovalerate + NH\u2083 + H\u2082, Ni/Pd/meteorite | **Yes** | H\u2082, NH\u2083 plus preformed C5 \u03b1-ketoacid | Origin and concentration of \u03b1-ketoisovalerate |\n| \u03b1-Ketoacid + CN\u207b/NH\u2083 Bucherer\u2013Bergs chemistry | **Mechanistically plausible; valine not directly shown in the 2022 substrate set** | \u03b1-Ketoacid, CN\u207b, ammonia source, bicarbonate | Requires preformed \u03b1-ketoisovalerate and concentrated cyanide/ammonia; long processing |\n| CO/H\u2082/NH\u2083 Fischer\u2013Tropsch-type mineral chemistry | **No in the principal 2012 study** | CO, H\u2082, NH\u2083 | Produces other amino acids, notably AIB; valine was not reported |\n| \u03b3-Radiolysis of HCHO/NH\u2083 parent-body solutions | **Yes, minor product** | HCHO, NH\u2083, H\u2082O, sometimes CH\u2083OH/glycolaldehyde | Product complexity, dose/concentration assumptions, bound precursors, extraterrestrial rather than terrestrial setting |\n\nThe route classifications above follow the direct experiments of Miller (1957, *Biochimica et Biophysica Acta*), Friedmann & Miller (1969, *Nature*), Bar-Nun, Bar-Nun, Bauer & Sagan (1970, *Science*), Fox & Windsor (1970, *Science*), Ring, Wolman, Friedmann & Miller (1972, *Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences*), Pizzarello (2012, *Meteoritics & Planetary Science*), Pulletikurti, Yadav, Springsteen & Krishnamurthy (2022, *Nature Chemistry*), Kaur et al. (2024, *Chem*), and Ishikawa, Kebukawa, Kobayashi & Yoda (2024, *Life*).\n\n## Known synthesis routes and mechanisms\n\n### Electric-discharge chemistry and the Strecker route\n\nThe most chemically explicit classical route is the one established mechanistically by **Stanley Miller (1957, *Biochimica et Biophysica Acta*)**. Electric discharge through CH\u2084\u2013NH\u2083\u2013H\u2082O\u2013H\u2082 mixtures produced **HCN and aldehydes**, which then entered Strecker chemistry. Miller showed that aminonitrile hydrolysis proceeded quickly enough to account for the amino acids accumulated after roughly a week of sparking. Replacing NH\u2083 with N\u2082 still generated the same general product types, but at lower yield. Addition of ferrous ammonium sulfate did not substantially change product yields, arguing that no Fe/sulfate mineral catalysis was necessary in this experiment.\n\nFor valine specifically, the chemically straightforward Strecker sequence is:\n\n\\[\n(CH_3)_2CHCHO + NH_3 \\rightleftharpoons (CH_3)_2CHCH{=}NH + H_2O\n\\]\n\n\\[\n(CH_3)_2CHCH{=}NH + HCN \\rightarrow (CH_3)_2CHCH(NH_2)CN\n\\]\n\nfollowed by nitrile hydrolysis:\n\n\\[\n(CH_3)_2CHCH(NH_2)CN + 2H_2O \\rightarrow\n(CH_3)_2CHCH(NH_2)CO_2H + NH_3\n\\]\n\nThus the crucial carbon precursor is **2-methylpropanal, or isobutyraldehyde**. Its four-carbon branched skeleton supplies valine C2\u2013C5, while cyanide supplies the carboxyl carbon. This is an important point for network design: making HCN is not enough; an efficient prebiotic valine network also has to generate a significant pool of **branched C4 aldehyde**.\n\nDirect confirmation that such discharge chemistry reaches valine came from **Ring, Wolman, Friedmann & Miller (1972, *PNAS*)**. They discharged a mixture of methane, nitrogen and water containing traces of ammonia and identified, using ion-exchange chromatography together with GC\u2013MS, glycine, alanine, \u03b1-aminobutyric acid, \u03b1-aminoisobutyric acid, **valine**, norvaline, isovaline, leucine, isoleucine, alloisoleucine, norleucine and several polar amino acids. The broad isomer distribution is significant: the experiment demonstrates chemical accessibility but **poor structural selectivity** for the biological amino-acid set.\n\nThe main environmental objection is now substantial. **Trail, Watson & Tailby (2011, *Nature*)** used Ce redox systematics in Hadean zircons to infer mantle-derived magmas near the fayalite\u2013magnetite\u2013quartz redox buffer by about 4.35 Ga. Such outgassing is predicted to favor H\u2082O, CO\u2082, SO\u2082 and N\u2082 rather than the CH\u2084-, NH\u2083- and H\u2082-rich mixture most favorable to Miller-type amino-acid synthesis. This does not prove that strongly reducing local or transient environments never existed, but it makes a globally persistent Miller-style atmosphere difficult to defend geochemically.\n\nAttempts to extend spark chemistry to more neutral CO\u2082/N\u2082 atmospheres illustrate the problem. **Cleaves, Chalmers, Lazcano, Miller & Bada (2008, *Origins of Life and Evolution of Biospheres*)** found that amino-acid recovery from CO\u2082/N\u2082 discharge mixtures could be raised considerably by controlling pH and suppressing oxidative destruction during work-up. In experiments with approximately 0.13 bar CO\u2082 and 0.13 bar N\u2082, CaCO\u2083 buffering raised total amino-acid recovery relative to unbuffered experiments. However, the published product set emphasized glycine, alanine, \u03b2-alanine, AIB, aspartate, glutamate and serine rather than valine. Moreover, **Kuwahara, Eto, Kawamoto, Kurihara, Kaneko, Obayashi & Kobayashi (2012, *Origins of Life and Evolution of Biospheres*)** used isotopic tests to show that ascorbate, which had been used as an oxidation inhibitor in some such experiments, could itself contribute carbon to apparent amino-acid products. Thus neutral-atmosphere spark chemistry remains relevant for simpler precursors, but **it is not currently a strong direct experimental route to valine**.\n\n### Ammonium cyanide plus ketones\n\n**Nadav Friedmann & Stanley Miller (1969, *Nature*)** directly revisited reports that polymerized ammonium cyanide produced the branched protein amino acids. Their result is unusually important because it contains both a positive result and a published refutation: they **could not detect valine, isoleucine or leucine from NH\u2084CN polymerization alone** when more stringent separation was used. Earlier identifications had relied primarily on amino-acid-analyzer elution positions, where non-protein amino acids can co-elute with valine or isoleucine.\n\nWhen **acetone or methyl ethyl ketone was added to NH\u2084CN**, however, Friedmann and Miller obtained significant quantities of valine and, depending on the ketone, alloisoleucine and isoleucine. This therefore constitutes a direct prebiotic valine synthesis route from a cyanide/ammonia pool plus simple ketones.\n\nMechanistically, this route is more problematic than the ordinary aldehyde Strecker pathway. A simple Strecker reaction of acetone, NH\u2083 and HCN gives **\u03b1-aminoisobutyric acid**, a C4 amino acid, not C5 valine. Consequently, the reported appearance of valine from acetone/NH\u2084CN must involve additional carbon\u2013carbon chemistry in the cyanide/ketone reaction network. Friedmann and Miller established the product much more securely than earlier NH\u2084CN work, but they did not establish a unique elementary mechanism for the C5 branch. This makes the route experimentally real but **mechanistically underconstrained for network reconstruction**.\n\nThe study also supplies one of the clearest historical warnings for the valine literature: **NH\u2084CN polymerization by itself should not be counted as an established valine synthesis**. The most careful direct re-investigation found that earlier assignments were probably chromatographic false positives.\n\n### Heated formaldehyde\u2013ammonia chemistry\n\nA chemically very different route was reported by **Sidney Fox & Charles Windsor (1970, *Science*)**. Heating formaldehyde with ammonia generated a complex product which, after hydrolysis, yielded aspartate, glutamate, serine, proline, **valine**, glycine and alanine; glycine was the dominant amino acid. Later experimental literature describing the original procedure reports heating at approximately **185 \u00b0C**, illustrating that this was a substantially hotter process than ordinary aqueous surface chemistry.\n\nThis is not best understood as an ordinary Strecker reaction because HCN and a discrete valine aldehyde were not supplied. The experimentally observed amino acids appeared importantly after hydrolysis, pointing to **nitrogen-containing oligomers or other hydrolysable bound precursors** rather than to a clean solution of free amino acids. The detailed route from C1 formaldehyde to a branched C5 valine skeleton was never resolved at an elementary-mechanism level. Modern work generally treats related HCHO/NH\u2083 chemistry as a nitrogen-containing, formose-like condensation network rather than a single selective synthesis.\n\nModern parent-body simulations expose a major reproducibility problem for valine in this chemistry. **Yoko Kebukawa and co-workers (2017, *Science Advances*)** heated formaldehyde, glycolaldehyde, ammonia and water at **90\u2013250 \u00b0C for 72 h**, producing complex insoluble organic matter and numerous amino-acid precursors. Their analytical standard included valine, but the valine recovered from experiments was detected only as the **L enantiomer**, unlike genuinely abiotic products that were racemic; the authors therefore regarded the apparent valine as probable biological contamination rather than evidence of synthesis.\n\nThat result does not disprove Fox & Windsor's 1970 observation, because the recipes differ substantially, but it means that **thermal HCHO/NH\u2083 chemistry should not be treated as a robust, reproducibly quantified modern valine pathway**. It does robustly generate simpler amino acids and hydrolysable organic material; the C5 branched products remain much less secure.\n\n### Radiation-driven formaldehyde\u2013ammonia chemistry in small bodies\n\nA more recent variant uses ionizing radiation rather than heat alone. **Yoko Kebukawa (2022, *ACS Central Science*)** showed that \u03b3 irradiation of aqueous formaldehyde and ammonia mixtures produced multiple amino acids after hydrolysis. Amino-acid yield scaled approximately linearly with accumulated \u03b3 dose and was comparatively insensitive to dose rate, which matters because the proposed natural energy source is \u03b3 radiation associated with radionuclides such as ^26Al in aqueously altered asteroid parent bodies. The estimated natural radiation production was argued to be chemically significant on parent-body timescales.\n\nThe expanded experiments of **Akari Ishikawa, Yoko Kebukawa, Kensei Kobayashi & Isao Yoda (2024, *Life*)** varied the formaldehyde/ammonia/methanol composition and reported **valine among the lower-abundance amino-acid products**, behind alanine, glycine, \u03b2-alanine, \u03b1-aminobutyric acid and several other species. Representative experiments used large integrated \u03b3 doses on the order of tens to ~90 kGy.\n\nMechanistically, this chemistry is more plausibly a **radical-driven reaction network** than a single Strecker event. Radiolysis generates reactive carbon-, oxygen- and nitrogen-centered species; formaldehyde can also form glycolaldehyde and higher carbonyl compounds, which then feed carbon-chain growth. A discrete, experimentally traced sequence from HCHO to the branched C5 valine skeleton has not yet been established, so the route is direct at the product level but not mechanistically closed.\n\nIts environmental relevance is strongest for **aqueously altered carbonaceous-asteroid parent bodies**, not for a particular early-Earth surface environment. It therefore represents a plausible contribution through extraterrestrial synthesis and delivery rather than a demonstrated endogenous terrestrial source.\n\n### Shock synthesis\n\n**A. Bar-Nun, N. Bar-Nun, S. H. Bauer & Carl Sagan (1970, *Science*)** subjected a reduced gas mixture containing approximately **3.3% CH\u2084, 11% C\u2082H\u2086 and 5.6% NH\u2083, with the balance Ar**, to single-pulse shock heating followed by rapid quenching. They directly observed **glycine, alanine, valine and leucine**. The reported overall efficiency for \u03b1-amino-acid production was about **5 \u00d7 10\u00b9\u2070 molecules per erg of shock energy**. The authors argued that rapid quenching could kinetically trap amino acids that would not be favored at the peak equilibrium temperature.\n\nThe mechanism was not resolved into a valine-specific sequence. At shock temperatures, extensive radical fragmentation and recombination occur, followed by chemistry during rapid cooling. In principle, aldehydes, HCN/nitriles and related intermediates can emerge during such processing, but the study established valine production rather than a unique pathway to it. No mineral catalyst was required.\n\nThe principal critique is environmental. The experiment requires substantial reduced carbon and ammonia. The Hadean zircon constraints of Trail, Watson & Tailby favor substantially more oxidized volcanic outgassing, making this particular bulk-gas composition difficult to justify as the persistent global atmosphere.\n\nModern impact experiments have substantially improved the geochemical starting composition, but they have **not yet closed the gap to valine**. **Takeuchi, Furukawa, Kobayashi, Sekine, Terada & Kakegawa (2020, *Scientific Reports*)** shocked mixtures of Fe, Ni, forsterite, water, bicarbonate/CO\u2082 and N\u2082, designed to represent an Fe-bearing impactor striking an ocean beneath a CO\u2082/N\u2082 atmosphere. Their experiments used an impact velocity of about **0.9 km s\u207b\u00b9**, pressure of about **7 GPa for 0.7 \u03bcs**, a shock-compression temperature near **300 \u00b0C**, and estimated post-shock temperatures around **1500 \u00b0C**. Isotopic labeling confirmed abiotic amino-acid formation. Glycine and alanine were prominent; additional low-molecular-weight amino acids appeared when ammonia was supplied, but **valine was not demonstrated**.\n\nThis creates an instructive contrast: **the old reduced-gas shock experiment reaches valine; the newer geochemically more conservative CO\u2082/N\u2082/Fe impact experiment does not**. Impact chemistry is therefore a credible source of valine precursors and simpler amino acids, but the direct valine evidence still depends on the less geochemically favored reduced gas mixture.\n\n### Reductive amination of \u03b1-ketoisovalerate with hydrogen\n\nThe clearest modern direct conversion of a specifically valine-like metabolic precursor is the work of **Harpreet Kaur, Sophia Rauscher, Emilie Werner, Youngdong Song, Jing Yi, Wahnyalo Kaz\u00f6ne, William Martin, Harun T\u00fcys\u00fcz & Joseph Moran (2024, *Chem*)**. They examined six biological \u03b1-ketoacids, including **\u03b1-ketoisovalerate**, under H\u2082 with ammonia and heterogeneous metal catalysts. \u03b1-Ketoisovalerate gave valine together with its competing carbonyl-reduction product, 2-hydroxyisovalerate.\n\nThe net valine reaction is simple:\n\n\\[\n(CH_3)_2CHCOCO_2H + NH_3 + H_2\n\\rightarrow\n(CH_3)_2CHCH(NH_2)CO_2H + H_2O\n\\]\n\nMechanistically, ammonia reacts with the \u03b1-keto carbonyl to form an imine/iminium-type intermediate, followed by surface-mediated hydrogenation. The competing reaction is direct hydrogenation of the carbonyl to the corresponding \u03b1-hydroxy acid.\n\nThe standard valine experiment used **30 mM \u03b1-ketoisovalerate**, five equivalents NH\u2084HCO\u2083, **5 bar H\u2082**, water at **pH 8**, **22 \u00b0C**, and **72 h**. Three catalysts worked: Pd/C containing 1 mol% Pd relative to substrate, Ni/SiO\u2082\u2013Al\u2082O\u2083 containing 10 mol% Ni, and 3 mg of ground **Aletai iron meteorite** containing substantial Ni. No reduction products were seen in catalyst-free controls.\n\nFor \u03b1-ketoisovalerate specifically, Figure 2 of Kaur et al. gives unusually useful quantitative data. The reported combined amino-acid plus hydroxy-acid yields and amino:hydroxy ratios were:\n\n| Catalyst | Combined valine + 2-hydroxyisovalerate yield | Valine:hydroxy-acid ratio | Approximate valine yield inferred from the figure |\n|---|---:|---:|---:|\n| Ni/SiO\u2082\u2013Al\u2082O\u2083 | 20% | 34:66 | **~6.8%** |\n| Pd/C | 77% | 83:17 | **~64%** |\n| Aletai meteorite | 74% | 18:82 | **~13%** |\n\nThe last column is an arithmetic inference from the published combined yield and product ratio, not a separately reported value. The striking point is the trade-off in plausibility versus selectivity: **Pd is much more amino-acid selective, whereas Ni and meteoritic metal are geochemically easier to motivate but favor substantial hydroxy-acid formation**.\n\nThe same study found that the chemistry can function at ammonium concentrations from **6 to 150 mM**. It also linked reductive amination to H\u2082-driven reductive-aldol chemistry involving glyoxylate, oxaloacetate and other \u03b1-ketoacids. Some aldol steps occur even without catalyst, whereas the critical hydrogenation steps require the metal catalyst. The paper notes that hydrothermal fluids can contain approximately **1\u201315 mmol kg\u207b\u00b9 dissolved H\u2082**, making the reductant geochemically attractive in serpentinization-associated systems.\n\nThis is therefore one of the strongest **final-step** valine syntheses in the literature, but it is not yet a de novo valine synthesis from CO\u2082/H\u2082. The experiment **supplied \u03b1-ketoisovalerate**. The coupled reaction network generated \u03b1-ketoglutarate and other \u03b1-ketoacids from simpler precursors but did not establish a pathway from CO\u2082, pyruvate or glyoxylate all the way to \u03b1-ketoisovalerate. In modern biochemistry, construction of the valine skeleton from pyruvate involves a highly specific branched-chain pathway; an experimentally validated nonenzymatic equivalent remains missing.\n\n### Bucherer\u2013Bergs chemistry from \u03b1-ketoacids\n\n**Sunil Pulletikurti, Mahipal Yadav, Greg Springsteen & Ramanarayanan Krishnamurthy (2022, *Nature Chemistry*)** demonstrated a general alternative in which \u03b1-ketoacids react with cyanide and ammonia sources through **Bucherer\u2013Bergs-type chemistry** to produce the corresponding \u03b1-amino acids. Their mechanistic sequence goes through cyanide addition/amino-nitrile chemistry, cyclization to a **hydantoin**, hydrolysis to an N-carbamoyl amino acid, and finally amino-acid formation.\n\nUnder their optimized general procedure, an \u03b1-ketoacid was used at roughly **0.35 M**, with 2 equivalents of NH\u2084Cl or diamidophosphate, 1.2 equivalents NaCN, and **0.5 M bicarbonate at pH 8.5**. The mixture was held at room temperature for **21 days** and then heated at **80 \u00b0C for 14 days** to complete amino-acid formation. Their supporting information shows explicitly that hydantoin hydrolysis can proceed through an N-carbamoyl amino acid and that ammonia-source chemistry affects the hydrolysis.\n\nFor valine-network design, the obvious substrate is **\u03b1-ketoisovalerate**, which would be expected to give an isopropyl-substituted hydantoin and ultimately valine. However, the experimental substrate set documented in the 2022 paper concentrates on pyruvate, \u03b1-ketoglutarate, oxaloacetate and glyoxylate; **the paper does not provide a direct \u03b1-ketoisovalerate-to-valine experiment**. The pathway is therefore a well-supported reaction class that is mechanistically transferable to valine, but it should not be counted as a direct valine demonstration from that study.\n\nIts principal prebiotic criticisms are the comparatively high substrate/buffer concentrations, long reaction periods, requirement for cyanide plus a concentrated ammonia source, and\u2014again\u2014the prior availability of \u03b1-ketoisovalerate. Kaur et al. explicitly contrasted their H\u2082 chemistry with Bucherer\u2013Bergs chemistry, noting both its use of cyanide and its two-stage temperature protocol.\n\n## Key intermediates, precursors, catalysts, and kinetic constraints\n\nFor network-design purposes, the literature reduces to two particularly important **valine-specific carbon skeletons**.\n\nThe first is **isobutyraldehyde (2-methylpropanal)**. A Strecker route needs this C4 branched aldehyde plus HCN and ammonia. The aldehyde carbon becomes valine's \u03b1 carbon and the cyanide carbon becomes the carboxyl carbon. The immediate intermediate is **2-amino-3-methylbutanenitrile**, whose hydrolysis yields valine. HCN and the imine/aminonitrile intermediates therefore matter kinetically as much as the aldehyde itself. Miller's 1957 experiments showed that aminonitrile hydrolysis rates were sufficient to account for amino-acid accumulation on approximately week-long discharge timescales.\n\nThe second is **\u03b1-ketoisovalerate, 3-methyl-2-oxobutanoate**. It already contains all five carbons in the correct topology; only the \u03b1-keto group must be converted into an \u03b1-amino group. It is the direct precursor in the Kaur H\u2082/Ni/Pd route and the obvious substrate for a valine-specific Bucherer\u2013Bergs pathway. This makes \u03b1-ketoisovalerate particularly attractive for metabolism-congruent models, but also shifts nearly the entire carbon-skeleton problem upstream.\n\nThe major catalytic evidence can be summarized as follows.\n\n| Reaction | Catalyst/surface experimentally tested | Requirement |\n|---|---|---|\n| Spark generation of HCN/aldehydes and subsequent Strecker chemistry | No mineral catalyst required; Fe ammonium sulfate gave no important enhancement in Miller's experiment | Electrical energy; reducing feedstock mixture |\n| NH\u2084CN + ketone valine network | No mineral catalyst reported as required | Cyanide/ammonia chemistry plus ketone |\n| Heated HCHO/NH\u2083 route | No mineral catalyst required in original Fox\u2013Windsor experiment | Strong heating, followed by hydrolysis |\n| Reduced-gas shock route | No heterogeneous catalyst required | Shock heating and rapid quench |\n| \u03b1-Ketoisovalerate + H\u2082/NH\u2083 | Ni/SiO\u2082\u2013Al\u2082O\u2083, Pd/C, Aletai meteorite | **Catalyst essential** under tested conditions |\n| General \u03b1-ketoacid reductive amination | Fresh FeS or Fe(OH)\u2082 in Huber\u2013W\u00e4chtersh\u00e4user experiments | Strongly surface/state dependent |\n| Photochemical \u03b1-ketoacid amination | ZnS/sphalerite in Wang et al. | UV illumination and electron donor/hole-scavenging chemistry |\n| FTT-type CO/H\u2082/NH\u2083 chemistry | Fe/Ni meteorite powder, magnetite, Murchison material; bentonite much less active | 370 \u00b0C catalytic surface chemistry |\n\nThe first four rows follow Miller (1957), Friedmann & Miller (1969), Fox & Windsor (1970), and Bar-Nun et al. (1970); the H\u2082 metal-catalysis result is from Kaur et al. (2024).\n\nTwo older mineral studies are important mechanistically even though they did **not directly make valine**. **Claudia Huber & G\u00fcnter W\u00e4chtersh\u00e4user (2003, *Tetrahedron Letters*)** showed that freshly precipitated FeS and Fe(OH)\u2082 can promote reductive amination of several \u03b1-ketoacids with ammonium salts. Their experiments produced alanine, glutamate, phenylalanine and tyrosine, with maximum yields for several systems of tens of mol%; the reaction was highly sensitive to the physical state of FeS, pH and ionic strength. Optimal pH with ammonia was around **8.8\u20139.3**, close to the NH\u2084\u207a/NH\u2083 pK\u2090; elevated ionic strength suppressed the reaction. At 100 \u00b0C the tested tyrosine-forming system became fast on approximately hourly timescales, whereas reaction could still occur slowly at 4 \u00b0C. No \u03b1-ketoisovalerate/valine experiment was reported, so this work is evidence for a *reaction class*, not for valine itself.\n\nSimilarly, **Wei Wang, Qiliang Li, Bin Yang, Xiaoyang Liu, Yanqiang Yang & Wenhui Su (2012, *Chemical Communications*)** demonstrated reversible photochemical amination of four reverse-TCA-related \u03b1-ketoacids on irradiated ZnS. Their experiments used an oxygen-free ZnS suspension, pH around 9 and a Hg\u2013Xe source; glutamate yield was only about 0.5% after four hours without a sulfite hole scavenger and increased when the surface and photoredox conditions were optimized. \u03b1-Ketoisovalerate was not one of the substrates, so ZnS is a plausible catalyst family to test for valine chemistry rather than an existing direct demonstration.\n\nThe **Fischer\u2013Tropsch-type framework** deserves the same caution. **Sandra Pizzarello (2012, *Meteoritics & Planetary Science*)** reacted CO:H\u2082:NH\u2083 in a 1:1:1 gas mixture over meteorite/mineral powders at about **370 \u00b0C for 24 h**. Fe/Ni meteorite powder produced glycine, alanine, \u03b1-aminobutyric acid and smaller amounts of higher homologues; magnetite strongly favored the branched non-protein amino acid **\u03b1-aminoisobutyric acid**, reaching about 540 nmol g\u207b\u00b9 catalyst, and also yielded ~1 nmol g\u207b\u00b9 isovaline. **Valine itself was not reported in the product table.** Bentonite was a poor catalyst. FTT chemistry therefore provides experimentally important CO/H\u2082/NH\u2083 carbon\u2013nitrogen chemistry but should not currently be listed as a demonstrated valine route.\n\nThe valine literature contains surprisingly little **valine-specific equilibrium thermodynamics** under proposed prebiotic conditions. Most constraints are kinetic/empirical: week-scale aminonitrile hydrolysis in Miller chemistry; ultrafast high-temperature formation followed by quenching in shock chemistry; 72-h hydrogenation at 22 \u00b0C for Kaur's route; weeks-long formation/hydrolysis in the Bucherer\u2013Bergs system; and approximately linear accumulated-dose dependence in \u03b3-radiolysis. Thus, for synthesis-network modeling, experimentally determined rate constants, activation parameters and competing decomposition rates for **isobutyraldehyde, valine aminonitrile and \u03b1-ketoisovalerate under realistic saline/mineral conditions are major missing datasets**.\n\n## Environmental context and geochemical plausibility\n\n### Atmosphere and surface waters\n\nClassical Miller\u2013Strecker chemistry has the strongest historical evidence for direct synthesis from very small molecules, but its environmental support is weakest at the level of a **globally reducing atmosphere**. Trail, Watson & Tailby's Hadean zircon measurements imply mantle oxidation states similar to later terrestrial mantle by ~4.35 Ga, which favors outgassing of H\u2082O, CO\u2082, SO\u2082 and N\u2082 rather than large steady-state CH\u2084 and NH\u2083 abundances. The key environmental question is therefore not whether electrical discharge can make valine\u2014it can\u2014but whether early Earth could repeatedly create **local mixtures rich enough in reduced C and N to produce the necessary HCN and branched C4 aldehydes**.\n\nNeutral CO\u2082/N\u2082 spark experiments demonstrate that some amino-acid chemistry remains possible under more oxidized atmospheric assumptions, particularly when solution pH and oxidative work-up chemistry are controlled, but they have not supplied comparable direct evidence for valine. The later ascorbate-contamination result of Kuwahara et al. further weakens claims of very high amino-acid yields in some neutral-atmosphere experiments.\n\n### Hydrothermal and serpentinization systems\n\nThe **Kaur et al. H\u2082/Ni chemistry** is especially attractive environmentally because it works at 22 \u00b0C and pH ~8 rather than requiring hydrothermal temperatures. H\u2082 is continuously generated by water\u2013rock reactions such as serpentinization, and the authors note measured hydrothermal-fluid H\u2082 values of roughly 1\u201315 mmol kg\u207b\u00b9. Ni-bearing metal/mineral phases and meteoritic material offer plausible heterogeneous catalysts.\n\nThe difficulty is upstream carbon chemistry. Hydrothermal plausibility of H\u2082 and Ni does **not** establish a hydrothermal source of 30 mM \u03b1-ketoisovalerate. Kaur et al.'s coupled H\u2082 network makes several ketoacids from oxaloacetate/glyoxylate chemistry, but the valine-specific branched ketoacid was externally supplied. Thus the route currently has strong environmental support for the **reductive-amination step**, not for the complete valine synthesis.\n\nFeS/Fe(OH)\u2082 reductive-amination chemistry is often associated with hydrothermal/mineral-surface models, but Huber & W\u00e4chtersh\u00e4user's own results caution against treating \u201cFeS\u201d as a generic catalytic label: freshly precipitated material worked substantially differently from other preparations, excess sulfide could suppress activity, increased ionic strength reduced yields, and several alternative metal sulfides failed. Those dependencies need to be confronted explicitly when extrapolating to seawater or vent fluids.\n\n### Impact environments\n\nImpact chemistry is geochemically attractive because it supplies both extreme energy and reducing power from Fe/Ni projectiles without demanding a globally reducing atmosphere. Takeuchi et al.'s 2020 ^13C-labeled experiment demonstrates this principle directly with CO\u2082/HCO\u2083\u207b, N\u2082, water and meteoritic metals. But the realistic-feedstock experiment does **not yet reach valine**, whereas Bar-Nun et al.'s direct valine synthesis starts with methane, ethane and ammonia already in the gas phase.\n\nThus \u201cimpact synthesis of valine\u201d currently joins two pieces of evidence that have not been experimentally unified: old direct valine synthesis under a strongly reduced gas mixture, and modern geochemically motivated synthesis of simpler amino acids from CO\u2082/N\u2082/Fe/Ni. Demonstrating valine in a ^13C/^15N-labeled CO\u2082/N\u2082 impact experiment would materially strengthen this framework.\n\n### Meteorite parent bodies\n\nRadiolytic and hydrothermal formaldehyde/ammonia chemistry has perhaps the clearest environmental case for an **extraterrestrial** contribution. Carbonaceous parent bodies experienced aqueous alteration, and ^26Al provides both heat and ionizing radiation. Kebukawa's \u03b3-radiolysis experiments showed dose-dependent amino-acid synthesis, while Ishikawa et al. subsequently included valine among the minor products.\n\nThe caveat is that amino acids are generated as members of large, low-selectivity product suites, frequently after acid hydrolysis of complex precursor material. The concentration of formaldehyde, ammonia and related small organics in actual parent-body pore solutions, the extent of transport and destruction, and the quantitative contribution of \u03b3 radiation relative to ordinary thermal chemistry remain model-dependent. The 2017 Kebukawa hydrothermal experiment is particularly important as an analytical warning because its apparent valine was L-only and was judged likely contamination.\n\n## Competing frameworks, evidence against them, and unresolved bottlenecks\n\nThe strongest general criticism that applies across the field is **precursor displacement**: a route can solve the final C\u2013N bond formation while moving the harder carbon-skeleton problem upstream. The 2024 Ni/H\u2082 route is the clearest example. Once \u03b1-ketoisovalerate exists, valine formation at 22 \u00b0C is straightforward. But \u03b1-ketoisovalerate is already almost valine; the experimentally unresolved problem is the abiotic creation of its branched C5 skeleton in appreciable concentration from simpler C1\u2013C3 compounds.\n\nThe converse problem affects Strecker chemistry. Its advantage is that it **actually constructs the extra carbon** of the amino-acid carboxyl group from HCN, and electric discharges can generate HCN and carbonyl compounds from small gases. But valine specifically demands isobutyraldehyde. Producing a broad soup of C1\u2013C4 aldehydes is chemically easy compared with generating the branched aldehyde at a concentration high enough for valine to compete with glycine, alanine, AIB, norvaline and numerous non-protein isomers. Ring et al.'s broad product distribution illustrates that selectivity problem experimentally.\n\nThe **NH\u2084CN/acetone route** has a particularly unresolved carbon-accounting problem. Because ordinary Strecker chemistry of acetone should lead to AIB rather than valine, direct valine formation implies secondary C\u2013C transformations in the cyanide polymerization network. Friedmann & Miller established the product, but the detailed sequence and its yield dependence on cyanide, ketone concentration, pH and temperature have never been developed into a modern isotope-resolved mechanism. Their failure to reproduce valine from NH\u2084CN alone also invalidates the simplest version of older cyanide-polymer claims.\n\nThe **formaldehyde/ammonia route** has an analogous mechanistic gap. It starts from admirably simple C1/N1 feedstocks but must execute several selective C\u2013C couplings to reach a branched C5 skeleton. Fox & Windsor demonstrated valine among hydrolysis products, yet modern HCHO/NH\u2083 experiments yield complex polymeric material and broad amino-acid suites, and Kebukawa et al.'s hydrothermal analogue did not produce analytically secure abiotic valine. The exact branch-forming sequence remains unknown.\n\nFor **shock chemistry**, the principal controversy is geochemical rather than chemical. Valine is demonstrably made by shock-heating a CH\u2084/C\u2082H\u2086/NH\u2083 mixture, but Hadean mantle redox constraints do not support that mixture as a general background atmosphere. More realistic CO\u2082/N\u2082 impact chemistry works, but so far only for simpler amino acids.\n\nThe **Bucherer\u2013Bergs \u03b1-ketoacid route** is chemically convincing but carries three distinct relevance questions: whether \u03b1-ketoisovalerate was available; whether cyanide and ammonia could coexist at useful concentrations; and whether the long room-temperature plus 80 \u00b0C processing sequence could occur without destroying other network components. Pulletikurti et al.'s optimized experiments used 0.5 M bicarbonate and comparatively concentrated substrates, making environmental concentration mechanisms an essential part of any proposed setting.\n\nThe **FTT framework** currently should not be promoted as a valine synthesis. Pizzarello's 2012 experiment is valuable because it uses exceptionally simple CO/H\u2082/NH\u2083 feedstocks and geologically relevant catalytic materials, but its 370 \u00b0C product suite contains AIB, norvaline and isovaline rather than valine. The strong production of AIB by magnetite shows that mineral surfaces can select branching patterns, but the selection is not the biological valine skeleton in that study.\n\nThere is also a broader **congruence-with-biochemistry debate**. Strecker chemistry is efficient and geochemically versatile but has no direct analogue in extant amino-acid biosynthesis. \u03b1-Ketoacid reductive amination is structurally congruent with extant metabolism, which is one reason Kaur et al. emphasize it, but metabolic resemblance by itself does not establish historical continuity. A reaction network is compelling only if the required \u03b1-ketoacid itself can arise from geochemically available starting materials under compatible conditions.\n\nFinally, there is the **chirality problem**. None of the strongest direct routes discussed here establishes a robust mechanism for generating protein-level L-valine enantiomeric excess from achiral feedstocks. Indeed, Kebukawa et al.'s observation of exclusively L-valine in an otherwise abiotic experiment was interpreted as a warning sign for contamination rather than as spontaneous enantioselection. For synthesis-network design, valine production and valine homochirality should therefore be treated as separate problems unless an explicit symmetry-breaking mechanism is demonstrated.\n\n## Implications for synthesis-network design\n\nThe most productive network representation is to treat valine as having **two convergent immediate-precursor nodes**:\n\n**Aldehyde branch**\n\\[\n\\mathrm{CH_4/CO/CO_2 \\rightarrow C_1-C_3\\ carbonyls \\rightarrow\nisobutyraldehyde}\n\\]\n\\[\n\\mathrm{isobutyraldehyde + NH_3 + HCN \\rightarrow\nvaline\\ aminonitrile \\rightarrow valine}\n\\]\n\nand\n\n**\u03b1-ketoacid branch**\n\\[\n\\mathrm{small\\ carbonyls/pyruvate \\rightarrow branched\\ C_5\\ chemistry\n\\rightarrow \\alpha\\!-\\!ketoisovalerate}\n\\]\n\\[\n\\mathrm{\\alpha\\!-\\!ketoisovalerate + NH_3 + H_2\n\\xrightarrow[22\\,^\\circ C,\\ pH\\sim8]{Ni/Pd} valine}\n\\]\n\nThe second arrow in each branch is reasonably well supported; the first arrow is the strategic bottleneck. The Strecker branch has abundant experimental evidence for the general **HCN + aldehyde \u2192 aminonitrile \u2192 amino acid** machinery, but insufficient quantitative work on prebiotic formation of isobutyraldehyde. The \u03b1-ketoacid branch has excellent modern evidence for **\u03b1-ketoisovalerate \u2192 valine**, but currently lacks a comparably convincing abiotic synthesis of \u03b1-ketoisovalerate from pyruvate or simple C1\u2013C3 feedstocks.\n\nFor experimental prioritization, the highest-value missing experiment would therefore not simply be \u201cmake valine again.\u201d It would be an isotope-resolved synthesis of the **branched skeleton**. Particularly discriminating experiments would test whether plausible atmospheric, photochemical, mineral, impact or H\u2082-driven networks can generate ^13C-labeled isobutyraldehyde or \u03b1-ketoisovalerate from CO\u2082/CO/HCHO/pyruvate, followed *in the same reaction system* by capture as valine. The need for this follows directly from the precursor gaps in both the Miller/Ring and Kaur pathways.\n\nA second priority is to quantify competition. In Kaur's \u03b1-ketoisovalerate experiment, geochemically attractive Ni and meteoritic metal make substantial **2-hydroxyisovalerate**, whereas Pd strongly favors valine. A realistic network therefore needs to model carbon partitioning between amino-, hydroxy-, decomposition- and adsorption products, not just report the presence of valine. Likewise, electric-discharge chemistry produces many constitutional isomers and non-protein amino acids.\n\nA third priority is to introduce realistic **salinity, mineral aging and concentration cycling**. Huber & W\u00e4chtersh\u00e4user showed that ionic strength, FeS preparation and sulfide loading can change reductive-amination behavior dramatically, while Pulletikurti et al.'s Bucherer\u2013Bergs chemistry uses much more concentrated bicarbonate, cyanide and organic substrate than would be expected in an unconfined ocean. Evaporation, freezing, adsorption, pore confinement or repeated wet\u2013dry cycling therefore cannot merely be environmental decoration; where high concentrations are required, a quantitative concentration mechanism is part of the chemical hypothesis.\n\nOverall, the **best-supported direct valine-forming chemical step under mild, geochemically interpretable conditions is presently \u03b1-ketoisovalerate reductive amination with H\u2082 over Ni-bearing or meteoritic material**, especially because Kaur et al. directly quantified valine/hydroxy-acid competition at 22 \u00b0C. The **best-supported route that constructs valine from substantially smaller organic molecules remains Strecker/discharge chemistry**, but its strongest demonstrations use reducing atmospheres whose global Hadean relevance is contested. **Parent-body \u03b3 chemistry and reduced-gas shock chemistry independently show that valine can also emerge from much less pathway-specific, energy-rich reaction networks**, though with low selectivity and substantial environmental qualifications. None of these results yet establishes a uniquely favored historical route.\n\n## Primary literature cited\n\n**Miller, Stanley L. (1957), *Biochimica et Biophysica Acta* 23, 480\u2013489, \u201cThe mechanism of synthesis of amino acids by electric discharges.\u201d** Established HCN/aldehyde/aminonitrile Strecker chemistry as the mechanism underlying much of electric-discharge amino-acid synthesis and found no substantial catalytic effect from ferrous ammonium sulfate.\n\n**Friedmann, Nadav & Stanley L. Miller (1969), *Nature* 221, 1152\u20131153, \u201cSynthesis of Valine and Isoleucine in Primitive Earth Conditions.\u201d** Failed to confirm valine from NH\u2084CN polymerization alone but directly obtained valine when acetone was added.\n\n**Bar-Nun, A.; Bar-Nun, N.; Bauer, S. H.; & Sagan, C. (1970), *Science* 168, 470\u2013472, \u201cShock Synthesis of Amino Acids in Simulated Primitive Environments.\u201d** Directly produced glycine, alanine, valine and leucine in a reduced gas mixture exposed to shock heating and rapid quenching.\n\n**Fox, Sidney W. & Charles R. Windsor (1970), *Science* 170, 984\u2013986, \u201cSynthesis of Amino Acids by the Heating of Formaldehyde and Ammonia.\u201d** Reported valine among amino acids obtained after hydrolysis of heated formaldehyde/ammonia reaction products.\n\n**Ring, D.; Wolman, Y.; Friedmann, N.; & Miller, S. L. (1972), *Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences USA* 69, 765\u2013768, \u201cPrebiotic Synthesis of Hydrophobic and Protein Amino Acids.\u201d** Confirmed valine and a broad suite of hydrophobic amino acids by chromatography and GC\u2013MS after electric discharge through methane/nitrogen/water with traces of ammonia.\n\n**Huber, Claudia & G\u00fcnter W\u00e4chtersh\u00e4user (2003), *Tetrahedron Letters* 44, 1695\u20131697, \u201cPrimordial Reductive Amination Revisited.\u201d** Demonstrated FeS/Fe(OH)\u2082-mediated reductive amination of several \u03b1-ketoacids; important mechanistic precedent, but not a direct valine experiment.\n\n**Cleaves, H. James; Chalmers, John H.; Lazcano, Antonio; Miller, Stanley L.; & Bada, Jeffrey L. (2008), *Origins of Life and Evolution of Biospheres* 38, 105\u2013115, \u201cA Reassessment of Prebiotic Organic Synthesis in Neutral Planetary Atmospheres.\u201d** Re-evaluated CO\u2082/N\u2082 discharge chemistry; relevant to atmospheric plausibility but not a direct valine demonstration.\n\n**Trail, Dustin; Watson, E. Bruce; & Tailby, Nicholas D. (2011), *Nature* 480, 79\u201382, \u201cThe Oxidation State of Hadean Magmas and Implications for Early Earth's Atmosphere.\u201d** Provided zircon-based evidence against persistently strongly reducing volcanic outgassing in much of the Hadean.\n\n**Wang, Wei; Li, Qiliang; Yang, Bin; Liu, Xiaoyang; Yang, Yanqiang; & Su, Wenhui (2012), *Chemical Communications* 48, 2146\u20132148, \u201cPhotocatalytic Reversible Amination of \u03b1-Keto Acids on a ZnS Surface: Implications for the Prebiotic Metabolism.\u201d** Demonstrated mineral-photocatalytic \u03b1-ketoacid amination, although not \u03b1-ketoisovalerate/valine.\n\n**Pizzarello, Sandra (2012), *Meteoritics & Planetary Science* 47, 1291\u20131296, \u201cCatalytic Syntheses of Amino Acids and Their Significance for Nebular and Planetary Chemistry.\u201d** Demonstrated CO/H\u2082/NH\u2083 FTT-type amino-acid synthesis on meteoritic and mineral catalysts; notably produced AIB and isovaline but did not report valine.\n\n**Kuwahara, Hideharu; Eto, Midori; Kawamoto, Yukinori; Kurihara, Hironari; Kaneko, Takeo; Obayashi, Yumiko; & Kobayashi, Kensei (2012), *Origins of Life and Evolution of Biospheres* 42, 533\u2013541, \u201cThe Use of Ascorbate as an Oxidation Inhibitor in Prebiotic Amino Acid Synthesis: A Cautionary Note.\u201d** Demonstrated that ascorbate can produce misleading apparent amino-acid signals in neutral-atmosphere synthesis experiments.\n\n**Kebukawa, Yoko et al. (2017), *Science Advances* 3, e1602093, \u201cOne-pot Synthesis of Amino Acid Precursors with Insoluble Organic Matter in Planetesimals with Aqueous Activity.\u201d** Produced extensive amino-acid precursor chemistry from formaldehyde/glycolaldehyde/ammonia but treated observed L-only valine as likely contamination.\n\n**Takeuchi, Y.; Furukawa, Y.; Kobayashi, T.; Sekine, T.; Terada, N.; & Kakegawa, T. (2020), *Scientific Reports* 10, 9220, \u201cImpact-induced amino acid formation on Hadean Earth and Noachian Mars.\u201d** Demonstrated isotopically verified amino-acid formation from CO\u2082/N\u2082/H\u2082O plus Fe/Ni-bearing impact materials, but not valine.\n\n**Kebukawa, Yoko (2022), *ACS Central Science* 8, 1664\u20131671, \u201cGamma-Ray-Induced Amino Acid Formation in Aqueous Small Bodies in the Early Solar System.\u201d** Established dose-dependent amino-acid production by \u03b3 irradiation of aqueous formaldehyde/ammonia mixtures.\n\n**Pulletikurti, Sunil; Yadav, Mahipal; Springsteen, Greg; & Krishnamurthy, Ramanarayanan (2022), *Nature Chemistry* 14, 1142\u20131150, \u201cPrebiotic Synthesis of \u03b1-Amino Acids and Orotate from \u03b1-Ketoacids Potentiates Transition to Extant Metabolic Pathways.\u201d** Established Bucherer\u2013Bergs conversion of several \u03b1-ketoacids to amino acids via cyanide/ammonia chemistry; directly relevant mechanistically to \u03b1-ketoisovalerate but did not itself provide a valine-specific substrate experiment.\n\n**Kaur, Harpreet; Rauscher, Sophia A.; Werner, Emilie; Song, Youngdong; Yi, Jing; Kaz\u00f6ne, Wahnyalo; Martin, William F.; T\u00fcys\u00fcz, Harun; & Moran, Joseph (2024), *Chem* 10, 1528\u20131540, \u201cA Prebiotic Krebs Cycle Analog Generates Amino Acids with H\u2082 and NH\u2083 over Nickel.\u201d** Provides the strongest modern quantitative direct experiment for \u03b1-ketoisovalerate \u2192 valine using H\u2082 and Ni, Pd or meteoritic catalysts under mild aqueous conditions.\n\n**Ishikawa, Akari; Kebukawa, Yoko; Kobayashi, Kensei; & Yoda, Isao (2024), *Life* 14, 103, \u201cGamma-Ray-Induced Amino Acid Formation during Aqueous Alteration in Small Bodies: The Effects of Compositions of Starting Solutions.\u201d** Extended parent-body radiolysis experiments and reported valine among minor amino-acid products.\n=== END DEEP RESEARCH FINDINGS ===\n\n\n\n=== END LITERATURE RESEARCH FINDINGS ===\n\nINSTRUCTIONS FOR LITERATURE-GUIDED SYNTHESIS:\n1. Prefer routes and conditions that are well supported experimentally in the research above.\n2. Weigh the critiques and contested points the research reports; do not adopt a route uncritically.\n3. You may depart from the described routes when the chemistry justifies it; explain why in \"reasoning\".\n4. Cite the supporting paper (author, year) in each reaction's \"reasoning\" field, or mark the reaction as speculative.\n\n\nPREVIOUS FAILED ATTEMPTS - Learn from this feedback:\n============================================================\n\nAttempt 1:\n- Confidence: 0.79\n- Feedback: The network has sufficient nodes, consistent molecule IDs and no isolated subgraphs. All 17 reactions are accepted as atom/charge conserving under the supplied deterministic check; five require explicit coefficients. The unparsable mol_018 InChI is a metadata defect, not a conservation violation, because its stated CO2 formula was checked. Formal balancing does not establish empirical product stoichiometry, particularly for rxn_006 and rxn_007. The network contains a well-supported productive core in rxn_009-rxn_011 and correctly represents rxn_012 as a competing sink; its scores under P5-P7 refer to that side branch, not additional productive steps. Imported alpha-ketoisovalerate is a legitimate starting material, and its unestablished simpler-feedstock origin limits claim scope rather than invalidating these conversions. Whole-network confidence is constrained by the unquantified branched-aldehyde output of rxn_001, unresolved empirical closures, weak modern validation of rxn_007, and untested valine-specific extensions in rxn_013, rxn_016 and rxn_017. Evidence grading aligns substantially with the supplied literature, but reaction-level citations and the thermal-route contamination caveat should be added. Separate environments and catalyst variants are acceptable and receive no numerical bonus or penalty. Environmental confidence is reduced by missing operating parameters and catalyst-state details; transfers need justification only where actually required. P6 is the strongest demonstrated conversion from its declared precursor, not a demonstrated natural end-to-end synthesis. The complete network does not meet the 0.90 approval threshold.\n- Issues: ['The identified aldehyde, imine, aminonitrile and amide form a chemically coherent Strecker route to valine. Miller (1957) supports the general mechanism, and Ring et al. (1972) directly demonstrated discharge-derived valine. However, those findings do not establish the specific isobutyraldehyde output or product proportions represented by rxn_001. Its conservation balance does not resolve that evidential gap. A transient discharge is compatible with a surface setting, but aqueous capture and hydrolysis conditions are not specified sufficiently to establish end-to-end operation.', 'Friedmann and Miller (1969) directly support valine formation from acetone plus ammonium cyanide, so retaining an empirical process node is reasonable. This must not be interpreted as ordinary acetone Strecker chemistry, which gives alpha-aminoisobutyric acid rather than valine. The proposed CO2 coproduct closure is not experimentally established. P2 uses the relatively well-supported HCN output of rxn_001 and does not require its uncertain isobutyraldehyde output, although the shared reaction node remains incompletely characterized.', 'Fox and Windsor (1970) provide historical product-level support, but this is not a resolved direct conversion of formaldehyde and ammonia into free valine. Amino acids were recovered after hydrolysis of complex material. The hypothetical CO2 closure does not establish the branched-carbon mechanism or actual product distribution. Kebukawa et al. (2017) identified apparent valine in a related thermal system as probable contamination; the differing recipe prevents treating that result as a direct refutation, but it substantially limits confidence in generalization. A hydrothermal label alone does not specify the required heating and work-up.', 'Interpreted as rxn_001 supplying HCN followed by rxn_013 through rxn_015, this matches the stated four-step pathway. Pulletikurti et al. (2022) support the reaction class and hydantoin/N-carbamoyl sequence, but not alpha-ketoisovalerate-to-valine specifically. The proposed intermediate identities are chemically reasonable, while substrate-specific cyclization, decarboxylation and hydrolysis performance remain untested. The main environmental gap is achieving and maintaining the required concentrations and long staged processing, not the order or number of settings.', 'Huber and Wachtershauser (2003) provide experimental precedent for ferrous-hydroxide-mediated reductive amination, but not for the valine substrate. The balanced two-iron oxidation supplies an appropriate formal reducing-equivalent inventory without requiring molecular oxygen. Nevertheless, alpha-ketoisovalerate conversion, the assumed FeOOH endpoint and effective mineral surface state are unverified. Freshness, pH, ionic strength and temperature materially affect the cited chemistry, so a generic hydrothermal designation is insufficient.', 'Wang et al. (2012) support ZnS-mediated photochemical amination of other alpha-ketoacids, not alpha-ketoisovalerate. Sulfite oxidation can supply the formal reducing equivalents, and the supplied check establishes conservation without an oxygen requirement. However, valine formation, selectivity, photostability and sulfate production have not been demonstrated for this substrate. An illuminated anoxic surface setting is plausible in principle, but light spectrum, photon flux, catalyst state and solution conditions are missing.']\n\n============================================================\nAddress the above issues in your new network. Do NOT repeat the same mistakes.\n", "raw_response": "{\n  \"target_molecule\": \"Valine\",\n  \"overall_strategy\": \"This precursor-resolved network contains 10 distinct reaction-node sequences: four Strecker processing variants, two aldehyde-derived hydantoin variants, one ketoacid-derived hydantoin hypothesis, and three experimentally tested metal-catalyzed reductive-amination variants. They are not 10 independent mechanisms or 10 demonstrated de novo geochemical syntheses. Isobutyraldehyde and alpha-ketoisovaleric acid are explicitly supplied starting materials because the provided literature does not establish their production from simpler feedstocks under compatible conditions. The strongest directly demonstrated conversions are the three Kaur et al. catalyst variants; valine-specific hydantoin chemistry and alternative Strecker processing conditions remain hypotheses. Cyanohydrin-first paths represent an alternative precursor-capture sequence, not a claim of a separate elementary amination mechanism. Unresolved acetone/cyanide, thermal formaldehyde, shock, and radiolytic product networks are excluded rather than assigned invented coproduct balances. All represented net reactions conserve atoms; inferred coefficients do not represent experimental yields. Neutral parent formulas describe aqueous acid-base components, with protonation and counterions suppressed. All settings are anoxic. Surface reactions require sheltered water or pore residence despite strong incident UV. Biochemical denotes abiotic concentrated solutions or mineral interfaces, not enzymes. Branches are alternative reactors, not a single compatible mixture: cyanide must not enter the metal-hydrogenation reactors because it can poison their catalysts. Competing hydroxy-acid formation is represented separately and is not counted as a productive pathway. No stereoselection is established; the target has unspecified stereochemistry and racemic formation is expected. environment_formed records a representative setting, not an exclusive one.\",\n  \"molecules\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_001\",\n      \"formula\": \"H2O\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"oxidane\",\n      \"common_name\": \"water\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/H2O/h1H2\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_002\",\n      \"formula\": \"H3N\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"azane\",\n      \"common_name\": \"ammonia\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/H3N/h1H3\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_003\",\n      \"formula\": \"CHN\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"hydrogen cyanide\",\n      \"common_name\": \"hydrogen cyanide\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/CHN/c1-2/h1H\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_004\",\n      \"formula\": \"C4H8O\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-methylpropanal\",\n      \"common_name\": \"isobutyraldehyde\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/C4H8O/c1-4(2)3-5/h3-4H,1-2H3\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_005\",\n      \"formula\": \"C4H9N\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-methylpropan-1-imine\",\n      \"common_name\": \"isobutyraldimine\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_006\",\n      \"formula\": \"C5H10N2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-amino-3-methylbutanenitrile\",\n      \"common_name\": \"valine aminonitrile\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"hub_intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_007\",\n      \"formula\": \"C5H9NO\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-hydroxy-3-methylbutanenitrile\",\n      \"common_name\": \"isobutyraldehyde cyanohydrin\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_008\",\n      \"formula\": \"C5H8O3\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"3-methyl-2-oxobutanoic acid\",\n      \"common_name\": \"alpha-ketoisovaleric acid\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/C5H8O3/c1-3(2)4(6)5(7)8/h3H,1-2H3,(H,7,8)\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_009\",\n      \"formula\": \"H2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"dihydrogen\",\n      \"common_name\": \"hydrogen\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/H2/h1H\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_010\",\n      \"formula\": \"C5H11NO2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-amino-3-methylbutanoic acid\",\n      \"common_name\": \"valine\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/C5H11NO2/c1-3(2)4(6)5(7)8/h3-4H,6H2,1-2H3,(H,7,8)\",\n      \"role\": \"target\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Biochemical\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_011\",\n      \"formula\": \"C6H10N2O2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"5-(propan-2-yl)imidazolidine-2,4-dione\",\n      \"common_name\": \"5-isopropylhydantoin\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"hub_intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_012\",\n      \"formula\": \"C6H12N2O3\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-(carbamoylamino)-3-methylbutanoic acid\",\n      \"common_name\": \"N-carbamoylvaline\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_013\",\n      \"formula\": \"CO2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"carbon dioxide\",\n      \"common_name\": \"carbon dioxide\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/CO2/c2-1-3\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_014\",\n      \"formula\": \"C7H10N2O4\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2,5-dioxo-4-(propan-2-yl)imidazolidine-4-carboxylic acid\",\n      \"common_name\": \"carboxylated isopropylhydantoin\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_015\",\n      \"formula\": \"C5H10O3\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-hydroxy-3-methylbutanoic acid\",\n      \"common_name\": \"2-hydroxyisovaleric acid\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Biochemical\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"reactions\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_001\",\n      \"name\": \"Reversible formation of the isobutyraldehyde aldimine\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_004\", \"mol_002\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_005\", \"mol_001\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-320 K; proposed operating window\",\n        \"pressure\": \"approximately 1 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Anoxic sheltered pool or pore water; proposed pH 8.5-9.5 with excess total ammonia. Maintain a liquid phase and limit aldehyde and ammonia volatilization. The imine is a transient equilibrium species, not an assumed isolable reservoir.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Ammonia adds to the aldehyde, giving a carbinolamine that reversibly eliminates water to form an aldimine. Subsequent cyanide capture can pull the equilibrium forward.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Miller (1957) supports aldehyde-ammonia-aminonitrile chemistry as the basis of discharge amino-acid synthesis, and Ring et al. (1972) directly identified valine in discharge products. The proposed operating window is not a measured valine-specific optimum. Water disfavors imine accumulation, so capture must compete with hydrolysis. The branched aldehyde is supplied explicitly; these papers do not establish its environmental production rate or concentration.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_002\",\n      \"name\": \"Cyanide capture of the aldimine\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_005\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_006\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-320 K; proposed operating window\",\n        \"pressure\": \"approximately 1 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Anoxic aqueous phase at proposed pH 8.5-9.5, allowing both ammonia and cyanide nucleophilicity. HCN denotes the total cyanide component after acid-base equilibration. Avoid strongly acidic processing until aminonitrile capture is complete.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Cyanide attacks the imine or iminium carbon and proton transfer gives 2-amino-3-methylbutanenitrile. Cyanide supplies the future carboxyl carbon.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"This is the established Strecker reaction class described by Miller (1957), consistent with the direct valine identification of Ring et al. (1972). The carbon skeleton is explicitly accounted for: the aldehyde contributes four carbons and cyanide contributes one. No mineral catalyst is justified by the supplied experiments. Valine-specific capture rates and competing cyanide-polymerization losses remain unquantified.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_003\",\n      \"name\": \"Alternative cyanohydrin-first capture of isobutyraldehyde\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_004\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_007\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-310 K; proposed test window\",\n        \"pressure\": \"approximately 1 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Anoxic, initially ammonia-poor water containing aldehyde and cyanide; proposed pH 8-9. Cyanohydrin formation is reversible. Sheltered residence reduces photochemical exposure, but environmental retention has not been quantified.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Cyanide adds to the aldehyde carbonyl and protonation gives its cyanohydrin.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Speculative network extension at the valine-specific environmental level. Carbonyl cyanohydrin formation is established chemistry, but the supplied Miller (1957) and Ring et al. (1972) findings do not validate a discrete isobutyraldehyde-cyanohydrin reservoir. This branch tests whether cyanide capture before ammonia enrichment changes precursor retention. It is not counted as a separately demonstrated prebiotic valine mechanism.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_004\",\n      \"name\": \"Net conversion of the cyanohydrin pool to valine aminonitrile\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_007\", \"mol_002\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_006\", \"mol_001\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-320 K; proposed test window\",\n        \"pressure\": \"approximately 1 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Ammonia enrichment of the cyanohydrin-containing water; proposed pH 8.5-9.5. Maintain exchange among cyanohydrin, aldehyde, imine and dissolved cyanide. Neither a conversion time nor a valine-specific yield is established.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Net cyanohydrin-to-aminonitrile conversion through reversible carbonyl/cyanohydrin and carbonyl/imine equilibria followed by cyanide trapping. Direct displacement of hydroxide by ammonia is not asserted.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Speculative valine-specific processing variant of the Strecker chemistry supported generally by Miller (1957). The net transformation is balanced, but the supplied literature does not demonstrate this loading order for the valine precursor. Its mechanistic overlap with rxn_001 and rxn_002 is explicit; it is retained only as an alternative precursor-capture sequence.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_005\",\n      \"name\": \"Acid-promoted aminonitrile hydrolysis to valine\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_006\", \"mol_001\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_010\", \"mol_002\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Aqueous hydronium supplied by local volcanic acidity\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"350-370 K; proposed warm-pool window\",\n        \"pressure\": \"approximately 1 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"hydronium\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Proposed pH 1-3 after aminonitrile accumulation; sustained aqueous residence in an acidic geothermal pool. Track nitrile and amide disappearance rather than assuming a fixed completion time. Ammonia represents the ammonium component at this pH; valine is also protonated.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Acid-promoted nitrile hydration gives a transient amino amide, followed by amide hydrolysis to valine and the ammonia component. The net reaction consumes two waters per aminonitrile.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Miller (1957) showed that aminonitrile hydrolysis could account for amino-acid accumulation during approximately week-long discharge experiments. That supports the reaction class, not completion of this particular precursor at every proposed pH and temperature. The acid window is a design hypothesis requiring kinetic validation. Volcanic acidity supplies a chemically justified agent without adding an arbitrary mineral catalyst.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_006\",\n      \"name\": \"Alternative alkaline aminonitrile hydrolysis to valine\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_006\", \"mol_001\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_010\", \"mol_002\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"350-370 K; proposed test window\",\n        \"pressure\": \"approximately 1 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Proposed pH 10-11 in warm alkaline freshwater or moderately saline pool water. Alkalinity is a solution reagent reservoir, not an assigned mineral catalyst. Products are predominantly valinate and ammonia; neutral-parent formulas include eventual proton equilibration. Residence time and conversion must be measured.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Hydroxide-promoted nitrile hydration and subsequent amide cleavage give valinate, represented by its neutral parent valine.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Speculative operating-condition extension of the hydrolysis chemistry discussed by Miller (1957), not a separately validated valine experiment. Alkaline hydrolysis is chemically possible, but rates at this modest alkalinity may be low and aminonitrile decomposition may compete. This is an alternative processing condition, not an additional carbon-skeleton construction mechanism.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_007\",\n      \"name\": \"Hypothesized carbon-dioxide capture and hydantoin formation\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_006\", \"mol_013\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_011\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"approximately 300 K; proposed implementation\",\n        \"pressure\": \"approximately 1 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Concentrated ammonium/bicarbonate water near pH 8.5. A 0.5 M total inorganic-carbon reservoir and weeks-long residence are reaction-class reference conditions, not a validated valine recipe. CO2 represents the reactive inorganic-carbon component, predominantly bicarbonate in the bulk solution.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Aminonitrile carbamylation, cyclization and rearrangement are represented as a net conversion to 5-isopropylhydantoin. Inorganic carbon supplies the additional hydantoin carbonyl carbon.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Speculative valine-specific extension of Bucherer-Bergs chemistry. Pulletikurti et al. (2022) support aminonitrile/hydantoin chemistry for other alpha-ketoacid systems, but the supplied research does not demonstrate this valine aminonitrile conversion. Hydantoin formation provides a chemically meaningful alternative to direct nitrile hydrolysis; its rate, selectivity and concentration requirements must be tested.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_008\",\n      \"name\": \"Hypothesized hydrolytic opening of isopropylhydantoin\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_011\", \"mol_001\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_012\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"353 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"approximately 1 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Proposed pH approximately 8.5 in concentrated ammonium/bicarbonate solution. Use the 14-day warm phase of the reaction-class reference procedure as an initial observation window, not a guaranteed completion time. Maintain liquid water and monitor ring-opening regioselectivity.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Hydrolysis of a hydantoin amide bond opens the ring to N-carbamoylvaline.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Pulletikurti et al. (2022) support hydantoin-to-N-carbamoyl-amino-acid hydrolysis in their studied systems. Applying it to 5-isopropylhydantoin is speculative in the supplied evidence. Ammonia-source chemistry influences hydrolysis, so the published general temperature schedule cannot establish a valine-specific yield or rate. This is a potential kinetic bottleneck.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_009\",\n      \"name\": \"Hypothesized N-carbamoylvaline hydrolysis\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_012\", \"mol_001\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_010\", \"mol_002\", \"mol_013\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"353 K; proposed initial test temperature\",\n        \"pressure\": \"approximately 1 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Continue the warm aqueous phase and determine whether decarbamoylation occurs near pH 8.5. The literature's nominal 14-day warm phase is a reference for the overall processing stage, not an independently established duration for this step. CO2 and ammonia products equilibrate with bicarbonate and ammonium.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Hydrolytic cleavage of the carbamoyl substituent liberates valine; the removed carbamoyl group ultimately yields carbon dioxide and ammonia.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Speculative valine-specific extrapolation from the N-carbamoyl-amino-acid sequence supported by Pulletikurti et al. (2022). The net coproducts follow carbamoyl hydrolysis rather than an arbitrary closure of an unresolved product mixture. Nevertheless, decarbamoylation under the proposed mild conditions is not demonstrated for this substrate and may prevent the hydantoin branches from yielding appreciable free valine.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_010\",\n      \"name\": \"Hypothesized Bucherer-Bergs capture of alpha-ketoisovalerate\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_008\", \"mol_003\", \"mol_002\", \"mol_013\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_014\", \"mol_001\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"approximately 300 K; proposed room-temperature implementation\",\n        \"pressure\": \"approximately 1 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Reaction-class reference composition: 0.35 M ketoacid, 0.42 M total cyanide, 0.70 M total ammonium/ammonia and 0.5 M bicarbonate at pH 8.5, with an initial 21-day residence. These concentrations were not validated for alpha-ketoisovalerate. From a hypothetical 5 mM ketoacid source, reaching 0.35 M would require at least 70-fold concentration without loss; that source concentration is not established.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"A proposed aminonitrile/carbamate/cyclization sequence forms a carboxylated isopropylhydantoin while retaining the substrate carboxyl group temporarily.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Speculative substrate extension of Pulletikurti et al. (2022), whose documented substrate set does not directly demonstrate alpha-ketoisovalerate-to-valine conversion. The carboxylated intermediate is a mechanistic hypothesis, not a reported valine-specific identification. The supplied branched ketoacid, concentrated cyanide/ammonia, long residence, and simultaneous solute retention are explicit bottlenecks. Evaporation alone does not establish their natural availability.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_011\",\n      \"name\": \"Hypothesized decarboxylation of carboxylated isopropylhydantoin\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_014\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_011\", \"mol_013\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-353 K during the proposed staged processing\",\n        \"pressure\": \"approximately 1 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Monitor carbon dioxide release and hydantoin identity during the room-temperature and warm phases. No substrate-specific decarboxylation rate or preferred temperature is established.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Loss of the substrate-derived carboxyl group followed by proton transfer produces 5-isopropylhydantoin. In this proposed atom mapping, cyanide subsequently supplies the valine carboxyl carbon.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Speculative mechanistic resolution of the alpha-ketoacid Bucherer-Bergs reaction class described by Pulletikurti et al. (2022). It explicitly accounts for the carbon removed when the carboxylated intermediate becomes the ordinary hydantoin, but neither this intermediate nor its decarboxylation has been demonstrated for the valine substrate in the provided research. Isotope tracing is needed to test the proposed atom mapping.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_012\",\n      \"name\": \"Nickel-catalyzed reductive amination of alpha-ketoisovalerate\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_008\", \"mol_002\", \"mol_009\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_010\", \"mol_001\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Biochemical\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Ni/SiO2-Al2O3 with accessible reduced nickel sites\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"295 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"5 bar H2\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Ni/SiO2-Al2O3\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Reported reference conditions: 30 mM alpha-ketoisovalerate, five equivalents NH4HCO3, pH 8, 72 h, and 10 mol% Ni relative to substrate. Use an oxygen-free, cyanide-free reactor. A natural analogue would require a confined H2-rich cool mineral interface; aged nickel sulfide is not assumed equivalent to the tested supported catalyst.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Ammonia forms an imine/iminium-type intermediate with the ketoacid; surface-activated hydrogen reduces it to valine.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Direct valine experiment: Kaur et al. (2024). Their approximately 20% combined amino- and hydroxy-acid yield with a 34:66 ratio implies about 6.8% valine, an arithmetic inference rather than a separately reported yield. Catalyst-free controls did not reduce the substrate. Nickel offers a geochemically motivated active metal, but the engineered support, metal surface state, passivation and supplied 30 mM branched ketoacid prevent this from establishing a natural end-to-end synthesis.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_013\",\n      \"name\": \"Palladium-catalyzed reductive amination benchmark\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_008\", \"mol_002\", \"mol_009\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_010\", \"mol_001\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Biochemical\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Pd/C\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"295 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"5 bar H2\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Pd/C\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Reported reference conditions: 30 mM alpha-ketoisovalerate, five equivalents NH4HCO3, pH 8, 72 h, and 1 mol% Pd relative to substrate. Oxygen and cyanide are excluded. This is a laboratory mineral-interface comparator; naturally available Pd/C is not assumed.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Palladium activates hydrogen and promotes hydrogenation of the ketoacid-derived imine or iminium species.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Direct valine experiment: Kaur et al. (2024). Approximately 77% combined yield and an 83:17 amino:hydroxy ratio imply about 64% valine. This provides the strongest reported selectivity among the three catalysts, but palladium scarcity and the engineered carbon-supported catalyst substantially weaken its prebiotic environmental case. It is retained for its experimentally demonstrated chemistry, not promoted as a demonstrated terrestrial source.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_014\",\n      \"name\": \"Meteoritic-metal reductive amination of alpha-ketoisovalerate\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_008\", \"mol_002\", \"mol_009\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_010\", \"mol_001\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Biochemical\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Ground Aletai iron meteorite containing nickel-bearing metal\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"295 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"5 bar H2\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"ground Aletai iron meteorite\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Reported reference conditions: 30 mM alpha-ketoisovalerate, five equivalents NH4HCO3, pH 8, 72 h, and 3 mg ground meteorite per experimental vial. The supplied report does not specify enough reactor-volume or exposed-metal-area information to scale that loading environmentally. Freshly exposed metal, anoxia and exclusion of cyanide are required assumptions.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Hydrogen activation on reactive meteoritic metal sites enables reduction of an ammonia-derived imine/iminium intermediate.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Direct valine experiment: Kaur et al. (2024). Approximately 74% combined yield and an 18:82 amino:hydroxy ratio imply about 13% valine. Meteoritic metal has a clearer natural provenance than engineered Pd/C, but grinding, accessible nickel sites, corrosion and sulfide passivation matter. The exact catalytic site is not established, and the branched ketoacid remains an imported precursor.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_015\",\n      \"name\": \"Competing nickel-catalyzed ketoacid hydrogenation\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_008\", \"mol_009\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_015\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Biochemical\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Ni/SiO2-Al2O3 with accessible reduced nickel sites\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"295 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"5 bar H2\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Ni/SiO2-Al2O3\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Same reaction mixture as rxn_012: 30 mM ketoacid, five equivalents NH4HCO3, pH 8, 72 h and 10 mol% Ni. This flux competes with rxn_012; it is not a later step in that pathway.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Direct hydrogenation of the keto carbonyl gives 2-hydroxyisovaleric acid instead of valine.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Kaur et al. (2024) directly observed the competing hydroxy acid. The reported combined yield and product ratio imply approximately 13.2% hydroxy-acid yield. Including this branch prevents nickel-mediated substrate reduction from being equated with valine synthesis. This molecule is a terminal competing product in the present graph, despite using the schema's intermediate role.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_016\",\n      \"name\": \"Competing palladium-catalyzed ketoacid hydrogenation\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_008\", \"mol_009\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_015\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Biochemical\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Pd/C\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"295 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"5 bar H2\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Pd/C\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Same reaction mixture as rxn_013: 30 mM ketoacid, five equivalents NH4HCO3, pH 8, 72 h and 1 mol% Pd. This is a competing flux, not part of the productive reaction sequence.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Palladium-catalyzed carbonyl hydrogenation produces the alpha-hydroxy acid.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Kaur et al. (2024) reported a 17% hydroxy-acid share within the approximately 77% combined product yield, implying about 13.1% hydroxy-acid yield. Palladium favors valine more strongly than the other tested catalysts but does not eliminate this sink. Natural catalyst availability remains a separate limitation.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_017\",\n      \"name\": \"Competing meteoritic-metal ketoacid hydrogenation\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_008\", \"mol_009\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_015\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Biochemical\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Ground Aletai iron meteorite containing nickel-bearing metal\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"295 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"5 bar H2\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"ground Aletai iron meteorite\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Same reaction mixture as rxn_014: 30 mM ketoacid, five equivalents NH4HCO3, pH 8, 72 h and 3 mg ground meteorite per reference vial. Environmental scaling and metal-surface aging are unresolved.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Surface-mediated hydrogenation of the keto carbonyl produces 2-hydroxyisovaleric acid.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Kaur et al. (2024) reported an 82% hydroxy-acid share within the approximately 74% combined yield, implying about 60.7% hydroxy-acid yield. This dominant competing flux is essential to evaluating the meteorite route: high total reduction does not imply high valine selectivity. No return pathway from this sink is assumed.\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"pathways\": [\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P1\",\n      \"name\": \"Imine-first Strecker synthesis with acidic hydrolysis\",\n      \"description\": \"Supplied isobutyraldehyde enters the imine/aminonitrile sequence, followed by acidic aqueous hydrolysis. This is a literature-supported reaction family with proposed, rather than experimentally established, valine-specific pool conditions. The branched aldehyde source remains outside the demonstrated network.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_002\", \"rxn_005\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_005\", \"mol_006\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P2\",\n      \"name\": \"Imine-first Strecker synthesis with alkaline hydrolysis\",\n      \"description\": \"The same carbon-skeleton construction as P1 is followed by an alternative alkaline processing stage. This is a condition-resolved graph pathway, not an independent synthesis mechanism. Slow hydrolysis and aminonitrile decomposition are unresolved risks.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_002\", \"rxn_006\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_005\", \"mol_006\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P3\",\n      \"name\": \"Cyanohydrin-first capture with acidic hydrolysis\",\n      \"description\": \"A proposed cyanohydrin capture stage precedes ammonia enrichment and aminonitrile formation. Acidic hydrolysis then yields valine. The cyanohydrin loading order is not directly validated for prebiotic valine and may converge mechanistically on the ordinary imine sequence.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_003\", \"rxn_004\", \"rxn_005\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_007\", \"mol_006\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P4\",\n      \"name\": \"Cyanohydrin-first capture with alkaline hydrolysis\",\n      \"description\": \"Combines the proposed cyanohydrin-first loading sequence with alkaline aminonitrile hydrolysis. It is a distinct operational sequence but carries both the untested precursor-capture assumption and the alkaline-hydrolysis kinetic uncertainty.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_003\", \"rxn_004\", \"rxn_006\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_007\", \"mol_006\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P5\",\n      \"name\": \"Imine-first synthesis through a hydantoin reservoir\",\n      \"description\": \"The aldehyde-derived aminonitrile is diverted into a hypothesized isopropylhydantoin, then hydrolyzed through N-carbamoylvaline. The valine-specific cyclization and final hydrolysis are not established by the supplied literature. Concentrated inorganic carbon and prolonged aqueous processing are required hypotheses.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_002\", \"rxn_007\", \"rxn_008\", \"rxn_009\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_006\", \"mol_011\", \"mol_012\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P6\",\n      \"name\": \"Cyanohydrin-first synthesis through a hydantoin reservoir\",\n      \"description\": \"The alternative cyanohydrin loading sequence feeds the same hypothesized hydantoin and N-carbamoyl intermediates as P5. This is a distinct graph path assembled from explicitly conditional steps, not an additional direct experimental demonstration.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_003\", \"rxn_004\", \"rxn_007\", \"rxn_008\", \"rxn_009\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_007\", \"mol_006\", \"mol_011\", \"mol_012\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P7\",\n      \"name\": \"Alpha-ketoisovalerate Bucherer-Bergs hypothesis\",\n      \"description\": \"Supplied alpha-ketoisovalerate undergoes proposed cyanide/ammonia/inorganic-carbon capture, decarboxylation and hydantoin hydrolysis. Pulletikurti et al. (2022) support the reaction class, not this substrate-specific route. Concentration, atom mapping, decarboxylation and decarbamoylation all require validation.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_010\", \"rxn_011\", \"rxn_008\", \"rxn_009\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_014\", \"mol_011\", \"mol_012\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P8\",\n      \"name\": \"Supported-nickel hydrogen reductive amination\",\n      \"description\": \"Directly demonstrated conversion of supplied alpha-ketoisovalerate to valine under the Kaur et al. conditions. The approximate inferred valine yield is 6.8%. Hydroxy-acid formation competes through rxn_015 and is not included as a productive step. Natural precursor concentration and catalyst equivalence remain unresolved.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_012\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_008\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P9\",\n      \"name\": \"Palladium hydrogen reductive-amination benchmark\",\n      \"description\": \"Directly demonstrated, comparatively selective conversion of supplied alpha-ketoisovalerate, with an approximate inferred valine yield of 64%. This is a laboratory-supported catalytic variant, not a demonstrated naturally available prebiotic system. The competing hydroxy-acid branch is rxn_016.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_013\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_008\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P10\",\n      \"name\": \"Meteoritic-metal hydrogen reductive amination\",\n      \"description\": \"Directly demonstrated conversion over ground Aletai meteorite, with an approximate inferred valine yield of 13%. The catalyst has a natural provenance, but accessible metal area, corrosion and precursor supply are unresolved. Most measured reduction product is hydroxy acid through rxn_017, not valine.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_014\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_008\"]\n    }\n  ],\n  \"hub_molecules\": [\n    \"mol_001\",\n    \"mol_002\",\n    \"mol_003\",\n    \"mol_004\",\n    \"mol_006\",\n    \"mol_008\",\n    \"mol_009\",\n    \"mol_010\",\n    \"mol_011\",\n    \"mol_013\",\n    \"mol_015\"\n  ],\n  \"convergence_points\": [\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_006\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\"rxn_002\", \"rxn_004\"],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\"rxn_005\", \"rxn_006\", \"rxn_007\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_011\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\"rxn_007\", \"rxn_011\"],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\"rxn_008\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_010\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\"rxn_005\", \"rxn_006\", \"rxn_009\", \"rxn_012\", \"rxn_013\", \"rxn_014\"],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": []\n    },\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_015\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\"rxn_015\", \"rxn_016\", \"rxn_017\"],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": []\n    }\n  ]\n}", "parsed_response": {"target_molecule": "Valine", "overall_strategy": "This precursor-resolved network contains 10 distinct reaction-node sequences: four Strecker processing variants, two aldehyde-derived hydantoin variants, one ketoacid-derived hydantoin hypothesis, and three experimentally tested metal-catalyzed reductive-amination variants. They are not 10 independent mechanisms or 10 demonstrated de novo geochemical syntheses. Isobutyraldehyde and alpha-ketoisovaleric acid are explicitly supplied starting materials because the provided literature does not establish their production from simpler feedstocks under compatible conditions. The strongest directly demonstrated conversions are the three Kaur et al. catalyst variants; valine-specific hydantoin chemistry and alternative Strecker processing conditions remain hypotheses. Cyanohydrin-first paths represent an alternative precursor-capture sequence, not a claim of a separate elementary amination mechanism. Unresolved acetone/cyanide, thermal formaldehyde, shock, and radiolytic product networks are excluded rather than assigned invented coproduct balances. All represented net reactions conserve atoms; inferred coefficients do not represent experimental yields. Neutral parent formulas describe aqueous acid-base components, with protonation and counterions suppressed. All settings are anoxic. Surface reactions require sheltered water or pore residence despite strong incident UV. Biochemical denotes abiotic concentrated solutions or mineral interfaces, not enzymes. Branches are alternative reactors, not a single compatible mixture: cyanide must not enter the metal-hydrogenation reactors because it can poison their catalysts. Competing hydroxy-acid formation is represented separately and is not counted as a productive pathway. No stereoselection is established; the target has unspecified stereochemistry and racemic formation is expected. environment_formed records a representative setting, not an exclusive one.", "molecules": [{"id": "mol_001", "formula": "H2O", "iupac_name": "oxidane", "common_name": "water", "inchi": "InChI=1S/H2O/h1H2", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_002", "formula": "H3N", "iupac_name": "azane", "common_name": "ammonia", "inchi": "InChI=1S/H3N/h1H3", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_003", "formula": "CHN", "iupac_name": "hydrogen cyanide", "common_name": "hydrogen cyanide", "inchi": "InChI=1S/CHN/c1-2/h1H", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_004", "formula": "C4H8O", "iupac_name": "2-methylpropanal", "common_name": "isobutyraldehyde", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C4H8O/c1-4(2)3-5/h3-4H,1-2H3", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_005", "formula": "C4H9N", "iupac_name": "2-methylpropan-1-imine", "common_name": "isobutyraldimine", "inchi": null, "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_006", "formula": "C5H10N2", "iupac_name": "2-amino-3-methylbutanenitrile", "common_name": "valine aminonitrile", "inchi": null, "role": "hub_intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_007", "formula": "C5H9NO", "iupac_name": "2-hydroxy-3-methylbutanenitrile", "common_name": "isobutyraldehyde cyanohydrin", "inchi": null, "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_008", "formula": "C5H8O3", "iupac_name": "3-methyl-2-oxobutanoic acid", "common_name": "alpha-ketoisovaleric acid", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C5H8O3/c1-3(2)4(6)5(7)8/h3H,1-2H3,(H,7,8)", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_009", "formula": "H2", "iupac_name": "dihydrogen", "common_name": "hydrogen", "inchi": "InChI=1S/H2/h1H", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_010", "formula": "C5H11NO2", "iupac_name": "2-amino-3-methylbutanoic acid", "common_name": "valine", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C5H11NO2/c1-3(2)4(6)5(7)8/h3-4H,6H2,1-2H3,(H,7,8)", "role": "target", "environment_formed": "Biochemical"}, {"id": "mol_011", "formula": "C6H10N2O2", "iupac_name": "5-(propan-2-yl)imidazolidine-2,4-dione", "common_name": "5-isopropylhydantoin", "inchi": null, "role": "hub_intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_012", "formula": "C6H12N2O3", "iupac_name": "2-(carbamoylamino)-3-methylbutanoic acid", "common_name": "N-carbamoylvaline", "inchi": null, "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_013", "formula": "CO2", "iupac_name": "carbon dioxide", "common_name": "carbon dioxide", "inchi": "InChI=1S/CO2/c2-1-3", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_014", "formula": "C7H10N2O4", "iupac_name": "2,5-dioxo-4-(propan-2-yl)imidazolidine-4-carboxylic acid", "common_name": "carboxylated isopropylhydantoin", "inchi": null, "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_015", "formula": "C5H10O3", "iupac_name": "2-hydroxy-3-methylbutanoic acid", "common_name": "2-hydroxyisovaleric acid", "inchi": null, "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Biochemical"}], "reactions": [{"id": "rxn_001", "name": "Reversible formation of the isobutyraldehyde aldimine", "inputs": ["mol_004", "mol_002"], "outputs": ["mol_005", "mol_001"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-320 K; proposed operating window", "pressure": "approximately 1 bar", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Anoxic sheltered pool or pore water; proposed pH 8.5-9.5 with excess total ammonia. Maintain a liquid phase and limit aldehyde and ammonia volatilization. The imine is a transient equilibrium species, not an assumed isolable reservoir."}, "mechanism": "Ammonia adds to the aldehyde, giving a carbinolamine that reversibly eliminates water to form an aldimine. Subsequent cyanide capture can pull the equilibrium forward.", "reasoning": "Miller (1957) supports aldehyde-ammonia-aminonitrile chemistry as the basis of discharge amino-acid synthesis, and Ring et al. (1972) directly identified valine in discharge products. The proposed operating window is not a measured valine-specific optimum. Water disfavors imine accumulation, so capture must compete with hydrolysis. The branched aldehyde is supplied explicitly; these papers do not establish its environmental production rate or concentration."}, {"id": "rxn_002", "name": "Cyanide capture of the aldimine", "inputs": ["mol_005", "mol_003"], "outputs": ["mol_006"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-320 K; proposed operating window", "pressure": "approximately 1 bar", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Anoxic aqueous phase at proposed pH 8.5-9.5, allowing both ammonia and cyanide nucleophilicity. HCN denotes the total cyanide component after acid-base equilibration. Avoid strongly acidic processing until aminonitrile capture is complete."}, "mechanism": "Cyanide attacks the imine or iminium carbon and proton transfer gives 2-amino-3-methylbutanenitrile. Cyanide supplies the future carboxyl carbon.", "reasoning": "This is the established Strecker reaction class described by Miller (1957), consistent with the direct valine identification of Ring et al. (1972). The carbon skeleton is explicitly accounted for: the aldehyde contributes four carbons and cyanide contributes one. No mineral catalyst is justified by the supplied experiments. Valine-specific capture rates and competing cyanide-polymerization losses remain unquantified."}, {"id": "rxn_003", "name": "Alternative cyanohydrin-first capture of isobutyraldehyde", "inputs": ["mol_004", "mol_003"], "outputs": ["mol_007"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-310 K; proposed test window", "pressure": "approximately 1 bar", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Anoxic, initially ammonia-poor water containing aldehyde and cyanide; proposed pH 8-9. Cyanohydrin formation is reversible. Sheltered residence reduces photochemical exposure, but environmental retention has not been quantified."}, "mechanism": "Cyanide adds to the aldehyde carbonyl and protonation gives its cyanohydrin.", "reasoning": "Speculative network extension at the valine-specific environmental level. Carbonyl cyanohydrin formation is established chemistry, but the supplied Miller (1957) and Ring et al. (1972) findings do not validate a discrete isobutyraldehyde-cyanohydrin reservoir. This branch tests whether cyanide capture before ammonia enrichment changes precursor retention. It is not counted as a separately demonstrated prebiotic valine mechanism."}, {"id": "rxn_004", "name": "Net conversion of the cyanohydrin pool to valine aminonitrile", "inputs": ["mol_007", "mol_002"], "outputs": ["mol_006", "mol_001"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-320 K; proposed test window", "pressure": "approximately 1 bar", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Ammonia enrichment of the cyanohydrin-containing water; proposed pH 8.5-9.5. Maintain exchange among cyanohydrin, aldehyde, imine and dissolved cyanide. Neither a conversion time nor a valine-specific yield is established."}, "mechanism": "Net cyanohydrin-to-aminonitrile conversion through reversible carbonyl/cyanohydrin and carbonyl/imine equilibria followed by cyanide trapping. Direct displacement of hydroxide by ammonia is not asserted.", "reasoning": "Speculative valine-specific processing variant of the Strecker chemistry supported generally by Miller (1957). The net transformation is balanced, but the supplied literature does not demonstrate this loading order for the valine precursor. Its mechanistic overlap with rxn_001 and rxn_002 is explicit; it is retained only as an alternative precursor-capture sequence."}, {"id": "rxn_005", "name": "Acid-promoted aminonitrile hydrolysis to valine", "inputs": ["mol_006", "mol_001"], "outputs": ["mol_010", "mol_002"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["Aqueous hydronium supplied by local volcanic acidity"], "conditions": {"temperature": "350-370 K; proposed warm-pool window", "pressure": "approximately 1 bar", "catalyst": "hydronium", "aqueous": true, "other": "Proposed pH 1-3 after aminonitrile accumulation; sustained aqueous residence in an acidic geothermal pool. Track nitrile and amide disappearance rather than assuming a fixed completion time. Ammonia represents the ammonium component at this pH; valine is also protonated."}, "mechanism": "Acid-promoted nitrile hydration gives a transient amino amide, followed by amide hydrolysis to valine and the ammonia component. The net reaction consumes two waters per aminonitrile.", "reasoning": "Miller (1957) showed that aminonitrile hydrolysis could account for amino-acid accumulation during approximately week-long discharge experiments. That supports the reaction class, not completion of this particular precursor at every proposed pH and temperature. The acid window is a design hypothesis requiring kinetic validation. Volcanic acidity supplies a chemically justified agent without adding an arbitrary mineral catalyst."}, {"id": "rxn_006", "name": "Alternative alkaline aminonitrile hydrolysis to valine", "inputs": ["mol_006", "mol_001"], "outputs": ["mol_010", "mol_002"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "350-370 K; proposed test window", "pressure": "approximately 1 bar", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Proposed pH 10-11 in warm alkaline freshwater or moderately saline pool water. Alkalinity is a solution reagent reservoir, not an assigned mineral catalyst. Products are predominantly valinate and ammonia; neutral-parent formulas include eventual proton equilibration. Residence time and conversion must be measured."}, "mechanism": "Hydroxide-promoted nitrile hydration and subsequent amide cleavage give valinate, represented by its neutral parent valine.", "reasoning": "Speculative operating-condition extension of the hydrolysis chemistry discussed by Miller (1957), not a separately validated valine experiment. Alkaline hydrolysis is chemically possible, but rates at this modest alkalinity may be low and aminonitrile decomposition may compete. This is an alternative processing condition, not an additional carbon-skeleton construction mechanism."}, {"id": "rxn_007", "name": "Hypothesized carbon-dioxide capture and hydantoin formation", "inputs": ["mol_006", "mol_013"], "outputs": ["mol_011"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "approximately 300 K; proposed implementation", "pressure": "approximately 1 bar", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Concentrated ammonium/bicarbonate water near pH 8.5. A 0.5 M total inorganic-carbon reservoir and weeks-long residence are reaction-class reference conditions, not a validated valine recipe. CO2 represents the reactive inorganic-carbon component, predominantly bicarbonate in the bulk solution."}, "mechanism": "Aminonitrile carbamylation, cyclization and rearrangement are represented as a net conversion to 5-isopropylhydantoin. Inorganic carbon supplies the additional hydantoin carbonyl carbon.", "reasoning": "Speculative valine-specific extension of Bucherer-Bergs chemistry. Pulletikurti et al. (2022) support aminonitrile/hydantoin chemistry for other alpha-ketoacid systems, but the supplied research does not demonstrate this valine aminonitrile conversion. Hydantoin formation provides a chemically meaningful alternative to direct nitrile hydrolysis; its rate, selectivity and concentration requirements must be tested."}, {"id": "rxn_008", "name": "Hypothesized hydrolytic opening of isopropylhydantoin", "inputs": ["mol_011", "mol_001"], "outputs": ["mol_012"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "353 K", "pressure": "approximately 1 bar", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Proposed pH approximately 8.5 in concentrated ammonium/bicarbonate solution. Use the 14-day warm phase of the reaction-class reference procedure as an initial observation window, not a guaranteed completion time. Maintain liquid water and monitor ring-opening regioselectivity."}, "mechanism": "Hydrolysis of a hydantoin amide bond opens the ring to N-carbamoylvaline.", "reasoning": "Pulletikurti et al. (2022) support hydantoin-to-N-carbamoyl-amino-acid hydrolysis in their studied systems. Applying it to 5-isopropylhydantoin is speculative in the supplied evidence. Ammonia-source chemistry influences hydrolysis, so the published general temperature schedule cannot establish a valine-specific yield or rate. This is a potential kinetic bottleneck."}, {"id": "rxn_009", "name": "Hypothesized N-carbamoylvaline hydrolysis", "inputs": ["mol_012", "mol_001"], "outputs": ["mol_010", "mol_002", "mol_013"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "353 K; proposed initial test temperature", "pressure": "approximately 1 bar", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Continue the warm aqueous phase and determine whether decarbamoylation occurs near pH 8.5. The literature's nominal 14-day warm phase is a reference for the overall processing stage, not an independently established duration for this step. CO2 and ammonia products equilibrate with bicarbonate and ammonium."}, "mechanism": "Hydrolytic cleavage of the carbamoyl substituent liberates valine; the removed carbamoyl group ultimately yields carbon dioxide and ammonia.", "reasoning": "Speculative valine-specific extrapolation from the N-carbamoyl-amino-acid sequence supported by Pulletikurti et al. (2022). The net coproducts follow carbamoyl hydrolysis rather than an arbitrary closure of an unresolved product mixture. Nevertheless, decarbamoylation under the proposed mild conditions is not demonstrated for this substrate and may prevent the hydantoin branches from yielding appreciable free valine."}, {"id": "rxn_010", "name": "Hypothesized Bucherer-Bergs capture of alpha-ketoisovalerate", "inputs": ["mol_008", "mol_003", "mol_002", "mol_013"], "outputs": ["mol_014", "mol_001"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "approximately 300 K; proposed room-temperature implementation", "pressure": "approximately 1 bar", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Reaction-class reference composition: 0.35 M ketoacid, 0.42 M total cyanide, 0.70 M total ammonium/ammonia and 0.5 M bicarbonate at pH 8.5, with an initial 21-day residence. These concentrations were not validated for alpha-ketoisovalerate. From a hypothetical 5 mM ketoacid source, reaching 0.35 M would require at least 70-fold concentration without loss; that source concentration is not established."}, "mechanism": "A proposed aminonitrile/carbamate/cyclization sequence forms a carboxylated isopropylhydantoin while retaining the substrate carboxyl group temporarily.", "reasoning": "Speculative substrate extension of Pulletikurti et al. (2022), whose documented substrate set does not directly demonstrate alpha-ketoisovalerate-to-valine conversion. The carboxylated intermediate is a mechanistic hypothesis, not a reported valine-specific identification. The supplied branched ketoacid, concentrated cyanide/ammonia, long residence, and simultaneous solute retention are explicit bottlenecks. Evaporation alone does not establish their natural availability."}, {"id": "rxn_011", "name": "Hypothesized decarboxylation of carboxylated isopropylhydantoin", "inputs": ["mol_014"], "outputs": ["mol_011", "mol_013"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-353 K during the proposed staged processing", "pressure": "approximately 1 bar", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Monitor carbon dioxide release and hydantoin identity during the room-temperature and warm phases. No substrate-specific decarboxylation rate or preferred temperature is established."}, "mechanism": "Loss of the substrate-derived carboxyl group followed by proton transfer produces 5-isopropylhydantoin. In this proposed atom mapping, cyanide subsequently supplies the valine carboxyl carbon.", "reasoning": "Speculative mechanistic resolution of the alpha-ketoacid Bucherer-Bergs reaction class described by Pulletikurti et al. (2022). It explicitly accounts for the carbon removed when the carboxylated intermediate becomes the ordinary hydantoin, but neither this intermediate nor its decarboxylation has been demonstrated for the valine substrate in the provided research. Isotope tracing is needed to test the proposed atom mapping."}, {"id": "rxn_012", "name": "Nickel-catalyzed reductive amination of alpha-ketoisovalerate", "inputs": ["mol_008", "mol_002", "mol_009"], "outputs": ["mol_010", "mol_001"], "environment": "Biochemical", "agents": ["Ni/SiO2-Al2O3 with accessible reduced nickel sites"], "conditions": {"temperature": "295 K", "pressure": "5 bar H2", "catalyst": "Ni/SiO2-Al2O3", "aqueous": true, "other": "Reported reference conditions: 30 mM alpha-ketoisovalerate, five equivalents NH4HCO3, pH 8, 72 h, and 10 mol% Ni relative to substrate. Use an oxygen-free, cyanide-free reactor. A natural analogue would require a confined H2-rich cool mineral interface; aged nickel sulfide is not assumed equivalent to the tested supported catalyst."}, "mechanism": "Ammonia forms an imine/iminium-type intermediate with the ketoacid; surface-activated hydrogen reduces it to valine.", "reasoning": "Direct valine experiment: Kaur et al. (2024). Their approximately 20% combined amino- and hydroxy-acid yield with a 34:66 ratio implies about 6.8% valine, an arithmetic inference rather than a separately reported yield. Catalyst-free controls did not reduce the substrate. Nickel offers a geochemically motivated active metal, but the engineered support, metal surface state, passivation and supplied 30 mM branched ketoacid prevent this from establishing a natural end-to-end synthesis."}, {"id": "rxn_013", "name": "Palladium-catalyzed reductive amination benchmark", "inputs": ["mol_008", "mol_002", "mol_009"], "outputs": ["mol_010", "mol_001"], "environment": "Biochemical", "agents": ["Pd/C"], "conditions": {"temperature": "295 K", "pressure": "5 bar H2", "catalyst": "Pd/C", "aqueous": true, "other": "Reported reference conditions: 30 mM alpha-ketoisovalerate, five equivalents NH4HCO3, pH 8, 72 h, and 1 mol% Pd relative to substrate. Oxygen and cyanide are excluded. This is a laboratory mineral-interface comparator; naturally available Pd/C is not assumed."}, "mechanism": "Palladium activates hydrogen and promotes hydrogenation of the ketoacid-derived imine or iminium species.", "reasoning": "Direct valine experiment: Kaur et al. (2024). Approximately 77% combined yield and an 83:17 amino:hydroxy ratio imply about 64% valine. This provides the strongest reported selectivity among the three catalysts, but palladium scarcity and the engineered carbon-supported catalyst substantially weaken its prebiotic environmental case. It is retained for its experimentally demonstrated chemistry, not promoted as a demonstrated terrestrial source."}, {"id": "rxn_014", "name": "Meteoritic-metal reductive amination of alpha-ketoisovalerate", "inputs": ["mol_008", "mol_002", "mol_009"], "outputs": ["mol_010", "mol_001"], "environment": "Biochemical", "agents": ["Ground Aletai iron meteorite containing nickel-bearing metal"], "conditions": {"temperature": "295 K", "pressure": "5 bar H2", "catalyst": "ground Aletai iron meteorite", "aqueous": true, "other": "Reported reference conditions: 30 mM alpha-ketoisovalerate, five equivalents NH4HCO3, pH 8, 72 h, and 3 mg ground meteorite per experimental vial. The supplied report does not specify enough reactor-volume or exposed-metal-area information to scale that loading environmentally. Freshly exposed metal, anoxia and exclusion of cyanide are required assumptions."}, "mechanism": "Hydrogen activation on reactive meteoritic metal sites enables reduction of an ammonia-derived imine/iminium intermediate.", "reasoning": "Direct valine experiment: Kaur et al. (2024). Approximately 74% combined yield and an 18:82 amino:hydroxy ratio imply about 13% valine. Meteoritic metal has a clearer natural provenance than engineered Pd/C, but grinding, accessible nickel sites, corrosion and sulfide passivation matter. The exact catalytic site is not established, and the branched ketoacid remains an imported precursor."}, {"id": "rxn_015", "name": "Competing nickel-catalyzed ketoacid hydrogenation", "inputs": ["mol_008", "mol_009"], "outputs": ["mol_015"], "environment": "Biochemical", "agents": ["Ni/SiO2-Al2O3 with accessible reduced nickel sites"], "conditions": {"temperature": "295 K", "pressure": "5 bar H2", "catalyst": "Ni/SiO2-Al2O3", "aqueous": true, "other": "Same reaction mixture as rxn_012: 30 mM ketoacid, five equivalents NH4HCO3, pH 8, 72 h and 10 mol% Ni. This flux competes with rxn_012; it is not a later step in that pathway."}, "mechanism": "Direct hydrogenation of the keto carbonyl gives 2-hydroxyisovaleric acid instead of valine.", "reasoning": "Kaur et al. (2024) directly observed the competing hydroxy acid. The reported combined yield and product ratio imply approximately 13.2% hydroxy-acid yield. Including this branch prevents nickel-mediated substrate reduction from being equated with valine synthesis. This molecule is a terminal competing product in the present graph, despite using the schema's intermediate role."}, {"id": "rxn_016", "name": "Competing palladium-catalyzed ketoacid hydrogenation", "inputs": ["mol_008", "mol_009"], "outputs": ["mol_015"], "environment": "Biochemical", "agents": ["Pd/C"], "conditions": {"temperature": "295 K", "pressure": "5 bar H2", "catalyst": "Pd/C", "aqueous": true, "other": "Same reaction mixture as rxn_013: 30 mM ketoacid, five equivalents NH4HCO3, pH 8, 72 h and 1 mol% Pd. This is a competing flux, not part of the productive reaction sequence."}, "mechanism": "Palladium-catalyzed carbonyl hydrogenation produces the alpha-hydroxy acid.", "reasoning": "Kaur et al. (2024) reported a 17% hydroxy-acid share within the approximately 77% combined product yield, implying about 13.1% hydroxy-acid yield. Palladium favors valine more strongly than the other tested catalysts but does not eliminate this sink. Natural catalyst availability remains a separate limitation."}, {"id": "rxn_017", "name": "Competing meteoritic-metal ketoacid hydrogenation", "inputs": ["mol_008", "mol_009"], "outputs": ["mol_015"], "environment": "Biochemical", "agents": ["Ground Aletai iron meteorite containing nickel-bearing metal"], "conditions": {"temperature": "295 K", "pressure": "5 bar H2", "catalyst": "ground Aletai iron meteorite", "aqueous": true, "other": "Same reaction mixture as rxn_014: 30 mM ketoacid, five equivalents NH4HCO3, pH 8, 72 h and 3 mg ground meteorite per reference vial. Environmental scaling and metal-surface aging are unresolved."}, "mechanism": "Surface-mediated hydrogenation of the keto carbonyl produces 2-hydroxyisovaleric acid.", "reasoning": "Kaur et al. (2024) reported an 82% hydroxy-acid share within the approximately 74% combined yield, implying about 60.7% hydroxy-acid yield. This dominant competing flux is essential to evaluating the meteorite route: high total reduction does not imply high valine selectivity. No return pathway from this sink is assumed."}], "pathways": [{"pathway_id": "P1", "name": "Imine-first Strecker synthesis with acidic hydrolysis", "description": "Supplied isobutyraldehyde enters the imine/aminonitrile sequence, followed by acidic aqueous hydrolysis. This is a literature-supported reaction family with proposed, rather than experimentally established, valine-specific pool conditions. The branched aldehyde source remains outside the demonstrated network.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_001", "rxn_002", "rxn_005"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_005", "mol_006"]}, {"pathway_id": "P2", "name": "Imine-first Strecker synthesis with alkaline hydrolysis", "description": "The same carbon-skeleton construction as P1 is followed by an alternative alkaline processing stage. This is a condition-resolved graph pathway, not an independent synthesis mechanism. Slow hydrolysis and aminonitrile decomposition are unresolved risks.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_001", "rxn_002", "rxn_006"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_005", "mol_006"]}, {"pathway_id": "P3", "name": "Cyanohydrin-first capture with acidic hydrolysis", "description": "A proposed cyanohydrin capture stage precedes ammonia enrichment and aminonitrile formation. Acidic hydrolysis then yields valine. The cyanohydrin loading order is not directly validated for prebiotic valine and may converge mechanistically on the ordinary imine sequence.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_003", "rxn_004", "rxn_005"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_007", "mol_006"]}, {"pathway_id": "P4", "name": "Cyanohydrin-first capture with alkaline hydrolysis", "description": "Combines the proposed cyanohydrin-first loading sequence with alkaline aminonitrile hydrolysis. It is a distinct operational sequence but carries both the untested precursor-capture assumption and the alkaline-hydrolysis kinetic uncertainty.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_003", "rxn_004", "rxn_006"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_007", "mol_006"]}, {"pathway_id": "P5", "name": "Imine-first synthesis through a hydantoin reservoir", "description": "The aldehyde-derived aminonitrile is diverted into a hypothesized isopropylhydantoin, then hydrolyzed through N-carbamoylvaline. The valine-specific cyclization and final hydrolysis are not established by the supplied literature. Concentrated inorganic carbon and prolonged aqueous processing are required hypotheses.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_001", "rxn_002", "rxn_007", "rxn_008", "rxn_009"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_006", "mol_011", "mol_012"]}, {"pathway_id": "P6", "name": "Cyanohydrin-first synthesis through a hydantoin reservoir", "description": "The alternative cyanohydrin loading sequence feeds the same hypothesized hydantoin and N-carbamoyl intermediates as P5. This is a distinct graph path assembled from explicitly conditional steps, not an additional direct experimental demonstration.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_003", "rxn_004", "rxn_007", "rxn_008", "rxn_009"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_007", "mol_006", "mol_011", "mol_012"]}, {"pathway_id": "P7", "name": "Alpha-ketoisovalerate Bucherer-Bergs hypothesis", "description": "Supplied alpha-ketoisovalerate undergoes proposed cyanide/ammonia/inorganic-carbon capture, decarboxylation and hydantoin hydrolysis. Pulletikurti et al. (2022) support the reaction class, not this substrate-specific route. Concentration, atom mapping, decarboxylation and decarbamoylation all require validation.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_010", "rxn_011", "rxn_008", "rxn_009"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_014", "mol_011", "mol_012"]}, {"pathway_id": "P8", "name": "Supported-nickel hydrogen reductive amination", "description": "Directly demonstrated conversion of supplied alpha-ketoisovalerate to valine under the Kaur et al. conditions. The approximate inferred valine yield is 6.8%. Hydroxy-acid formation competes through rxn_015 and is not included as a productive step. Natural precursor concentration and catalyst equivalence remain unresolved.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_012"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_008"]}, {"pathway_id": "P9", "name": "Palladium hydrogen reductive-amination benchmark", "description": "Directly demonstrated, comparatively selective conversion of supplied alpha-ketoisovalerate, with an approximate inferred valine yield of 64%. This is a laboratory-supported catalytic variant, not a demonstrated naturally available prebiotic system. The competing hydroxy-acid branch is rxn_016.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_013"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_008"]}, {"pathway_id": "P10", "name": "Meteoritic-metal hydrogen reductive amination", "description": "Directly demonstrated conversion over ground Aletai meteorite, with an approximate inferred valine yield of 13%. The catalyst has a natural provenance, but accessible metal area, corrosion and precursor supply are unresolved. Most measured reduction product is hydroxy acid through rxn_017, not valine.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_014"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_008"]}], "hub_molecules": ["mol_001", "mol_002", "mol_003", "mol_004", "mol_006", "mol_008", "mol_009", "mol_010", "mol_011", "mol_013", "mol_015"], "convergence_points": [{"molecule_id": "mol_006", "incoming_reactions": ["rxn_002", "rxn_004"], "outgoing_reactions": ["rxn_005", "rxn_006", "rxn_007"]}, {"molecule_id": "mol_011", "incoming_reactions": ["rxn_007", "rxn_011"], "outgoing_reactions": ["rxn_008"]}, {"molecule_id": "mol_010", "incoming_reactions": ["rxn_005", "rxn_006", "rxn_009", "rxn_012", "rxn_013", "rxn_014"], "outgoing_reactions": []}, {"molecule_id": "mol_015", "incoming_reactions": ["rxn_015", "rxn_016", "rxn_017"], "outgoing_reactions": []}]}, "reasoning": "", "duration_ms": 363559.5953464508, "success": true, "error_message": null}
{"timestamp": "2026-10-19T16:27:01.548960", "agent_name": "CriticAgent", "method_name": "evaluate_network", "step": null, "attempt": null, "system_message": "You are evaluating a COMPLETE SYNTHESIS NETWORK, not individual reactions.\n\n## Neutrality\n- Do not reward or penalize a network for using (or not using) any particular feedstock, intermediate,\n  reaction family, or prebiotic framework. Judge only the evidence and the chemistry.\n- The number of shared/hub molecules, the number of convergence points, and the order or number of\n  environments used are NOT scoring criteria.\n\n## Evaluation Criteria\n\n### 1. Network Validity (0.0-1.0)\n- Are there sufficient molecule and reaction nodes?\n- Is the network connected (no isolated subgraphs)?\n- Are molecule IDs used consistently, and are reactions approximately balanced?\n- When a DETERMINISTIC ATOM/CHARGE BALANCE CHECK section is provided, treat it as ground truth for\n  mass and charge conservation; reactions it reports as UNBALANCED are conservation violations.\n\n### 2. Chemical Feasibility (0.0-1.0)\n- Is each reaction chemically sound?\n- Are the conditions appropriate for each environment?\n- Are the mechanisms plausible?\n\n### 3. Environmental Consistency (0.0-1.0)\n- Are each reaction's conditions (temperature, pressure, pH, catalysts, energy source) consistent with its stated environment?\n- If molecules are transferred between environments, is the transfer physically justified?\n- If a catalyst or mineral agent is cited, is it realistic and present in that environment?\n\n### 4. Pathway Coherence (0.0-1.0)\n- Do pathways form valid routes to the target?\n- Are key intermediates properly identified?\n- Is the overall strategy sound?\n\n### 5. Target Achievement (0.0-1.0)\n- Does the network actually produce the target molecule?\n- Are the final steps chemically valid?\n\n### 6. Literature Alignment (0.0-1.0) - When research findings are provided\n- Are reactions supported by experimental evidence from cited studies?\n- Are literature citations present in reaction reasoning?\n- Does the network acknowledge critiques and known challenges identified in the research?\n- Departures from the research are acceptable when explicitly and soundly justified.\n\n## Scoring Guidelines\n- 0.9-1.0: Excellent network, valid and chemically sound\n- 0.8-0.9: Good network with minor issues\n- 0.7-0.8: Acceptable but with notable concerns\n- 0.5-0.7: Significant problems with validity or chemistry\n- 0.0-0.5: Major issues, network is not viable\n\nA network is APPROVED if overall_confidence >= 0.9\n\nOutput your evaluation as JSON matching the NetworkEvaluation schema.\n", "user_message": "Evaluate this synthesis network:\n\nTARGET MOLECULE: Valine\nOVERALL STRATEGY: This precursor-resolved network contains 10 distinct reaction-node sequences: four Strecker processing variants, two aldehyde-derived hydantoin variants, one ketoacid-derived hydantoin hypothesis, and three experimentally tested metal-catalyzed reductive-amination variants. They are not 10 independent mechanisms or 10 demonstrated de novo geochemical syntheses. Isobutyraldehyde and alpha-ketoisovaleric acid are explicitly supplied starting materials because the provided literature does not establish their production from simpler feedstocks under compatible conditions. The strongest directly demonstrated conversions are the three Kaur et al. catalyst variants; valine-specific hydantoin chemistry and alternative Strecker processing conditions remain hypotheses. Cyanohydrin-first paths represent an alternative precursor-capture sequence, not a claim of a separate elementary amination mechanism. Unresolved acetone/cyanide, thermal formaldehyde, shock, and radiolytic product networks are excluded rather than assigned invented coproduct balances. All represented net reactions conserve atoms; inferred coefficients do not represent experimental yields. Neutral parent formulas describe aqueous acid-base components, with protonation and counterions suppressed. All settings are anoxic. Surface reactions require sheltered water or pore residence despite strong incident UV. Biochemical denotes abiotic concentrated solutions or mineral interfaces, not enzymes. Branches are alternative reactors, not a single compatible mixture: cyanide must not enter the metal-hydrogenation reactors because it can poison their catalysts. Competing hydroxy-acid formation is represented separately and is not counted as a productive pathway. No stereoselection is established; the target has unspecified stereochemistry and racemic formation is expected. environment_formed records a representative setting, not an exclusive one.\n\nMOLECULES (15 nodes):\n  - mol_001: H2O (water) [starting]\n  - mol_002: H3N (ammonia) [starting]\n  - mol_003: CHN (hydrogen cyanide) [starting]\n  - mol_004: C4H8O (isobutyraldehyde) [starting]\n  - mol_005: C4H9N (isobutyraldimine) [intermediate]\n  - mol_006: C5H10N2 (valine aminonitrile) [hub_intermediate]\n  - mol_007: C5H9NO (isobutyraldehyde cyanohydrin) [intermediate]\n  - mol_008: C5H8O3 (alpha-ketoisovaleric acid) [starting]\n  - mol_009: H2 (hydrogen) [starting]\n  - mol_010: C5H11NO2 (valine) [target]\n  - mol_011: C6H10N2O2 (5-isopropylhydantoin) [hub_intermediate]\n  - mol_012: C6H12N2O3 (N-carbamoylvaline) [intermediate]\n  - mol_013: CO2 (carbon dioxide) [starting]\n  - mol_014: C7H10N2O4 (carboxylated isopropylhydantoin) [intermediate]\n  - mol_015: C5H10O3 (2-hydroxyisovaleric acid) [intermediate]\n\nREACTIONS (17 nodes):\n  - rxn_001: mol_004, mol_002 -> mol_005, mol_001 (Reversible formation of the isobutyraldehyde aldimine) [Surface]\n  - rxn_002: mol_005, mol_003 -> mol_006 (Cyanide capture of the aldimine) [Surface]\n  - rxn_003: mol_004, mol_003 -> mol_007 (Alternative cyanohydrin-first capture of isobutyraldehyde) [Surface]\n  - rxn_004: mol_007, mol_002 -> mol_006, mol_001 (Net conversion of the cyanohydrin pool to valine aminonitrile) [Surface]\n  - rxn_005: mol_006, mol_001 -> mol_010, mol_002 (Acid-promoted aminonitrile hydrolysis to valine) [Surface]\n  - rxn_006: mol_006, mol_001 -> mol_010, mol_002 (Alternative alkaline aminonitrile hydrolysis to valine) [Surface]\n  - rxn_007: mol_006, mol_013 -> mol_011 (Hypothesized carbon-dioxide capture and hydantoin formation) [Surface]\n  - rxn_008: mol_011, mol_001 -> mol_012 (Hypothesized hydrolytic opening of isopropylhydantoin) [Surface]\n  - rxn_009: mol_012, mol_001 -> mol_010, mol_002, mol_013 (Hypothesized N-carbamoylvaline hydrolysis) [Surface]\n  - rxn_010: mol_008, mol_003, mol_002, mol_013 -> mol_014, mol_001 (Hypothesized Bucherer-Bergs capture of alpha-ketoisovalerate) [Surface]\n  - rxn_011: mol_014 -> mol_011, mol_013 (Hypothesized decarboxylation of carboxylated isopropylhydantoin) [Surface]\n  - rxn_012: mol_008, mol_002, mol_009 -> mol_010, mol_001 (Nickel-catalyzed reductive amination of alpha-ketoisovalerate) [Biochemical]\n  - rxn_013: mol_008, mol_002, mol_009 -> mol_010, mol_001 (Palladium-catalyzed reductive amination benchmark) [Biochemical]\n  - rxn_014: mol_008, mol_002, mol_009 -> mol_010, mol_001 (Meteoritic-metal reductive amination of alpha-ketoisovalerate) [Biochemical]\n  - rxn_015: mol_008, mol_009 -> mol_015 (Competing nickel-catalyzed ketoacid hydrogenation) [Biochemical]\n  - rxn_016: mol_008, mol_009 -> mol_015 (Competing palladium-catalyzed ketoacid hydrogenation) [Biochemical]\n  - rxn_017: mol_008, mol_009 -> mol_015 (Competing meteoritic-metal ketoacid hydrogenation) [Biochemical]\n\nPATHWAYS (10):\n  - P1: Imine-first Strecker synthesis with acidic hydrolysis (3 steps)\n  - P2: Imine-first Strecker synthesis with alkaline hydrolysis (3 steps)\n  - P3: Cyanohydrin-first capture with acidic hydrolysis (3 steps)\n  - P4: Cyanohydrin-first capture with alkaline hydrolysis (3 steps)\n  - P5: Imine-first synthesis through a hydantoin reservoir (5 steps)\n  - P6: Cyanohydrin-first synthesis through a hydantoin reservoir (5 steps)\n  - P7: Alpha-ketoisovalerate Bucherer-Bergs hypothesis (4 steps)\n  - P8: Supported-nickel hydrogen reductive amination (1 steps)\n  - P9: Palladium hydrogen reductive-amination benchmark (1 steps)\n  - P10: Meteoritic-metal hydrogen reductive amination (1 steps)\n\n\n=== DETERMINISTIC ATOM/CHARGE BALANCE CHECK (RDKit) ===\nComputed programmatically from each molecule's InChI (via RDKit) or stated formula. Reactions list no coefficients, so a reaction counts as balanced if some positive integer coefficients (<= 24) conserve every element and net charge.\nSummary: 17 reactions | 15 balanced as written | 2 balanceable with coefficients | 0 UNBALANCED | 0 unchecked\n  - rxn_005 balanceable: C5H10N2 + 2 H2O -> C5H11NO2 + H3N\n  - rxn_006 balanceable: C5H10N2 + 2 H2O -> C5H11NO2 + H3N\nTreat this check as ground truth for mass and charge conservation. Reactions that cannot be balanced violate conservation: reflect this in chemical_feasibility and reaction_scores, list them in problematic_reactions, and name the specific reaction IDs (with the missing species, if hinted) in your feedback so the network can be corrected. Note that implausibly large inferred coefficients may also indicate a mis-specified reaction.\n=== END BALANCE CHECK ===\n\n\n=== LITERATURE RESEARCH ON TARGET MOLECULE ===\nThe following research was conducted on the target molecule before network generation. Use it to assess whether the network aligns with known prebiotic chemistry literature.\n\n=== DEEP RESEARCH FINDINGS ===\n# Prebiotic Synthesis of Valine: Routes, Precursors, Catalysts, Environmental Context, and Open Problems\n\n## Executive assessment\n\nValine is a useful stress test for prebiotic synthesis models because it is not merely an \u03b1-amino acid: it requires construction of a **branched C5 carbon skeleton**, HOOC\u2013CH(NH\u2082)\u2013CH(CH\u2083)\u2082. The literature contains several direct demonstrations of abiotic valine formation, but the evidence is uneven. The strongest direct demonstrations fall into five chemically distinct families: **Strecker-type atmospheric/electric-discharge chemistry; ammonium-cyanide/ketone chemistry; high-temperature formaldehyde\u2013ammonia chemistry; shock chemistry; and reductive amination of \u03b1-ketoisovalerate with H\u2082 over metal catalysts**. More recently, **\u03b3-radiolysis of formaldehyde/ammonia solutions in meteorite-parent-body analogues** has also generated valine as a minor product.\n\nThe central conclusion is that **no published route yet closes the entire synthesis from the simplest geochemical feedstocks to valine under one experimentally demonstrated, geochemically well-constrained environment**. The classical Strecker family can, in principle, build the C5 skeleton by adding the carbon of HCN to the C4 aldehyde isobutyraldehyde, and electric-discharge experiments genuinely produce valine, but the strongly reducing atmospheric mixtures that produce aldehydes and HCN most efficiently are difficult to reconcile with evidence that Hadean mantle outgassing was comparatively oxidized. Conversely, the 2024 H\u2082/Ni chemistry of Kaur and co-workers converts the biologically congruent precursor **\u03b1-ketoisovalerate directly to valine under remarkably mild conditions**, but \u03b1-ketoisovalerate was supplied at 30 mM: its abiotic synthesis from CO\u2082, CO, pyruvate, or other simpler feedstocks under the same conditions remains the major missing step.\n\nA useful evidence hierarchy is therefore:\n\n| Route family | Direct valine demonstrated? | Simplest feedstocks experimentally connected to the route | Main bottleneck |\n|---|---:|---|---|\n| Electric discharge \u2192 aldehydes + HCN \u2192 Strecker chemistry | **Yes** | CH\u2084, NH\u2083 or N\u2082, H\u2082O, H\u2082 | Reduced atmosphere; production of enough C4 isobutyraldehyde; low selectivity |\n| NH\u2084CN + ketone network | **Yes** | HCN/NH\u2083 plus acetone | Mechanism of C5 branching is poorly resolved; NH\u2084CN alone does **not** reliably give valine |\n| Heated HCHO + NH\u2083 | **Yes, historically** | Formaldehyde, ammonia, water | Harsh heating; poorly defined bound intermediates; weak valine-specific quantitation/reproducibility |\n| Shock-heated reduced gases | **Yes** | CH\u2084, C\u2082H\u2086, NH\u2083 | Highly reducing gas composition not representative of favored global Hadean atmosphere |\n| CO\u2082/N\u2082 impact chemistry | **Not yet** | CO\u2082/HCO\u2083\u207b, N\u2082, H\u2082O, Fe, Ni, silicates | Experiments stop at simpler amino acids; no demonstrated valine |\n| \u03b1-Ketoisovalerate + NH\u2083 + H\u2082, Ni/Pd/meteorite | **Yes** | H\u2082, NH\u2083 plus preformed C5 \u03b1-ketoacid | Origin and concentration of \u03b1-ketoisovalerate |\n| \u03b1-Ketoacid + CN\u207b/NH\u2083 Bucherer\u2013Bergs chemistry | **Mechanistically plausible; valine not directly shown in the 2022 substrate set** | \u03b1-Ketoacid, CN\u207b, ammonia source, bicarbonate | Requires preformed \u03b1-ketoisovalerate and concentrated cyanide/ammonia; long processing |\n| CO/H\u2082/NH\u2083 Fischer\u2013Tropsch-type mineral chemistry | **No in the principal 2012 study** | CO, H\u2082, NH\u2083 | Produces other amino acids, notably AIB; valine was not reported |\n| \u03b3-Radiolysis of HCHO/NH\u2083 parent-body solutions | **Yes, minor product** | HCHO, NH\u2083, H\u2082O, sometimes CH\u2083OH/glycolaldehyde | Product complexity, dose/concentration assumptions, bound precursors, extraterrestrial rather than terrestrial setting |\n\nThe route classifications above follow the direct experiments of Miller (1957, *Biochimica et Biophysica Acta*), Friedmann & Miller (1969, *Nature*), Bar-Nun, Bar-Nun, Bauer & Sagan (1970, *Science*), Fox & Windsor (1970, *Science*), Ring, Wolman, Friedmann & Miller (1972, *Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences*), Pizzarello (2012, *Meteoritics & Planetary Science*), Pulletikurti, Yadav, Springsteen & Krishnamurthy (2022, *Nature Chemistry*), Kaur et al. (2024, *Chem*), and Ishikawa, Kebukawa, Kobayashi & Yoda (2024, *Life*).\n\n## Known synthesis routes and mechanisms\n\n### Electric-discharge chemistry and the Strecker route\n\nThe most chemically explicit classical route is the one established mechanistically by **Stanley Miller (1957, *Biochimica et Biophysica Acta*)**. Electric discharge through CH\u2084\u2013NH\u2083\u2013H\u2082O\u2013H\u2082 mixtures produced **HCN and aldehydes**, which then entered Strecker chemistry. Miller showed that aminonitrile hydrolysis proceeded quickly enough to account for the amino acids accumulated after roughly a week of sparking. Replacing NH\u2083 with N\u2082 still generated the same general product types, but at lower yield. Addition of ferrous ammonium sulfate did not substantially change product yields, arguing that no Fe/sulfate mineral catalysis was necessary in this experiment.\n\nFor valine specifically, the chemically straightforward Strecker sequence is:\n\n\\[\n(CH_3)_2CHCHO + NH_3 \\rightleftharpoons (CH_3)_2CHCH{=}NH + H_2O\n\\]\n\n\\[\n(CH_3)_2CHCH{=}NH + HCN \\rightarrow (CH_3)_2CHCH(NH_2)CN\n\\]\n\nfollowed by nitrile hydrolysis:\n\n\\[\n(CH_3)_2CHCH(NH_2)CN + 2H_2O \\rightarrow\n(CH_3)_2CHCH(NH_2)CO_2H + NH_3\n\\]\n\nThus the crucial carbon precursor is **2-methylpropanal, or isobutyraldehyde**. Its four-carbon branched skeleton supplies valine C2\u2013C5, while cyanide supplies the carboxyl carbon. This is an important point for network design: making HCN is not enough; an efficient prebiotic valine network also has to generate a significant pool of **branched C4 aldehyde**.\n\nDirect confirmation that such discharge chemistry reaches valine came from **Ring, Wolman, Friedmann & Miller (1972, *PNAS*)**. They discharged a mixture of methane, nitrogen and water containing traces of ammonia and identified, using ion-exchange chromatography together with GC\u2013MS, glycine, alanine, \u03b1-aminobutyric acid, \u03b1-aminoisobutyric acid, **valine**, norvaline, isovaline, leucine, isoleucine, alloisoleucine, norleucine and several polar amino acids. The broad isomer distribution is significant: the experiment demonstrates chemical accessibility but **poor structural selectivity** for the biological amino-acid set.\n\nThe main environmental objection is now substantial. **Trail, Watson & Tailby (2011, *Nature*)** used Ce redox systematics in Hadean zircons to infer mantle-derived magmas near the fayalite\u2013magnetite\u2013quartz redox buffer by about 4.35 Ga. Such outgassing is predicted to favor H\u2082O, CO\u2082, SO\u2082 and N\u2082 rather than the CH\u2084-, NH\u2083- and H\u2082-rich mixture most favorable to Miller-type amino-acid synthesis. This does not prove that strongly reducing local or transient environments never existed, but it makes a globally persistent Miller-style atmosphere difficult to defend geochemically.\n\nAttempts to extend spark chemistry to more neutral CO\u2082/N\u2082 atmospheres illustrate the problem. **Cleaves, Chalmers, Lazcano, Miller & Bada (2008, *Origins of Life and Evolution of Biospheres*)** found that amino-acid recovery from CO\u2082/N\u2082 discharge mixtures could be raised considerably by controlling pH and suppressing oxidative destruction during work-up. In experiments with approximately 0.13 bar CO\u2082 and 0.13 bar N\u2082, CaCO\u2083 buffering raised total amino-acid recovery relative to unbuffered experiments. However, the published product set emphasized glycine, alanine, \u03b2-alanine, AIB, aspartate, glutamate and serine rather than valine. Moreover, **Kuwahara, Eto, Kawamoto, Kurihara, Kaneko, Obayashi & Kobayashi (2012, *Origins of Life and Evolution of Biospheres*)** used isotopic tests to show that ascorbate, which had been used as an oxidation inhibitor in some such experiments, could itself contribute carbon to apparent amino-acid products. Thus neutral-atmosphere spark chemistry remains relevant for simpler precursors, but **it is not currently a strong direct experimental route to valine**.\n\n### Ammonium cyanide plus ketones\n\n**Nadav Friedmann & Stanley Miller (1969, *Nature*)** directly revisited reports that polymerized ammonium cyanide produced the branched protein amino acids. Their result is unusually important because it contains both a positive result and a published refutation: they **could not detect valine, isoleucine or leucine from NH\u2084CN polymerization alone** when more stringent separation was used. Earlier identifications had relied primarily on amino-acid-analyzer elution positions, where non-protein amino acids can co-elute with valine or isoleucine.\n\nWhen **acetone or methyl ethyl ketone was added to NH\u2084CN**, however, Friedmann and Miller obtained significant quantities of valine and, depending on the ketone, alloisoleucine and isoleucine. This therefore constitutes a direct prebiotic valine synthesis route from a cyanide/ammonia pool plus simple ketones.\n\nMechanistically, this route is more problematic than the ordinary aldehyde Strecker pathway. A simple Strecker reaction of acetone, NH\u2083 and HCN gives **\u03b1-aminoisobutyric acid**, a C4 amino acid, not C5 valine. Consequently, the reported appearance of valine from acetone/NH\u2084CN must involve additional carbon\u2013carbon chemistry in the cyanide/ketone reaction network. Friedmann and Miller established the product much more securely than earlier NH\u2084CN work, but they did not establish a unique elementary mechanism for the C5 branch. This makes the route experimentally real but **mechanistically underconstrained for network reconstruction**.\n\nThe study also supplies one of the clearest historical warnings for the valine literature: **NH\u2084CN polymerization by itself should not be counted as an established valine synthesis**. The most careful direct re-investigation found that earlier assignments were probably chromatographic false positives.\n\n### Heated formaldehyde\u2013ammonia chemistry\n\nA chemically very different route was reported by **Sidney Fox & Charles Windsor (1970, *Science*)**. Heating formaldehyde with ammonia generated a complex product which, after hydrolysis, yielded aspartate, glutamate, serine, proline, **valine**, glycine and alanine; glycine was the dominant amino acid. Later experimental literature describing the original procedure reports heating at approximately **185 \u00b0C**, illustrating that this was a substantially hotter process than ordinary aqueous surface chemistry.\n\nThis is not best understood as an ordinary Strecker reaction because HCN and a discrete valine aldehyde were not supplied. The experimentally observed amino acids appeared importantly after hydrolysis, pointing to **nitrogen-containing oligomers or other hydrolysable bound precursors** rather than to a clean solution of free amino acids. The detailed route from C1 formaldehyde to a branched C5 valine skeleton was never resolved at an elementary-mechanism level. Modern work generally treats related HCHO/NH\u2083 chemistry as a nitrogen-containing, formose-like condensation network rather than a single selective synthesis.\n\nModern parent-body simulations expose a major reproducibility problem for valine in this chemistry. **Yoko Kebukawa and co-workers (2017, *Science Advances*)** heated formaldehyde, glycolaldehyde, ammonia and water at **90\u2013250 \u00b0C for 72 h**, producing complex insoluble organic matter and numerous amino-acid precursors. Their analytical standard included valine, but the valine recovered from experiments was detected only as the **L enantiomer**, unlike genuinely abiotic products that were racemic; the authors therefore regarded the apparent valine as probable biological contamination rather than evidence of synthesis.\n\nThat result does not disprove Fox & Windsor's 1970 observation, because the recipes differ substantially, but it means that **thermal HCHO/NH\u2083 chemistry should not be treated as a robust, reproducibly quantified modern valine pathway**. It does robustly generate simpler amino acids and hydrolysable organic material; the C5 branched products remain much less secure.\n\n### Radiation-driven formaldehyde\u2013ammonia chemistry in small bodies\n\nA more recent variant uses ionizing radiation rather than heat alone. **Yoko Kebukawa (2022, *ACS Central Science*)** showed that \u03b3 irradiation of aqueous formaldehyde and ammonia mixtures produced multiple amino acids after hydrolysis. Amino-acid yield scaled approximately linearly with accumulated \u03b3 dose and was comparatively insensitive to dose rate, which matters because the proposed natural energy source is \u03b3 radiation associated with radionuclides such as ^26Al in aqueously altered asteroid parent bodies. The estimated natural radiation production was argued to be chemically significant on parent-body timescales.\n\nThe expanded experiments of **Akari Ishikawa, Yoko Kebukawa, Kensei Kobayashi & Isao Yoda (2024, *Life*)** varied the formaldehyde/ammonia/methanol composition and reported **valine among the lower-abundance amino-acid products**, behind alanine, glycine, \u03b2-alanine, \u03b1-aminobutyric acid and several other species. Representative experiments used large integrated \u03b3 doses on the order of tens to ~90 kGy.\n\nMechanistically, this chemistry is more plausibly a **radical-driven reaction network** than a single Strecker event. Radiolysis generates reactive carbon-, oxygen- and nitrogen-centered species; formaldehyde can also form glycolaldehyde and higher carbonyl compounds, which then feed carbon-chain growth. A discrete, experimentally traced sequence from HCHO to the branched C5 valine skeleton has not yet been established, so the route is direct at the product level but not mechanistically closed.\n\nIts environmental relevance is strongest for **aqueously altered carbonaceous-asteroid parent bodies**, not for a particular early-Earth surface environment. It therefore represents a plausible contribution through extraterrestrial synthesis and delivery rather than a demonstrated endogenous terrestrial source.\n\n### Shock synthesis\n\n**A. Bar-Nun, N. Bar-Nun, S. H. Bauer & Carl Sagan (1970, *Science*)** subjected a reduced gas mixture containing approximately **3.3% CH\u2084, 11% C\u2082H\u2086 and 5.6% NH\u2083, with the balance Ar**, to single-pulse shock heating followed by rapid quenching. They directly observed **glycine, alanine, valine and leucine**. The reported overall efficiency for \u03b1-amino-acid production was about **5 \u00d7 10\u00b9\u2070 molecules per erg of shock energy**. The authors argued that rapid quenching could kinetically trap amino acids that would not be favored at the peak equilibrium temperature.\n\nThe mechanism was not resolved into a valine-specific sequence. At shock temperatures, extensive radical fragmentation and recombination occur, followed by chemistry during rapid cooling. In principle, aldehydes, HCN/nitriles and related intermediates can emerge during such processing, but the study established valine production rather than a unique pathway to it. No mineral catalyst was required.\n\nThe principal critique is environmental. The experiment requires substantial reduced carbon and ammonia. The Hadean zircon constraints of Trail, Watson & Tailby favor substantially more oxidized volcanic outgassing, making this particular bulk-gas composition difficult to justify as the persistent global atmosphere.\n\nModern impact experiments have substantially improved the geochemical starting composition, but they have **not yet closed the gap to valine**. **Takeuchi, Furukawa, Kobayashi, Sekine, Terada & Kakegawa (2020, *Scientific Reports*)** shocked mixtures of Fe, Ni, forsterite, water, bicarbonate/CO\u2082 and N\u2082, designed to represent an Fe-bearing impactor striking an ocean beneath a CO\u2082/N\u2082 atmosphere. Their experiments used an impact velocity of about **0.9 km s\u207b\u00b9**, pressure of about **7 GPa for 0.7 \u03bcs**, a shock-compression temperature near **300 \u00b0C**, and estimated post-shock temperatures around **1500 \u00b0C**. Isotopic labeling confirmed abiotic amino-acid formation. Glycine and alanine were prominent; additional low-molecular-weight amino acids appeared when ammonia was supplied, but **valine was not demonstrated**.\n\nThis creates an instructive contrast: **the old reduced-gas shock experiment reaches valine; the newer geochemically more conservative CO\u2082/N\u2082/Fe impact experiment does not**. Impact chemistry is therefore a credible source of valine precursors and simpler amino acids, but the direct valine evidence still depends on the less geochemically favored reduced gas mixture.\n\n### Reductive amination of \u03b1-ketoisovalerate with hydrogen\n\nThe clearest modern direct conversion of a specifically valine-like metabolic precursor is the work of **Harpreet Kaur, Sophia Rauscher, Emilie Werner, Youngdong Song, Jing Yi, Wahnyalo Kaz\u00f6ne, William Martin, Harun T\u00fcys\u00fcz & Joseph Moran (2024, *Chem*)**. They examined six biological \u03b1-ketoacids, including **\u03b1-ketoisovalerate**, under H\u2082 with ammonia and heterogeneous metal catalysts. \u03b1-Ketoisovalerate gave valine together with its competing carbonyl-reduction product, 2-hydroxyisovalerate.\n\nThe net valine reaction is simple:\n\n\\[\n(CH_3)_2CHCOCO_2H + NH_3 + H_2\n\\rightarrow\n(CH_3)_2CHCH(NH_2)CO_2H + H_2O\n\\]\n\nMechanistically, ammonia reacts with the \u03b1-keto carbonyl to form an imine/iminium-type intermediate, followed by surface-mediated hydrogenation. The competing reaction is direct hydrogenation of the carbonyl to the corresponding \u03b1-hydroxy acid.\n\nThe standard valine experiment used **30 mM \u03b1-ketoisovalerate**, five equivalents NH\u2084HCO\u2083, **5 bar H\u2082**, water at **pH 8**, **22 \u00b0C**, and **72 h**. Three catalysts worked: Pd/C containing 1 mol% Pd relative to substrate, Ni/SiO\u2082\u2013Al\u2082O\u2083 containing 10 mol% Ni, and 3 mg of ground **Aletai iron meteorite** containing substantial Ni. No reduction products were seen in catalyst-free controls.\n\nFor \u03b1-ketoisovalerate specifically, Figure 2 of Kaur et al. gives unusually useful quantitative data. The reported combined amino-acid plus hydroxy-acid yields and amino:hydroxy ratios were:\n\n| Catalyst | Combined valine + 2-hydroxyisovalerate yield | Valine:hydroxy-acid ratio | Approximate valine yield inferred from the figure |\n|---|---:|---:|---:|\n| Ni/SiO\u2082\u2013Al\u2082O\u2083 | 20% | 34:66 | **~6.8%** |\n| Pd/C | 77% | 83:17 | **~64%** |\n| Aletai meteorite | 74% | 18:82 | **~13%** |\n\nThe last column is an arithmetic inference from the published combined yield and product ratio, not a separately reported value. The striking point is the trade-off in plausibility versus selectivity: **Pd is much more amino-acid selective, whereas Ni and meteoritic metal are geochemically easier to motivate but favor substantial hydroxy-acid formation**.\n\nThe same study found that the chemistry can function at ammonium concentrations from **6 to 150 mM**. It also linked reductive amination to H\u2082-driven reductive-aldol chemistry involving glyoxylate, oxaloacetate and other \u03b1-ketoacids. Some aldol steps occur even without catalyst, whereas the critical hydrogenation steps require the metal catalyst. The paper notes that hydrothermal fluids can contain approximately **1\u201315 mmol kg\u207b\u00b9 dissolved H\u2082**, making the reductant geochemically attractive in serpentinization-associated systems.\n\nThis is therefore one of the strongest **final-step** valine syntheses in the literature, but it is not yet a de novo valine synthesis from CO\u2082/H\u2082. The experiment **supplied \u03b1-ketoisovalerate**. The coupled reaction network generated \u03b1-ketoglutarate and other \u03b1-ketoacids from simpler precursors but did not establish a pathway from CO\u2082, pyruvate or glyoxylate all the way to \u03b1-ketoisovalerate. In modern biochemistry, construction of the valine skeleton from pyruvate involves a highly specific branched-chain pathway; an experimentally validated nonenzymatic equivalent remains missing.\n\n### Bucherer\u2013Bergs chemistry from \u03b1-ketoacids\n\n**Sunil Pulletikurti, Mahipal Yadav, Greg Springsteen & Ramanarayanan Krishnamurthy (2022, *Nature Chemistry*)** demonstrated a general alternative in which \u03b1-ketoacids react with cyanide and ammonia sources through **Bucherer\u2013Bergs-type chemistry** to produce the corresponding \u03b1-amino acids. Their mechanistic sequence goes through cyanide addition/amino-nitrile chemistry, cyclization to a **hydantoin**, hydrolysis to an N-carbamoyl amino acid, and finally amino-acid formation.\n\nUnder their optimized general procedure, an \u03b1-ketoacid was used at roughly **0.35 M**, with 2 equivalents of NH\u2084Cl or diamidophosphate, 1.2 equivalents NaCN, and **0.5 M bicarbonate at pH 8.5**. The mixture was held at room temperature for **21 days** and then heated at **80 \u00b0C for 14 days** to complete amino-acid formation. Their supporting information shows explicitly that hydantoin hydrolysis can proceed through an N-carbamoyl amino acid and that ammonia-source chemistry affects the hydrolysis.\n\nFor valine-network design, the obvious substrate is **\u03b1-ketoisovalerate**, which would be expected to give an isopropyl-substituted hydantoin and ultimately valine. However, the experimental substrate set documented in the 2022 paper concentrates on pyruvate, \u03b1-ketoglutarate, oxaloacetate and glyoxylate; **the paper does not provide a direct \u03b1-ketoisovalerate-to-valine experiment**. The pathway is therefore a well-supported reaction class that is mechanistically transferable to valine, but it should not be counted as a direct valine demonstration from that study.\n\nIts principal prebiotic criticisms are the comparatively high substrate/buffer concentrations, long reaction periods, requirement for cyanide plus a concentrated ammonia source, and\u2014again\u2014the prior availability of \u03b1-ketoisovalerate. Kaur et al. explicitly contrasted their H\u2082 chemistry with Bucherer\u2013Bergs chemistry, noting both its use of cyanide and its two-stage temperature protocol.\n\n## Key intermediates, precursors, catalysts, and kinetic constraints\n\nFor network-design purposes, the literature reduces to two particularly important **valine-specific carbon skeletons**.\n\nThe first is **isobutyraldehyde (2-methylpropanal)**. A Strecker route needs this C4 branched aldehyde plus HCN and ammonia. The aldehyde carbon becomes valine's \u03b1 carbon and the cyanide carbon becomes the carboxyl carbon. The immediate intermediate is **2-amino-3-methylbutanenitrile**, whose hydrolysis yields valine. HCN and the imine/aminonitrile intermediates therefore matter kinetically as much as the aldehyde itself. Miller's 1957 experiments showed that aminonitrile hydrolysis rates were sufficient to account for amino-acid accumulation on approximately week-long discharge timescales.\n\nThe second is **\u03b1-ketoisovalerate, 3-methyl-2-oxobutanoate**. It already contains all five carbons in the correct topology; only the \u03b1-keto group must be converted into an \u03b1-amino group. It is the direct precursor in the Kaur H\u2082/Ni/Pd route and the obvious substrate for a valine-specific Bucherer\u2013Bergs pathway. This makes \u03b1-ketoisovalerate particularly attractive for metabolism-congruent models, but also shifts nearly the entire carbon-skeleton problem upstream.\n\nThe major catalytic evidence can be summarized as follows.\n\n| Reaction | Catalyst/surface experimentally tested | Requirement |\n|---|---|---|\n| Spark generation of HCN/aldehydes and subsequent Strecker chemistry | No mineral catalyst required; Fe ammonium sulfate gave no important enhancement in Miller's experiment | Electrical energy; reducing feedstock mixture |\n| NH\u2084CN + ketone valine network | No mineral catalyst reported as required | Cyanide/ammonia chemistry plus ketone |\n| Heated HCHO/NH\u2083 route | No mineral catalyst required in original Fox\u2013Windsor experiment | Strong heating, followed by hydrolysis |\n| Reduced-gas shock route | No heterogeneous catalyst required | Shock heating and rapid quench |\n| \u03b1-Ketoisovalerate + H\u2082/NH\u2083 | Ni/SiO\u2082\u2013Al\u2082O\u2083, Pd/C, Aletai meteorite | **Catalyst essential** under tested conditions |\n| General \u03b1-ketoacid reductive amination | Fresh FeS or Fe(OH)\u2082 in Huber\u2013W\u00e4chtersh\u00e4user experiments | Strongly surface/state dependent |\n| Photochemical \u03b1-ketoacid amination | ZnS/sphalerite in Wang et al. | UV illumination and electron donor/hole-scavenging chemistry |\n| FTT-type CO/H\u2082/NH\u2083 chemistry | Fe/Ni meteorite powder, magnetite, Murchison material; bentonite much less active | 370 \u00b0C catalytic surface chemistry |\n\nThe first four rows follow Miller (1957), Friedmann & Miller (1969), Fox & Windsor (1970), and Bar-Nun et al. (1970); the H\u2082 metal-catalysis result is from Kaur et al. (2024).\n\nTwo older mineral studies are important mechanistically even though they did **not directly make valine**. **Claudia Huber & G\u00fcnter W\u00e4chtersh\u00e4user (2003, *Tetrahedron Letters*)** showed that freshly precipitated FeS and Fe(OH)\u2082 can promote reductive amination of several \u03b1-ketoacids with ammonium salts. Their experiments produced alanine, glutamate, phenylalanine and tyrosine, with maximum yields for several systems of tens of mol%; the reaction was highly sensitive to the physical state of FeS, pH and ionic strength. Optimal pH with ammonia was around **8.8\u20139.3**, close to the NH\u2084\u207a/NH\u2083 pK\u2090; elevated ionic strength suppressed the reaction. At 100 \u00b0C the tested tyrosine-forming system became fast on approximately hourly timescales, whereas reaction could still occur slowly at 4 \u00b0C. No \u03b1-ketoisovalerate/valine experiment was reported, so this work is evidence for a *reaction class*, not for valine itself.\n\nSimilarly, **Wei Wang, Qiliang Li, Bin Yang, Xiaoyang Liu, Yanqiang Yang & Wenhui Su (2012, *Chemical Communications*)** demonstrated reversible photochemical amination of four reverse-TCA-related \u03b1-ketoacids on irradiated ZnS. Their experiments used an oxygen-free ZnS suspension, pH around 9 and a Hg\u2013Xe source; glutamate yield was only about 0.5% after four hours without a sulfite hole scavenger and increased when the surface and photoredox conditions were optimized. \u03b1-Ketoisovalerate was not one of the substrates, so ZnS is a plausible catalyst family to test for valine chemistry rather than an existing direct demonstration.\n\nThe **Fischer\u2013Tropsch-type framework** deserves the same caution. **Sandra Pizzarello (2012, *Meteoritics & Planetary Science*)** reacted CO:H\u2082:NH\u2083 in a 1:1:1 gas mixture over meteorite/mineral powders at about **370 \u00b0C for 24 h**. Fe/Ni meteorite powder produced glycine, alanine, \u03b1-aminobutyric acid and smaller amounts of higher homologues; magnetite strongly favored the branched non-protein amino acid **\u03b1-aminoisobutyric acid**, reaching about 540 nmol g\u207b\u00b9 catalyst, and also yielded ~1 nmol g\u207b\u00b9 isovaline. **Valine itself was not reported in the product table.** Bentonite was a poor catalyst. FTT chemistry therefore provides experimentally important CO/H\u2082/NH\u2083 carbon\u2013nitrogen chemistry but should not currently be listed as a demonstrated valine route.\n\nThe valine literature contains surprisingly little **valine-specific equilibrium thermodynamics** under proposed prebiotic conditions. Most constraints are kinetic/empirical: week-scale aminonitrile hydrolysis in Miller chemistry; ultrafast high-temperature formation followed by quenching in shock chemistry; 72-h hydrogenation at 22 \u00b0C for Kaur's route; weeks-long formation/hydrolysis in the Bucherer\u2013Bergs system; and approximately linear accumulated-dose dependence in \u03b3-radiolysis. Thus, for synthesis-network modeling, experimentally determined rate constants, activation parameters and competing decomposition rates for **isobutyraldehyde, valine aminonitrile and \u03b1-ketoisovalerate under realistic saline/mineral conditions are major missing datasets**.\n\n## Environmental context and geochemical plausibility\n\n### Atmosphere and surface waters\n\nClassical Miller\u2013Strecker chemistry has the strongest historical evidence for direct synthesis from very small molecules, but its environmental support is weakest at the level of a **globally reducing atmosphere**. Trail, Watson & Tailby's Hadean zircon measurements imply mantle oxidation states similar to later terrestrial mantle by ~4.35 Ga, which favors outgassing of H\u2082O, CO\u2082, SO\u2082 and N\u2082 rather than large steady-state CH\u2084 and NH\u2083 abundances. The key environmental question is therefore not whether electrical discharge can make valine\u2014it can\u2014but whether early Earth could repeatedly create **local mixtures rich enough in reduced C and N to produce the necessary HCN and branched C4 aldehydes**.\n\nNeutral CO\u2082/N\u2082 spark experiments demonstrate that some amino-acid chemistry remains possible under more oxidized atmospheric assumptions, particularly when solution pH and oxidative work-up chemistry are controlled, but they have not supplied comparable direct evidence for valine. The later ascorbate-contamination result of Kuwahara et al. further weakens claims of very high amino-acid yields in some neutral-atmosphere experiments.\n\n### Hydrothermal and serpentinization systems\n\nThe **Kaur et al. H\u2082/Ni chemistry** is especially attractive environmentally because it works at 22 \u00b0C and pH ~8 rather than requiring hydrothermal temperatures. H\u2082 is continuously generated by water\u2013rock reactions such as serpentinization, and the authors note measured hydrothermal-fluid H\u2082 values of roughly 1\u201315 mmol kg\u207b\u00b9. Ni-bearing metal/mineral phases and meteoritic material offer plausible heterogeneous catalysts.\n\nThe difficulty is upstream carbon chemistry. Hydrothermal plausibility of H\u2082 and Ni does **not** establish a hydrothermal source of 30 mM \u03b1-ketoisovalerate. Kaur et al.'s coupled H\u2082 network makes several ketoacids from oxaloacetate/glyoxylate chemistry, but the valine-specific branched ketoacid was externally supplied. Thus the route currently has strong environmental support for the **reductive-amination step**, not for the complete valine synthesis.\n\nFeS/Fe(OH)\u2082 reductive-amination chemistry is often associated with hydrothermal/mineral-surface models, but Huber & W\u00e4chtersh\u00e4user's own results caution against treating \u201cFeS\u201d as a generic catalytic label: freshly precipitated material worked substantially differently from other preparations, excess sulfide could suppress activity, increased ionic strength reduced yields, and several alternative metal sulfides failed. Those dependencies need to be confronted explicitly when extrapolating to seawater or vent fluids.\n\n### Impact environments\n\nImpact chemistry is geochemically attractive because it supplies both extreme energy and reducing power from Fe/Ni projectiles without demanding a globally reducing atmosphere. Takeuchi et al.'s 2020 ^13C-labeled experiment demonstrates this principle directly with CO\u2082/HCO\u2083\u207b, N\u2082, water and meteoritic metals. But the realistic-feedstock experiment does **not yet reach valine**, whereas Bar-Nun et al.'s direct valine synthesis starts with methane, ethane and ammonia already in the gas phase.\n\nThus \u201cimpact synthesis of valine\u201d currently joins two pieces of evidence that have not been experimentally unified: old direct valine synthesis under a strongly reduced gas mixture, and modern geochemically motivated synthesis of simpler amino acids from CO\u2082/N\u2082/Fe/Ni. Demonstrating valine in a ^13C/^15N-labeled CO\u2082/N\u2082 impact experiment would materially strengthen this framework.\n\n### Meteorite parent bodies\n\nRadiolytic and hydrothermal formaldehyde/ammonia chemistry has perhaps the clearest environmental case for an **extraterrestrial** contribution. Carbonaceous parent bodies experienced aqueous alteration, and ^26Al provides both heat and ionizing radiation. Kebukawa's \u03b3-radiolysis experiments showed dose-dependent amino-acid synthesis, while Ishikawa et al. subsequently included valine among the minor products.\n\nThe caveat is that amino acids are generated as members of large, low-selectivity product suites, frequently after acid hydrolysis of complex precursor material. The concentration of formaldehyde, ammonia and related small organics in actual parent-body pore solutions, the extent of transport and destruction, and the quantitative contribution of \u03b3 radiation relative to ordinary thermal chemistry remain model-dependent. The 2017 Kebukawa hydrothermal experiment is particularly important as an analytical warning because its apparent valine was L-only and was judged likely contamination.\n\n## Competing frameworks, evidence against them, and unresolved bottlenecks\n\nThe strongest general criticism that applies across the field is **precursor displacement**: a route can solve the final C\u2013N bond formation while moving the harder carbon-skeleton problem upstream. The 2024 Ni/H\u2082 route is the clearest example. Once \u03b1-ketoisovalerate exists, valine formation at 22 \u00b0C is straightforward. But \u03b1-ketoisovalerate is already almost valine; the experimentally unresolved problem is the abiotic creation of its branched C5 skeleton in appreciable concentration from simpler C1\u2013C3 compounds.\n\nThe converse problem affects Strecker chemistry. Its advantage is that it **actually constructs the extra carbon** of the amino-acid carboxyl group from HCN, and electric discharges can generate HCN and carbonyl compounds from small gases. But valine specifically demands isobutyraldehyde. Producing a broad soup of C1\u2013C4 aldehydes is chemically easy compared with generating the branched aldehyde at a concentration high enough for valine to compete with glycine, alanine, AIB, norvaline and numerous non-protein isomers. Ring et al.'s broad product distribution illustrates that selectivity problem experimentally.\n\nThe **NH\u2084CN/acetone route** has a particularly unresolved carbon-accounting problem. Because ordinary Strecker chemistry of acetone should lead to AIB rather than valine, direct valine formation implies secondary C\u2013C transformations in the cyanide polymerization network. Friedmann & Miller established the product, but the detailed sequence and its yield dependence on cyanide, ketone concentration, pH and temperature have never been developed into a modern isotope-resolved mechanism. Their failure to reproduce valine from NH\u2084CN alone also invalidates the simplest version of older cyanide-polymer claims.\n\nThe **formaldehyde/ammonia route** has an analogous mechanistic gap. It starts from admirably simple C1/N1 feedstocks but must execute several selective C\u2013C couplings to reach a branched C5 skeleton. Fox & Windsor demonstrated valine among hydrolysis products, yet modern HCHO/NH\u2083 experiments yield complex polymeric material and broad amino-acid suites, and Kebukawa et al.'s hydrothermal analogue did not produce analytically secure abiotic valine. The exact branch-forming sequence remains unknown.\n\nFor **shock chemistry**, the principal controversy is geochemical rather than chemical. Valine is demonstrably made by shock-heating a CH\u2084/C\u2082H\u2086/NH\u2083 mixture, but Hadean mantle redox constraints do not support that mixture as a general background atmosphere. More realistic CO\u2082/N\u2082 impact chemistry works, but so far only for simpler amino acids.\n\nThe **Bucherer\u2013Bergs \u03b1-ketoacid route** is chemically convincing but carries three distinct relevance questions: whether \u03b1-ketoisovalerate was available; whether cyanide and ammonia could coexist at useful concentrations; and whether the long room-temperature plus 80 \u00b0C processing sequence could occur without destroying other network components. Pulletikurti et al.'s optimized experiments used 0.5 M bicarbonate and comparatively concentrated substrates, making environmental concentration mechanisms an essential part of any proposed setting.\n\nThe **FTT framework** currently should not be promoted as a valine synthesis. Pizzarello's 2012 experiment is valuable because it uses exceptionally simple CO/H\u2082/NH\u2083 feedstocks and geologically relevant catalytic materials, but its 370 \u00b0C product suite contains AIB, norvaline and isovaline rather than valine. The strong production of AIB by magnetite shows that mineral surfaces can select branching patterns, but the selection is not the biological valine skeleton in that study.\n\nThere is also a broader **congruence-with-biochemistry debate**. Strecker chemistry is efficient and geochemically versatile but has no direct analogue in extant amino-acid biosynthesis. \u03b1-Ketoacid reductive amination is structurally congruent with extant metabolism, which is one reason Kaur et al. emphasize it, but metabolic resemblance by itself does not establish historical continuity. A reaction network is compelling only if the required \u03b1-ketoacid itself can arise from geochemically available starting materials under compatible conditions.\n\nFinally, there is the **chirality problem**. None of the strongest direct routes discussed here establishes a robust mechanism for generating protein-level L-valine enantiomeric excess from achiral feedstocks. Indeed, Kebukawa et al.'s observation of exclusively L-valine in an otherwise abiotic experiment was interpreted as a warning sign for contamination rather than as spontaneous enantioselection. For synthesis-network design, valine production and valine homochirality should therefore be treated as separate problems unless an explicit symmetry-breaking mechanism is demonstrated.\n\n## Implications for synthesis-network design\n\nThe most productive network representation is to treat valine as having **two convergent immediate-precursor nodes**:\n\n**Aldehyde branch**\n\\[\n\\mathrm{CH_4/CO/CO_2 \\rightarrow C_1-C_3\\ carbonyls \\rightarrow\nisobutyraldehyde}\n\\]\n\\[\n\\mathrm{isobutyraldehyde + NH_3 + HCN \\rightarrow\nvaline\\ aminonitrile \\rightarrow valine}\n\\]\n\nand\n\n**\u03b1-ketoacid branch**\n\\[\n\\mathrm{small\\ carbonyls/pyruvate \\rightarrow branched\\ C_5\\ chemistry\n\\rightarrow \\alpha\\!-\\!ketoisovalerate}\n\\]\n\\[\n\\mathrm{\\alpha\\!-\\!ketoisovalerate + NH_3 + H_2\n\\xrightarrow[22\\,^\\circ C,\\ pH\\sim8]{Ni/Pd} valine}\n\\]\n\nThe second arrow in each branch is reasonably well supported; the first arrow is the strategic bottleneck. The Strecker branch has abundant experimental evidence for the general **HCN + aldehyde \u2192 aminonitrile \u2192 amino acid** machinery, but insufficient quantitative work on prebiotic formation of isobutyraldehyde. The \u03b1-ketoacid branch has excellent modern evidence for **\u03b1-ketoisovalerate \u2192 valine**, but currently lacks a comparably convincing abiotic synthesis of \u03b1-ketoisovalerate from pyruvate or simple C1\u2013C3 feedstocks.\n\nFor experimental prioritization, the highest-value missing experiment would therefore not simply be \u201cmake valine again.\u201d It would be an isotope-resolved synthesis of the **branched skeleton**. Particularly discriminating experiments would test whether plausible atmospheric, photochemical, mineral, impact or H\u2082-driven networks can generate ^13C-labeled isobutyraldehyde or \u03b1-ketoisovalerate from CO\u2082/CO/HCHO/pyruvate, followed *in the same reaction system* by capture as valine. The need for this follows directly from the precursor gaps in both the Miller/Ring and Kaur pathways.\n\nA second priority is to quantify competition. In Kaur's \u03b1-ketoisovalerate experiment, geochemically attractive Ni and meteoritic metal make substantial **2-hydroxyisovalerate**, whereas Pd strongly favors valine. A realistic network therefore needs to model carbon partitioning between amino-, hydroxy-, decomposition- and adsorption products, not just report the presence of valine. Likewise, electric-discharge chemistry produces many constitutional isomers and non-protein amino acids.\n\nA third priority is to introduce realistic **salinity, mineral aging and concentration cycling**. Huber & W\u00e4chtersh\u00e4user showed that ionic strength, FeS preparation and sulfide loading can change reductive-amination behavior dramatically, while Pulletikurti et al.'s Bucherer\u2013Bergs chemistry uses much more concentrated bicarbonate, cyanide and organic substrate than would be expected in an unconfined ocean. Evaporation, freezing, adsorption, pore confinement or repeated wet\u2013dry cycling therefore cannot merely be environmental decoration; where high concentrations are required, a quantitative concentration mechanism is part of the chemical hypothesis.\n\nOverall, the **best-supported direct valine-forming chemical step under mild, geochemically interpretable conditions is presently \u03b1-ketoisovalerate reductive amination with H\u2082 over Ni-bearing or meteoritic material**, especially because Kaur et al. directly quantified valine/hydroxy-acid competition at 22 \u00b0C. The **best-supported route that constructs valine from substantially smaller organic molecules remains Strecker/discharge chemistry**, but its strongest demonstrations use reducing atmospheres whose global Hadean relevance is contested. **Parent-body \u03b3 chemistry and reduced-gas shock chemistry independently show that valine can also emerge from much less pathway-specific, energy-rich reaction networks**, though with low selectivity and substantial environmental qualifications. None of these results yet establishes a uniquely favored historical route.\n\n## Primary literature cited\n\n**Miller, Stanley L. (1957), *Biochimica et Biophysica Acta* 23, 480\u2013489, \u201cThe mechanism of synthesis of amino acids by electric discharges.\u201d** Established HCN/aldehyde/aminonitrile Strecker chemistry as the mechanism underlying much of electric-discharge amino-acid synthesis and found no substantial catalytic effect from ferrous ammonium sulfate.\n\n**Friedmann, Nadav & Stanley L. Miller (1969), *Nature* 221, 1152\u20131153, \u201cSynthesis of Valine and Isoleucine in Primitive Earth Conditions.\u201d** Failed to confirm valine from NH\u2084CN polymerization alone but directly obtained valine when acetone was added.\n\n**Bar-Nun, A.; Bar-Nun, N.; Bauer, S. H.; & Sagan, C. (1970), *Science* 168, 470\u2013472, \u201cShock Synthesis of Amino Acids in Simulated Primitive Environments.\u201d** Directly produced glycine, alanine, valine and leucine in a reduced gas mixture exposed to shock heating and rapid quenching.\n\n**Fox, Sidney W. & Charles R. Windsor (1970), *Science* 170, 984\u2013986, \u201cSynthesis of Amino Acids by the Heating of Formaldehyde and Ammonia.\u201d** Reported valine among amino acids obtained after hydrolysis of heated formaldehyde/ammonia reaction products.\n\n**Ring, D.; Wolman, Y.; Friedmann, N.; & Miller, S. L. (1972), *Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences USA* 69, 765\u2013768, \u201cPrebiotic Synthesis of Hydrophobic and Protein Amino Acids.\u201d** Confirmed valine and a broad suite of hydrophobic amino acids by chromatography and GC\u2013MS after electric discharge through methane/nitrogen/water with traces of ammonia.\n\n**Huber, Claudia & G\u00fcnter W\u00e4chtersh\u00e4user (2003), *Tetrahedron Letters* 44, 1695\u20131697, \u201cPrimordial Reductive Amination Revisited.\u201d** Demonstrated FeS/Fe(OH)\u2082-mediated reductive amination of several \u03b1-ketoacids; important mechanistic precedent, but not a direct valine experiment.\n\n**Cleaves, H. James; Chalmers, John H.; Lazcano, Antonio; Miller, Stanley L.; & Bada, Jeffrey L. (2008), *Origins of Life and Evolution of Biospheres* 38, 105\u2013115, \u201cA Reassessment of Prebiotic Organic Synthesis in Neutral Planetary Atmospheres.\u201d** Re-evaluated CO\u2082/N\u2082 discharge chemistry; relevant to atmospheric plausibility but not a direct valine demonstration.\n\n**Trail, Dustin; Watson, E. Bruce; & Tailby, Nicholas D. (2011), *Nature* 480, 79\u201382, \u201cThe Oxidation State of Hadean Magmas and Implications for Early Earth's Atmosphere.\u201d** Provided zircon-based evidence against persistently strongly reducing volcanic outgassing in much of the Hadean.\n\n**Wang, Wei; Li, Qiliang; Yang, Bin; Liu, Xiaoyang; Yang, Yanqiang; & Su, Wenhui (2012), *Chemical Communications* 48, 2146\u20132148, \u201cPhotocatalytic Reversible Amination of \u03b1-Keto Acids on a ZnS Surface: Implications for the Prebiotic Metabolism.\u201d** Demonstrated mineral-photocatalytic \u03b1-ketoacid amination, although not \u03b1-ketoisovalerate/valine.\n\n**Pizzarello, Sandra (2012), *Meteoritics & Planetary Science* 47, 1291\u20131296, \u201cCatalytic Syntheses of Amino Acids and Their Significance for Nebular and Planetary Chemistry.\u201d** Demonstrated CO/H\u2082/NH\u2083 FTT-type amino-acid synthesis on meteoritic and mineral catalysts; notably produced AIB and isovaline but did not report valine.\n\n**Kuwahara, Hideharu; Eto, Midori; Kawamoto, Yukinori; Kurihara, Hironari; Kaneko, Takeo; Obayashi, Yumiko; & Kobayashi, Kensei (2012), *Origins of Life and Evolution of Biospheres* 42, 533\u2013541, \u201cThe Use of Ascorbate as an Oxidation Inhibitor in Prebiotic Amino Acid Synthesis: A Cautionary Note.\u201d** Demonstrated that ascorbate can produce misleading apparent amino-acid signals in neutral-atmosphere synthesis experiments.\n\n**Kebukawa, Yoko et al. (2017), *Science Advances* 3, e1602093, \u201cOne-pot Synthesis of Amino Acid Precursors with Insoluble Organic Matter in Planetesimals with Aqueous Activity.\u201d** Produced extensive amino-acid precursor chemistry from formaldehyde/glycolaldehyde/ammonia but treated observed L-only valine as likely contamination.\n\n**Takeuchi, Y.; Furukawa, Y.; Kobayashi, T.; Sekine, T.; Terada, N.; & Kakegawa, T. (2020), *Scientific Reports* 10, 9220, \u201cImpact-induced amino acid formation on Hadean Earth and Noachian Mars.\u201d** Demonstrated isotopically verified amino-acid formation from CO\u2082/N\u2082/H\u2082O plus Fe/Ni-bearing impact materials, but not valine.\n\n**Kebukawa, Yoko (2022), *ACS Central Science* 8, 1664\u20131671, \u201cGamma-Ray-Induced Amino Acid Formation in Aqueous Small Bodies in the Early Solar System.\u201d** Established dose-dependent amino-acid production by \u03b3 irradiation of aqueous formaldehyde/ammonia mixtures.\n\n**Pulletikurti, Sunil; Yadav, Mahipal; Springsteen, Greg; & Krishnamurthy, Ramanarayanan (2022), *Nature Chemistry* 14, 1142\u20131150, \u201cPrebiotic Synthesis of \u03b1-Amino Acids and Orotate from \u03b1-Ketoacids Potentiates Transition to Extant Metabolic Pathways.\u201d** Established Bucherer\u2013Bergs conversion of several \u03b1-ketoacids to amino acids via cyanide/ammonia chemistry; directly relevant mechanistically to \u03b1-ketoisovalerate but did not itself provide a valine-specific substrate experiment.\n\n**Kaur, Harpreet; Rauscher, Sophia A.; Werner, Emilie; Song, Youngdong; Yi, Jing; Kaz\u00f6ne, Wahnyalo; Martin, William F.; T\u00fcys\u00fcz, Harun; & Moran, Joseph (2024), *Chem* 10, 1528\u20131540, \u201cA Prebiotic Krebs Cycle Analog Generates Amino Acids with H\u2082 and NH\u2083 over Nickel.\u201d** Provides the strongest modern quantitative direct experiment for \u03b1-ketoisovalerate \u2192 valine using H\u2082 and Ni, Pd or meteoritic catalysts under mild aqueous conditions.\n\n**Ishikawa, Akari; Kebukawa, Yoko; Kobayashi, Kensei; & Yoda, Isao (2024), *Life* 14, 103, \u201cGamma-Ray-Induced Amino Acid Formation during Aqueous Alteration in Small Bodies: The Effects of Compositions of Starting Solutions.\u201d** Extended parent-body radiolysis experiments and reported valine among minor amino-acid products.\n=== END DEEP RESEARCH FINDINGS ===\n\n\n=== END LITERATURE RESEARCH ===\n\nConsider how well the network aligns with this literature in your evaluation.\n\n\nPlease evaluate the network and respond with JSON:\n{\n    \"pathway_evaluations\": [\n        {\n            \"pathway_id\": \"<id>\",\n            \"reaction_scores\": {\"rxn_001\": 0.85, ...},\n            \"chemical_feasibility\": <0.0-1.0>,\n            \"pathway_coherence\": <0.0-1.0>,\n            \"environmental_progression\": <0.0-1.0>,\n            \"target_achievement\": <0.0-1.0>,\n            \"overall_confidence\": <0.0-1.0>,\n            \"feedback\": \"<pathway-specific feedback>\",\n            \"approved\": <true if confidence >= 0.8>,\n            \"problematic_reactions\": [\"rxn_ids with issues\"],\n            \"suggestions\": [\"improvements\"]\n        }\n    ],\n    \"best_pathway_id\": \"<id of best pathway>\",\n    \"overall_confidence\": <0.0-1.0>,\n    \"network_feedback\": \"<overall assessment>\"\n}\n\nNote: \"environmental_progression\" scores Environmental Consistency (conditions fit each\nreaction's stated environment), not any particular order of environments.\n", "raw_response": "{\n  \"pathway_evaluations\": [\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P1\",\n      \"reaction_scores\": {\n        \"rxn_001\": 0.91,\n        \"rxn_002\": 0.94,\n        \"rxn_005\": 0.90\n      },\n      \"chemical_feasibility\": 0.91,\n      \"pathway_coherence\": 0.94,\n      \"environmental_progression\": 0.72,\n      \"target_achievement\": 0.90,\n      \"overall_confidence\": 0.86,\n      \"feedback\": \"This is a chemically coherent Strecker route from explicit