,\n      \"overall_confidence\": 0.61,\n      \"feedback\": \"The balanced stoichiometry requires two Fe(OH)2 units and two FeOOH units, consistent with a two-electron reduction supplied by Fe(II) oxidation. Huber and W\u00e4chtersh\u00e4user (2003) supports freshly prepared ferrous-hydroxide-mediated reductive amination as a reaction class, but not valine production or this particular oxidation product. The candidate designation is scientifically appropriate; successful electron transfer, mineral identity and substrate-specific conversion remain unverified.\",\n      \"approved\": false,\n      \"problematic_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_017\"\n      ],\n      \"suggestions\": [\n        \"Measure valine formation alongside Fe(II) consumption and identify the actual oxidized iron phase rather than assuming FeOOH.\",\n        \"Specify fresh-mineral preparation, oxygen exclusion, temperature, pH and ionic strength, using the cited study as qualified reaction-class precedent.\",\n        \"Include controls for adsorption, ketoacid decomposition and competing hydroxy-acid production.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P9\",\n      \"reaction_scores\": {\n        \"rxn_018\": 0.53\n      },\n      \"chemical_feasibility\": 0.53,\n      \"pathway_coherence\": 0.7,\n      \"environmental_progression\": 0.53,\n      \"target_achievement\": 0.47,\n      \"overall_confidence\": 0.55,\n      \"feedback\": \"The reaction passes the supplied conservation check, and pyrite formation provides a conceivable source of reducing equivalents. However, evidence that fresh FeS can promote other reductive aminations does not establish coupling of alpha-ketoisovalerate reduction to FeS2 formation. Huber and W\u00e4chtersh\u00e4user (2003) also identifies sensitivity to mineral preparation, ionic strength and sulfide loading. Neither the proposed coupling nor valine production is demonstrated.\",\n      \"approved\": false,\n      \"problematic_reactions\": [\n        \"rx